    "name" : "Legend",
    "text" : "A frontal film of the abdomen obtained 10 minutes following the intravenous (IV) administration of iodinated contrast medium is shown. The IV contrast media utilized for IV urography (also known as IVU, excretory urography, intravenous pyelography, or IVP) is identical to that used for a contrast-enhanced CT scan. Because the CT scan can also reveal so much more than the morphology of the renal collecting system, ureters, and bladder, it has largely replaced the IVU for most purposes. ​\n​\nThe ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) lies at about the L2 level (lower on standing), while the ureterovesical junction (UVJ) lies at the level of the ischial spine or, as shown on axial CT, at the level of the seminal vesicles (male) or the cervix (female). Note the subtle deviation of the ureters as they cross the iliac vessels and pelvic brim."
