[
  {
    "name" : "Legend",
    "text" : "A catheter angiogram of the aorta shows many of the major branches of the aorta overlapped to a degree due to the nonselective (upper aortic) injection and the nontomographic nature of the radiograph.​\n​\nThe four paired lumbar arteries can be seen arising from the posterolateral abdominal aorta. A fifth lumbar artery may arise from the median sacral or internal iliac artery.​\n​\nThe last branches of the abdominal aorta are the median sacral and common iliac arteries. The latter branch into the external and internal iliac arteries. The internal iliac (hypogastric) artery supplies all of the pelvic viscera and muscles, while the external iliac supplies the anterior abdominal wall (through the deep circumflex iliac and inferior epigastric arteries) before leaving the abdomen (behind the inguinal ligament) to supply the lower extremity."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Imaging Technique",
    "text" : "An angiogram is a diagnostic test that uses X-rays to visualize blood vessels. Unlike a conventional radiograph (or X-ray), angiograms require the injection of a radiopaque contrast agent (iodine) into the bloodstream. Catheterization of the contrast agent is usually carried out through the femoral artery, femoral vein, or jugular vein, depending on the area of interest. Angiograms can also be performed with computerized tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging.  ​​\n​​\nAngiograms may be used to detect blockages or lesions that may be amendable through surgical treatment. Cerebral angiograms are used to detect cerebrovascular abnormalities such as aneurysm or arteriovenous malformation, to study vascular displacement, and to evaluate postoperative status of blood vessels. Renal angiograms are used to visualize renal parenchyma and renal vasculature to evaluate chronic renal disease, renal failure, and conduct post-transplant evaluation of the kidney. Coronary angiograms can detect blood flow restrictions, or stenosis, in the coronary vessels."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Copyright",
    "text" : "Images courtesy Raman, S. P., Federle, M. P. STATdx. Elsevier. Copyright 2025. Images reused with permission."
  }
]

[
