    "text" : "A catheter arteriogram with injection of the celiac trunk shows its main branches:​\nleft gastric artery​\nsplenic artery​\ncommon hepatic artery. ​\n​\nThe lesser curvature of the stomach is supplied by the left and right gastric arteries, with the latter being a branch of the proper hepatic artery. ​\n​\nThe greater curvature is supplied by the right and left gastroomental (gastroepiploic) arteries, branches of the gastroduodenal and splenic arteries, respectively. The splenic artery also supplies short gastric arteries to the fundus of the stomach. ​\n​\nThe gastroduodenal artery gives rise to the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, which anastomose with branches from the superior mesenteric artery to provide a rich vascular supply to the pancreas and duodenum. ​\n​\nThere are numerous congenital variations in the blood supply to the upper abdominal organs, and many interconnecting pathways of the celiac and superior mesenteric arteries and their branches."
