[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica media a blood vessel is made up of transverse elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe tunica media is generally the largest layer within arteries and much thinner in corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of elastic arteries is modified by having multiple concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae wrapped around the vessel. The fenestrations allow nutrients to pass to the cells buried deep within the arteries walls. Smooth muscle cells are interspersed between the elastic laminae and synthesize and secrete collagen and elastin. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media is the thickest layer of muscular arteries and is dominated by concentric layers of smooth muscle, with a small number of thin elastic sheets and collagen fibers between them. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of arterioles and venules contain one or two layers of helically arranged smooth muscle. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of the good-sized medium veins (such as the brachial or basilic veins) has significant amounts of smooth muscle separated by sheets of collagen, although it’s much thinner than a corresponding muscular artery.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of large veins is not well developed and is relatively narrow. The presence of circumferentially arranged smooth muscle in this layer depends on the location of the vein, e.g., uterine veins have several layers of smooth muscle, while retinal veins have none (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021).\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica media separates the tunica interna from the tunica externa. In arteries, the external elastic membrane separates the tunica media from the tunica externa, while the internal elastic membrane divides the tunica media from the tunica interna."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The mechanical strength of the tunica media comes from structural collagen and smooth muscle. The smooth muscle coiled around the vessel can contract or relax, altering the diameter of the vessel’s lumen, which causes the lumen to decrease or increase. This is known as vasoconstriction and vasodilation, respectively.\r\n\r\nThe function of the tunica media of veins is distinctly different from that of arteries. Veins maintain the tone in their walls to regulate the volume of blood that is held in them. Veins can hold approximately 70% of the blood volume and can alter this reserve by contraction.\r\n"
  },
