    "text" : "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a high-resolution cross-sectional imaging technique. It utilizes hydrogen protons, which are abundantly present in water and water makes up approximately 63% of the average male, and 52% in the average female (Lote, 2012). Hydrogen protons are normally randomly aligned, however, when placed in a magnetic field, the protons align along the same axis as the magnetic field. ​\n ​\nA radiofrequency pulse is applied and causes disruption to the magnetization and pushes the protons out of alignment, causing them to spin. This produces two magnetization vectors, longitudinal magnetization (in the direction of the magnetic field) and transverse magnetization (perpendicular to the magnetic field). When the radiofrequency pulse is removed, the magnetization vectors realign with the magnetic field. As a result, longitudinal magnetization starts to increase, known as T1 relaxation or recovery, and transverse magnetization starts to decrease, known as T2 relaxation or decay. Energy is released from the spinning protons as radiofrequency signals (known as the echo). The radiofrequency pulses are repeated and detected to produce an image. ​\n ​\nRadiographers can vary the sequence of pulses to create different types of images. The repetition time (TR) between two radiofrequency pulses influences T1 and T2 weighting, meaning that a shorter amount of repetition time between two pulses will generate a T1-weighted image, while pulses with a long echo time (TE) produces T2-weighted images. ​\n ​\nDifferent tissues in the body have different T1 and T2 values, these tissues will appear, not only different from each other, but also different on T1 and T2 MRIs. The bright, or whiter, image implies an increased signal intensity, while darker, or blacker, images have decreased signal intensity. Water or cerebrospinal fluid will appear dark on T1 weighted images and bright on T2-weighted images. ​\n ​\nMRI’s are useful to detect cerebral infarction, abnormalities in the brain and spine, diagnosis of knee injuries, and has eliminated the need for myelography. When used in areas such as the abdomen/pelvis and breast it has shown to have high sensitivity in detecting cancer. ​\n ​\nProton-density MRI is another MRI technique, where there is longer TR between two radiofrequency pulses and shorter TE. This technique is useful for detecting joint disease and injury."
