    "text" : "The sternum is located in the midline of the anterior thoracic cage. It runs inferiorly and slightly anterior along the vertical length of the thoracic cage. The anterior surface is convex, while the posterior surface is concave. \n\nThe sternum is composed of three main elements, the manubrium of sternum, body of sternum, and xiphoid process. \n\nThe manubrium of sternum is the most superior part of the bone. It is wedged-shaped and is the widest element of the sternum. It articulates with both clavicles, forming sternoclavicular joints, as well as with the costal cartilages of first and second ribs (or part thereof), forming sternocostal joints. The manubrium of sternum articulates with the body of sternum at the manubriosternal joint. \n\nThe body of sternum is the long rectangular part of the bone. It is thinner than the manubrium of sternum and bears narrowed superior and inferior ends. It articulates with the costal cartilages of the second to seventh ribs, forming sternocostal joints. The body of sternum is attached inferiorly to the xiphoid process at the xiphisternal joint. Up until puberty, the body of sternum is composed of four induvial elements, known as sternebrae. \n\nThe xiphoid process is the most inferior part of the sternum."
