[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ducts that transport saliva from the sublingual gland into the oral cavity comprise between eight to twenty small ducts.\n\nThe major sublingual duct (aka Bartholin’s duct), is the largest of the multiple excretory ducts that carry saliva from the sublingual gland to the oral cavity. It is formed by the union of one or more smaller sublingual ducts, and opens into the submandibular duct. As such, saliva in this duct eventually enters the oral cavity at the sublingual caruncle.\n\nThe minor sublingual ducts (aka Rivinus’ ducts, canals of Walther) are the smaller excretory ducts that carry saliva from the sublingual gland to the oral cavity. They open into the sublingual fold."
