    "text" : "The cartilaginous part of pharyngotympanic tube (aka auditory tube, or Eustachian tube) is formed of elastic fibrocartilage. The apex of the cartilage is attached to the base of the skull in a groove between the petrous part of the temporal bone and the greater wing of the sphenoid bone. Its base lies directly under the mucous membrane of the nasopharynx, where is forms an elevation, called the torus tubarius. \n\nThe cartilage consists of medial and lateral laminae that are separated by an elastin hinge. Fibrous tissue and Ostmann's fat pad lie along the inferolateral aspect of the pharyngotympanic tube. \n\nThe fibrocartilaginous portion of the pharyngotympanic tube is closed at rest and opens only on swallowing, yawning, or forceful inflation. The pharyngotympanic tube is opened and closed by the tensor veil palatine, elevator veil palatini, salpingopharyngeus and the tensor tympani muscles."
  }
