    "text" : "The ethmoidal cells (aka ethmoidal air cells, ethmoid sinuses, or ethmoidal sinuses) are one of the four groups of bilateral paranasal sinuses and are situated in the skull between the orbits and the nasal cavities. They are made up of a number of air cells that perforate the ethmoid bone. \n\nThe ethmoidal cells consist of a variable number of small osseous air-filled sinuses between the thin orbital and nasal plates of the ethmoid bone. They can be divided into anterior, medial, and posterior groups. \n\nThe function of the ethmoidal cells is unknown, but proposed roles include reduction of skull weight, protection from blows, temperature and pressure control, and vocal resonance."
