[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The testes are a pair of ovoid primary sex organs that play a crucial role in both the male reproductive and endocrine systems. 

They are composed of up to 900 coiled seminiferous tubules, each averaging more than half a meter in length, in which the sperm are formed. The seminiferous tubules are contained and protected within the tunica albuginea, a layer of connective tissue that gives the testicles their shape.

From the seminiferous tubules the sperm then empty into the epididymis, another coiled tube approximately 6 meters long. The epididymis leads into the vas deferens, which enlarges into the ampulla of the vas deferens immediately before the vas enters the body of the prostate gland. 

The two testes form up to 120 million sperm in the human adult each day. They can remain stored, maintaining their fertility, for at least a month. During this time, they are kept in a deeply suppressed inactive state by multiple inhibitory substances in the secretions of the ducts. 

In addition to producing the male reproductive cell the testicles are heavily involved in the endocrine system. Leydig cells present in between the seminiferous tubules produce and secrete testosterone and other androgenic hormones. These hormones promote development of the male reproductive system and secondary male characteristics."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the zygomatic bone forms the prominence of the cheekbones. It extends into temporal, maxillary, frontal and orbital processes which articulate with the temporal, maxilla, frontal and sphenoid bones respectively. The orbital surface of the body is perforated by a small foramen, the zygomaticofacial foramen which allows passage of the zygomaticofacial nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The temporomandibular joint is a synovial joint between the mandibular fossa and articular tubercle of the temporal bone and the mandibular condyle. The joint allows the opening and closing, and lateral movement of the lower jaw. 

While most bone articular surfaces in synovial joints are covered by a layer of hyaline cartilage, the temporomandibular joint is unusual as its articular surfaces are lined by fibrocartilage. Its joints cavity is also divided into two by an articular disc. 

The articular disc divides the joint capsule into a superior and inferior compartment. The superior compartment facilitates protraction or the mandible onto the articular tubercle and retraction of the mandible into the mandibular fossa. Whereas, the inferior compartment facilitates depression and elevation of the mandible. 

The temporomandibular joint comprises of joint capsule and three extra capsular ligaments - the lateral, sphenomandibular, and the stylomandibular ligaments."
  }
]

