[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pharynx is the part of the throat between the mouth and nasal cavity superiorly and the esophagus and larynx inferiorly. As a musculomembranous tube connected to both esophagus and larynx, it is part of both digestive and respiratory systems. \n\nIt is divided into the nasopharynx (epipharynx), oropharynx (mesopharynx), and the laryngopharynx (hypopharynx). \n\nThere are six pharyngeal muscles, including inferior, middle and superior constrictors. These originate on the cricoid & thyroid cartilage, from the hyoid bone & stylohyoid ligament, and from the mandible and sphenoid bones respectively. The other three pharyngeal muscles are the stylopharyngeus (originating on the styloid bone), the salpingopharnygeus (from the inferior auditory tube), and the pharyngopalatinus (continuous with the salpingopharyngeus)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal cavity is the air-filled space comprising the most rostral extension of the respiratory system. As such it is involved in processing and conditioning air en route to the other areas of the respiratory tract. \n\nThis conditioning involves stabilizing of air temperature (to within one degree of body temperature), humidification, and filtration. The temperature stabilization and humidification are facilitated by the large surface area created within the cavity by the folds of mucous membrane that line the nasal conchae on the lateral sides of the cavity. Filtration is carried out by vibrissae and the cilia of the respiratory epithelium. \n\nThe air processing segment of the nasal cavity is termed the respiratory segment, while the olfactory segment is lined with olfactory epithelium, which contains neuronal tissue including bipolar neurons with axons projecting directly to the brain via the cribriform plate."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The testes are a pair of ovoid primary sex organs that play a crucial role in both the male reproductive and endocrine systems. 

They are composed of up to 900 coiled seminiferous tubules, each averaging more than half a meter in length, in which the sperm are formed. The seminiferous tubules are contained and protected within the tunica albuginea, a layer of connective tissue that gives the testicles their shape.

From the seminiferous tubules the sperm then empty into the epididymis, another coiled tube approximately 6 meters long. The epididymis leads into the vas deferens, which enlarges into the ampulla of the vas deferens immediately before the vas enters the body of the prostate gland. 

The two testes form up to 120 million sperm in the human adult each day. They can remain stored, maintaining their fertility, for at least a month. During this time, they are kept in a deeply suppressed inactive state by multiple inhibitory substances in the secretions of the ducts. 

In addition to producing the male reproductive cell the testicles are heavily involved in the endocrine system. Leydig cells present in between the seminiferous tubules produce and secrete testosterone and other androgenic hormones. These hormones promote development of the male reproductive system and secondary male characteristics."
  }
]

