    "text" : "The salivary glands are a group of exocrine glands responsible for the production of saliva, including salivary amylase. Saliva is brought from the glands to the oral cavity via a multi-stage system of ducts, and some saliva is eventually transported to the stomach via the esophagus. \n\nThe major salivary glands are the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands. While the latter two glands produce mixtures of serous fluid (containing amylase) and mucus in different proportions, the parotid gland produces serous fluid only. \n\nThere are also over 600 minor salivary glands located throughout the oral cavity. These mostly produce mucous, with the exception of Von Ebner's glands, which are found on the tongue and secrete a serous fluid containing enzymes for lipid hydrolysis."
