[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The small intestine is the part of the gastrointestinal tract that immediately follows the stomach, from which it is separated by the pyloric sphincter muscle. The small intestine is made up of three parts: (i) the duodenum that comprises the first section following the stomach, (ii) the jejunum that follows the duodenum, and (iii) the ileum that follows the jejunum. 

The length of the duodenum is approximately 20-25% of the length of the small intestine, the jejunum approximately 30%, and the ileum approximately 45%.

These three structural parts of the small intestine are responsible for different functions in the digestive process. The duodenum is primarily responsible for the breakdown of chyme from the stomach, which is accomplished by the addition of bile salts from the gallbladder, and digestive enzymes from the pancreas to the duodenum. The jejunum is responsible for the absorption of small particles of nutrients and other small molecules such as drugs. The ileum is responsible for both the absorption of bile salts and water soluble vitamins, and to a lesser extent the absorption of particles and nutrients not absorbed by the jejunum. 

The absorptive properties of the small intestine are derived from the microvilli that line its walls to increase the surface area exposed to nutrients. The extensive network of capillaries that are embedded in the walls of the small intestine take up the amino acids, sugars and other small particles and transport them to the liver for cleansing and processing via the hepatic portal vein. Additionally, in the ileum, a large number of microscopic lymph vessels surrounding the microvilli are responsible for absorbing fatty acids and glycerol.

The ileum transfers its contents to the large intestine via the cecum. The ileocecal valve separates the ileum from the cecum, and allows the passage of the waste products of the small intestine into the large intestine for reabsorption of water."
  }
