The sacral plexus arises from the fourth and fifth lumbar nerves, numbered L4 and L5, and first three sacral spinal nerves, numbered S1 to S3. The anterior division of the fourth lumbar spinal nerve joins the fifth lumbar spinal nerve, L5, to form the lumbosacral trunk. This trunk receives contributions from the first three sacral spinal nerves, S1 to S3, forming the sacral plexus. The sacral plexus and the lumbar plexus are often collectively referred to as the lumbosacral plexus. 

The sacral plexus gives rise to nerve branches, which divide into collateral and terminal branches. Collateral branches of the sacral plexus include the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves. 

Terminal branches of the sacral plexus include the sciatic nerve (which divides into the tibial and fibular nerves) and the pudendal nerves. The most inferior spinal nerve is the coccygeal nerve, which arises from the sacral plexus. The fourth and fifth sacral nerves and the coccygeal nerve send ventral contributions to form the coccygeal plexus."
