    "text" : "An angiogram is a diagnostic test that uses X-rays to visualize blood vessels. Unlike a conventional radiograph (or X-ray), angiograms require the injection of a radiopaque contrast agent (iodine) into the bloodstream. Catheterization of the contrast agent is usually carried out through the femoral artery, femoral vein, or jugular vein, depending on the area of interest. Angiograms can also be performed with computerized tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging.  

Angiograms may be used to detect blockages or lesions that may be amendable through surgical treatment. Cerebral angiograms are used to detect cerebrovascular abnormalities such as aneurysm or arteriovenous malformation, to study vascular displacement, and to evaluate postoperative status of blood vessels. Renal angiograms are used to visualize renal parenchyma and renal vasculature to evaluate chronic renal disease, renal failure, and conduct post-transplant evaluation of the kidney. Coronary angiograms can detect blood flow restrictions, or stenosis, in the coronary vessels."
