[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The vertebral foramen is the large, triangular, centrally located opening of the second lumbar vertebra. Its margins are formed:

—anteromedially, by the vertebral body;

—anterolaterally, by both the right and left pedicles;

—posterolaterally, by both the right and left laminae;

—posteromedially, by the spinous process.

The vertebral foramen of the second lumbar vertebra contributes to the formation of the vertebral canal, which extends throughout the length of the vertebral column. The vertebral canal allows for the passage of the spinal cord, along with its accompanying meninges and vasculature.

Overall, the size and shape of the vertebral foramina in the different regions of the vertebral column are as follows:

—cervical vertebral foramina are the largest and triangular;

—thoracic vertebral foramina are the smallest and circular;

