    "text" : "The superior articular facet is the upper, smaller, smooth area located on the medial aspect of the head of the seventh rib. It is one of two articular facets on the head of the seventh rib, the other being the inferior articular facet, these two facets are separated from each other by the crest of the head of the rib.\n\nThis superior articular facet articulates with the inferior costal facet of the sixth thoracic vertebra, forming the upper part of the joint of the head of the seventh rib. The lower part of this joint is formed by the articulation between the inferior articular facet on the head of the seventh rib and the superior costal facet of the seventh thoracic vertebra.\n\nOverall, the first, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth ribs have a single articular facet on their heads, while the remaining ribs have two articular facets, known as the superior and inferior articular facets on heads of ribs."
