    "text" : "The inferior articular processes (inferior zygapophyses) are the two downwardly projecting processes located at the lateral parts of the vertebral arch of a lumbar vertebra. They extend inferiorly from the laminae.\n\nOverall, the inferior articular processes of lumbar vertebrae consist of anterolaterally facing inferior articular facets. They articulate with the superior articular processes of the vertebra below, forming zygapophyseal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The foramen ovale (oval foramen) is a small, oval, posteromedially located opening found on each greater wing of the sphenoid bone. Specifically, it is located anteromedial to the foramen spinosum, posterolateral to the foramen rotundum, and anterolateral to the foramen lacerum;\n\nEach foramen ovale extends inferiorly from the cerebral surface of the greater wing of sphenoid bone to its infratemporal surface.\n\nThe foramen ovale allows for the passage of the mandibular nerve, lesser petrosal nerve, and the venous plexus of the foramen ovale."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The manubrium is the wide, quadrilateral, upper part of the sternum. It is located at the level of the third and fourth thoracic vertebrae and contains two surfaces (anterior and posterior) and four borders (superior, inferior and two lateral).

