[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The vertebral foramen is the small, circular, centrally located opening of the second thoracic vertebra. Its margins are formed:

—anteromedially, by the vertebral body;

—anterolaterally, by both the right and left pedicles;

—posterolaterally, by both the right and left laminae;

—posteromedially, by the spinous process.

The vertebral foramen of the second thoracic vertebra contributes to the formation of the vertebral canal, which extends throughout the length of the vertebral column. The vertebral canal allows for the passage of the spinal cord, along with its accompanying meninges and vasculature.

Overall, the size and shape of the vertebral foramina in the different regions of the vertebral column are as follows:

—cervical vertebral foramina are the largest and triangular;

—thoracic vertebral foramina are the smallest and circular;

—lumbar vertebral foramina are intermediate in size and triangular."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The body (shaft) is the short, thin, tubular part of the middle phalanx of second toe. It is located between its head and base and consists of a concave plantar surface and a smooth, flat dorsal surface. It provides an insertion site for the flexor digitorum brevis muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior border (lower border) of fourth rib is its sharp lower edge. It lies immediately inferior to the costal groove, which itself is located on the ribs inner surface. It is one of the two borders of the body of the fourth rib, the other being the superior border.\n\nThe inferior border of fourth rib provides an origin site for the external, internal and innermost intercostal muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spines of sphenoid bone (sphenoidal spines, or angular spines of sphenoid bone) are the two pointed, posteroinferiorly projecting processes found along the posterior aspects of each of the greater wings of sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each spine of sphenoid bone;

—is found at the point where the squamosal and petrosal margins of a greater wing of sphenoid bone meet;

—is located posterior to a foramen spinosum and lateral to a sulcus of auditory tube;

—extends into the angle formed between the anterior aspects of the squamous and petrous parts of a temporal bone;

—contributes to the formation of both a sphenosquamous suture and sphenopetrosal synchondrosis;

—provides attachments sites for sphenomandibular and pterygospinous ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The mesial surface is the aspect of the mandibular third molar tooth that faces towards the mandibular second molar tooth. It is one of five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, distal, and occlusal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The mesial surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the mesiobuccal angle;

- lingual surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the mesioocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the mesial surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- occlusal third;

- middle third;

- cervical third.

Furthermore, the mesial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to buccal direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- buccal third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The cervical line (cementoenamel junction) is a thin, irregular margin that forms the boundary between the enamel covering the crown and the cementum covering the root. Therefore, the cervical line separates the crown and root of the tooth."
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Location: Neurocranium.

Bone Type: Flat bone.

Key Features: Frontal, occipital, sphenoidal, and mastoid angles, internal and external surfaces, parietal eminence, and superior and inferior temporal lines.

Articulates With: Opposite parietal bone, frontal, occipital, temporal, and sphenoid bones.

Arterial Supply: Middle meningeal artery."}, {"name": "Key Features & Anatomical Relations", "text": "The parietal bones are a pair of large, quadrilateral bones found along the superolateral aspects of the cranium. They are classified as flat bones and contribute to the formation of the neurocranium. Each parietal bone includes the following bony features:

- parts: frontal, occipital, sphenoidal and mastoid angles;

