[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Origin: Medial aspect of coronoid process of ulna and superior three quarters of anterior and medial surfaces of body of ulna.

Insertion: Palmar aspects of bases of the distal phalanges of index, middle, ring, and little fingers.

Action: Flexes index, middle, ring, and little fingers.

Innervation: Medial part: ulnar nerve (C8-T1); Lateral part: anterior interosseous branch of median nerve (C8-T1).

Arterial Supply: Ulnar artery; inferior ulnar collateral artery; ulnar recurrent artery; anterior interosseous artery."}, {"name": "Origin", "text": "The flexor digitorum profundus muscle originates from the:

- medial aspect of the coronoid process of ulna;

- superior three quarters of the anterior and medial surfaces of the body of ulna;

- anterior surface of the adjacent interosseous membrane of forearm."}, {"name": "Insertion", "text": "The fibers of the flexor digitorum profundus muscle travel inferiorly to the hand and insert onto the palmar aspects of the bases of distal phalanges of the index, middle, ring, and little fingers."}, {"name": "Key Features & Anatomical Relations", "text": "The flexor digitorum profundus muscle is one of the muscles of the deep part of the anterior compartment of the forearm. It is a thick, fusiform type of skeletal muscle that divides into four tendons. The tendon for the index finger separates from the other tendons in the forearm, while the others separate in the hand. In the forearm and hand, each of these tendons travels deep to the tendons of the flexor digitorum superficialis muscle. At the wrist, the tendons of both muscles travel together, deep to the flexor retinaculum of the hand, where they pass through the common flexor sheath. The tendons of both muscles travel through the fibrous sheaths that are found along the palmar aspects of each finger. Along each proximal phalanx, the tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus pass through the tendons of the flexor digitorum superficialis, then travel to their insertion sites.

For descriptive purposes, the flexor digitorum profundus muscle is divided into two parts:

- a medial part, which refers to the two tendons that attach to the distal phalanges of the ring and little fingers, along with the portion of the muscle belly that controls them;

- a lateral part, which refers to the two tendons that attach to the distal phalanges of the index and middle fingers, along with the portion of the muscle belly that controls them.

The flexor digitorum profundus muscle is located:

- anterior (superficial) to the ulna, interosseous membrane of forearm, and the pronator quadratus muscle;

- posterior (deep) to the flexor digitorum superficialis muscle;

- lateral to the ulnar artery and ulnar nerve.

Within the palm of the hand, each of the four lumbrical muscles of the hand originates from each of the four tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus."}, {"name": "Actions & Testing", "text": "The flexor digitorum profundus muscle is involved in multiple actions:

- flexes the distal phalanges at the distal interphalangeal joints of the index, middle, ring, and little fingers;

- assists in flexion of the middle phalanges at the proximal interphalangeal joints of the same fingers;

- assists in flexion of the proximal phalanges at the metacarpophalangeal joints of the same fingers;

- assists in flexion of the hand at the radiocarpal (wrist) joint.

This muscle is capable of flexing the index finger independently of the other fingers.

The flexor digitorum profundus muscle can be tested by flexing the distal phalanx at the distal interphalangeal joints while its corresponding middle phalanx is held in its extended position (Standring, 2016)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Standring, S. (2016) Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice. Gray's Anatomy Series 41st edn.: Elsevier Limited."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior border (upper border) of third rib is its blunt, smooth upper edge. It is one of the two borders of the body of the third rib, the other being the inferior border. It consists of inner and outer lips:\n\n—The inner lip provides insertion sites for the internal and innermost intercostal muscles.\n\n—The outer lip provides an insertion site for the external intercostal muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The tubercle of tenth rib is a small prominence on the external surface of the junction between its neck and its body. It consists of two parts:

—a smooth, medially located articular facet, which articulates with the transverse process of tenth thoracic vertebra;

—a rough, laterally located non-articular part, which provides the attachment site for the costotransverse ligament.

Overall, only the first to tenth ribs have a tubercle, while the eleventh and twelfth ribs lack this bony feature."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The body of talus is the expanded, quadrilateral, posterior part of the bone. It is the proximal continuation of its neck and contains the trochlea, medial and lateral malleolar facets, posterior calcaneal facet, and the lateral and posterior processes. It provides attachment sites for the:

—medial, lateral and posterior talocalcaneal ligaments;

—talocalcaneal interosseous ligament;

—posterior talofibular ligament;

—posterior tibiotalar part of medial collateral ligament of ankle joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Angle of sixth rib"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The head is the expanded, pulley-shaped, distal end of the middle (intermediate) phalanx of the index finger. It is the distal continuation of its body and contains a distal articular facet, which articulates with the articular facet of the distal phalanx of the index finger, forming the distal interphalangeal joint of the index finger. \n\nThe head of the middle phalanx of the index finger provides attachment sites for the collateral, accessory collateral, and palmar ligaments of the distal interphalangeal joint of the index finger."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The costal groove (costal sulcus) is the short, shallow depression found along the body of the sixth rib. It begins lateral to the tubercle and travels along the internal surface of the body, adjacent to the inferior border, and gradually becomes indistinguishable as it travels to the more anterior part of the body of the sixth rib.\n\nThe intercostal vein, artery and nerve travel along the costal groove, with the vein lying most superior and the nerve most inferior. The upper edge of the costal groove provides an attachment site for the innermost intercostal muscle.\n\nOverall, only the second to eleventh ribs have a costal groove, while the first and twelfth ribs lack this bony feature."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The medial (first) cuneiform articular facet is the flat, triangular, medially located area found on the distal articular surface of the navicular bone. It is the largest of the three facets found on the distal articular surface of the navicular bone, the other two being the intermediate and lateral cuneiform articular facets.\n\nThe medial cuneiform articular facet of navicular bone articulates with the proximal articular surface of the medial cuneiform bone, contributing to the formation of the cuneonavicular joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior temporal line is a curved ridge found along the external surface of the parietal bone. It is located inferior to the superior temporal line and runs parallel to it.\n\nThe inferior temporal line is the posteroinferior continuation of the temporal line of the frontal bone. It extends posteriorly from the frontal border of the parietal bone and then curves in a posteroinferior direction towards the mastoid angle, where it becomes continuous with the supramastoid crest of the temporal bone. It provides an origin site for the temporalis muscle and forms the posterosuperior boundary of the temporal fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The quadrate tubercle is the small prominence on the posterior aspect of the proximal end of the femur. It is found along the intertrochanteric crest, close to its mid-point, and provides an insertion site for the quadratus femoris muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior border (lower border) of third rib is its sharp lower edge. It lies immediately inferior to the costal groove, which itself is located on the ribs inner surface. It is one of the two borders of the body of the third rib, the other being the superior border.\n\nThe inferior border of third rib provides origin sites for the external, internal and innermost intercostal muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Inferior border of sixth rib"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Spinous process of first lumbar vertebra, via posterior layer of the thoracolumbar fascia"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lacrimal process is the thin, pointed, anterosuperiorly located projection found along the superior border of the inferior nasal concha. It is one of three processes found along the superior border of inferior nasal concha, the other two being the maxillary and ethmoidal processes.\n\nThe lacrimal process of inferior nasal concha articulates with the lacrimal bone, forming the lacrimoconchal suture. The lacrimal process also contributes to the formation of the nasolacrimal canal by its combination with the lacrimal grooves of both the lacrimal bone and maxilla. This canal allows for the passage of the nasolacrimal duct."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The ischial tuberosity is the large, rounded, rough elevation on the dorsal surface of body of ischium. It provides the origin sites for multiple muscles, including the:

—hamstring muscles (i.e., semitendinosus, semimembranosus and long head of biceps femoris muscles);

—ischiocondylar part (hamstring part) of adductor magnus muscle;

—quadratus femoris muscle;

—inferior gemellus muscle.

The ischial tuberosity also provides an attachment site for the sacrotuberous ligament and is the structure that supports the body when sitting."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The laminae are the two broad, flat, bony plates that project posteromedially from the posterior ends of the pedicles of a typical cervical vertebra. They form the posterolateral parts of the vertebral arch, the posterolateral walls of the vertebral foramen, and their posterior ends unite at the midline.\r\rThe transverse and superior articular processes extend from the junctions between the pedicles and laminae, the spinous process extends from the junction between the two laminae, and the inferior articular processes extend inferiorly from the laminae.\r\rOverall, the laminae of the vertebral column provide attachment sites for deep back muscles and the ligamenta flava."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The medial malleolar facet of talus is the smooth, crescent-shaped area found along the medial aspect of the body of talus. It is continuous laterally with the trochlea of talus and articulates with the articular facet of medial malleolus on the tibia, contributing to the formation of the ankle joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The cusp is the inferiorly located, pointed elevation found along the labial aspect of the crown of maxillary canine tooth. It is located inferior to the lingual ridge and consists of both mesial and distal cusp ridges."
  }
]

[{"name": "Description", "text": "The diploic veins are a valveless intraosseous network of veins draining the skull itself. These are situated in the diploe or the spongy layer of bone in the cranial vault and run inside the diploic channels, between the inner and the outer layers of the cortical bone of the skull.

The diploic veins communicate with the dural venous sinuses and meningeal veins along the compact inner table of the skull and with the pericranial veins on the outer table via emissary veins (Jivraj et al., 2009; Garcia-Gonzalez et al., 2009).

The diploic veins can be categorized into four categories based on their location in the human skull:

- frontal diploic veins; 

- anterior temporal diploic veins;

- posterior temporal diploic veins;

- occipital diploic veins."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Garcia-Gonzalez, U., Cavalcanti, D. D., Agrawal, A., Gonzalez, L. F., Wallace, R. C., Spetzler, R. F. and Preul, M. C. (2009) 'The diploic venous system: surgical anatomy and neurosurgical implications', Neurosurg Focus, 27(5), pp. E2.

Jivraj, K., Bhargava, R., Aronyk, K., Quateen, A. and Walji, A. (2009) 'Diploic venous anatomy studied in-vivo by MRI', Clin Anat, 22(3), pp. 296-301."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The orbital plate (lamina papyracea) is the thin, smooth area found along the superolateral aspect of the ethmoidal labyrinth. It contributes to the formation of the medial wall of the orbit and articulates:

—anteriorly, with the posterior border of lacrimal bone, forming the ethmoidolacrimal suture;

—posteriorly, with the anterior surface of the body of sphenoid bone, contributing to the formation of the sphenoethmoidal suture;

—superiorly, with the inferomedial aspect of the frontal bone, contributing to the formation of the frontoethmoidal suture;

—anteroinferiorly, with the orbital surface of the maxilla, forming the ethmoidomaxillary suture;

—posteroinferiorly, with the orbital process of palatine bone, forming the palatoethmoidal suture."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spinous process is the single, posteriorly projecting process located at the posterior part of the vertebral arch of a thoracic vertebra. It extends from the junction between the two laminae, which itself is found at the midline.\n\nOverall, the spinous processes of thoracic vertebrae are generally long, thin, and tapered. They are obliquely oriented in the posteroinferior direction, resulting in adjacent spinous processes overlapping each other. They provide attachment sites for both intrinsic and extrinsic back muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The posterior surface is the smooth area on the posteromedial aspect of the body of pubis. It is one of the three surfaces of the body of pubis, the other two being the anterior and symphyseal surfaces, and it is an area that is bounded:

—superiorly, by the crest of pubis;

—anteromedially, by the symphyseal surface of body of pubis.

Its inferior end is continuous with the internal surface of inferior pubic ramus, and its posterolateral end is continuous with the pelvic surface of superior pubic ramus.

The posterior surface of body of pubis provides origin sites for the levator ani and obturator internus muscles, as well as an attachment site for the puboprostatic ligament in males. It lies immediately anterior to the urinary bladder."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spinoglenoid notch (inferior scapular notch or notch of scapular spine) is the indentation formed between the neck of the scapula and the lateral border of its spine. It serves as a point of communication between the supraspinous and infraspinous fossae and provides a passage for the suprascapular nerve and vessels to travel between these two fossae."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lesser sciatic (ischiatic or ischiadic) notch is the smaller of the two indentations found along the posterior border of the coxal bone. From the ischial spine, it extends inferiorly along the posterior border of the body of ischium, to the upper margin of the ischial tuberosity.\n\nThe lesser sciatic notch is converted into the lesser sciatic foramen by the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments. This foramen allows for the passage of the tendon of the obturator internus muscle, the internal pudendal vessels and the pudendal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The costal end (anterior end or sternal end) of seventh rib is located at the anterior end of the shaft. It has a small, rough, concave facet, which attaches to the seventh costal cartilage, forming the seventh costochondral joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The hiatus for lesser petrosal nerve is a small, laterally located opening found along the anterior surface of the petrous part of each temporal bone. On its corresponding side, each hiatus for lesser petrosal nerve;

—is located lateral to a hiatus for greater petrosal nerve and anterior to an arcuate eminence;

—has a groove for lesser petrosal nerve extending anteromedially from it;

—allows for the passage of a lesser petrosal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The chiasmatic sulcus (chiasmatic groove, prechiasmatic sulcus, or prechiasmatic groove) is the horizontal, shallow depression found along the upper aspect of the body of sphenoid bone. The chiasmatic sulcus:

—is located anterior to the tuberculum sellae of the sella turcica;

—is separated from the anteriorly located sphenoidal yoke by the limbus of sphenoid bone;

—is continuous laterally with both the right and left optic canals;

—lodges the optic chiasm."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lateral condyle of femur (lateral femoral condyle, external condyle of femur, or fibular condyle of femur) is the laterally located, round, articular eminence on the distal end of the femur. It is one of the two articular condyles of the femur, the other being the medial condyle.\n\nIt contains the lateral epicondyle of femur and the groove for popliteus muscle, and its medial aspect contributes to the formation of the intercondylar fossa. It provides an origin site for the lateral head of gastrocnemius muscle, and attachment sites for the fibular collateral and anterior cruciate ligaments.\n\nThe lateral condyle of femur articulates with the lateral condyle of tibia, via the lateral meniscus, forming the lateral femorotibial joint. Overall, the lateral femorotibial, medial femorotibial and patellofemoral joints collectively form the knee joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The labial surface is the aspect of the mandibular canine tooth that faces towards the lips and vestibule. It is one of the four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the lingual, mesial, and distal surfaces. The labial surface consists of three labial lobes.

The labial surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolabial angle;

- distal surface at the distolabial angle;

- incisal margin at the labioincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the labial surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- incisal third;

- middle third;

- cervical third.

Furthermore, the labial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior intervertebral surface is the flat, rough area on the lower aspect of the body of a thoracic vertebra. It is surrounded by a smooth outer rim, known as the inferior annular epiphysis.\n\nOverall, the intervertebral surfaces of vertebrae provide attachment sites for the intervertebral discs. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lateral surface of fifth metatarsal bone is the concave, lateral aspect of the body of the bone. It is one of the two surfaces of the fifth metatarsal bone, the other being the medial surface."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior articular facets are the smooth, posteromedially facing articular areas on the superior articular processes of a lumbar vertebra. They articulate with the corresponding inferior articular facets of the vertebra above, forming zygapophyseal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Muscle: Longus Colli\nArticulated Area: Cervical Spine\nRange of Motion: Ipsilateral Rotation 0°- 50°"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The external surface (outer surface) of fourth rib is its smooth, convex, outer area. It is one of the two surfaces of the body of the fourth rib, the other being the internal surface.

The external surface of fourth rib consists of the angle, which is located on its posterolateral aspect. It provides:

—origin sites for the iliocostalis cervicis, pectoralis minor and serratus anterior muscles;

—insertion sites for the serratus posterior superior, levatores costarum breves, iliocostalis thoracis and longissimus thoracis muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The scaphoid articular facet is the flat, crescent-shaped area found on the lateral aspect of the lunate bone. It articulates with the lunate articular facet of the scaphoid bone, forming the scapholunate joint. It also provides an attachment site for the scapholunate interosseous ligament, which connects the scaphoid and lunate bones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior tubercle is the rough, small prominence found along the midline on the anterior aspect of the anterior arch of atlas (first cervical vertebra). It provides an attachment site for the anterior longitudinal ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The marginal ridges are the curved elevations found along the occlusal surface of the maxillary first premolar tooth. They consist of:\n\n- A mesial marginal ridge, which extends lingually from the mesial end of the buccal cusp to the mesial end of the lingual cusp.\n\n- A distal marginal ridge, which extends lingually from the distal end of the buccal cusp to the distal end of the lingual cusp."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior arch is the short, curved, anteromedially located part of the atlas (first cervical vertebra). It is the shorter of the two arches of the atlas, the other being the posterior arch. The anterior arch:

—is continuous posterolaterally with both the right and left lateral masses of atlas;

—consists of the anterior tubercle of atlas and the articular facet of dens;

—provides insertion sites for the superior oblique parts of both the right and left longus cervicis muscles;

—provides attachment sites for the anterior longitudinal ligament and anterior atlantooccipital membrane;

—articulates posteriorly with the dens axis, forming the median atlantoaxial joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The orbital surfaces are the smooth, flat, quadrilateral areas found along the anterosuperior aspects of both the right and left greater wings of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each orbital surface:\n\n—is located lateral to a superior orbital fissure and superior to an inferior orbital fissure;\n\n—contributes to the formation of the lateral wall of an orbit."
