    "text" : "The internal surface (inner surface) of fifth rib is its smooth, concave, inner area. It is one of the two surfaces of the body of the fifth rib, the other being the external surface.\n\nThe internal surface of fifth rib consists of the costal groove, which is located on its inferior edge, adjacent to the ribs inferior border. It provides an origin site for the insertion site for the transversus thoracis muscle, attachment sites for the innermost intercostal muscles, and is covered by endothoracic fascia."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior articular processes (inferior zygapophyses) are the two downwardly projecting processes located at the lateral parts of the vertebral arch of a thoracic vertebra. They extend inferiorly from the laminae.\n\nOverall, the inferior articular processes of thoracic vertebrae generally consist of inferior articular facets that face anteriorly and slightly medially. They articulate with the superior articular processes of the vertebra below, forming zygapophyseal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior articular processes (superior zygapophyses) are the two upwardly projecting processes located at the lateral parts of the vertebral arch of a thoracic vertebra. They extend from the junction between the pedicles and laminae.\n\nOverall, the superior articular processes of thoracic vertebrae generally consist of superior articular facets that face posteriorly and slightly laterally. They articulate with the inferior articular processes of the vertebra above, forming zygapophyseal joints."
