[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Vascular endothelium is the layer of epithelial cells that lines the lumina of blood vessels; it originates from the mesoderm (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium is a key component of the tunica interna of a blood vessel. It forms the innermost layer surrounding the lumen, which separates the various cells and molecules within the blood from the surrounding tissue. The endothelium is therefore in direct contact with the bloodstream.\r\n\r\nThe endothelium is composed of a single layer of simple squamous endothelial cells. These cells form a continuous and uninterrupted sheet, or lining, around the lumen of the artery. \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The endothelium lies on the basement membrane of the blood vessel. This membrane affixes the endothelium to the connective tissue as well as providing the vessel wall with strength. The basement membrane is attached to the subendothelial connective tissue layer of the tunica interna.  "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium presides over the movement of cells in and out the blood as well as the exchange of molecules between the blood and surrounding tissues. Endothelium also influences blood flow by acting as a mechanoreceptor when shear stress is applied to the vessel and transmitting the signal to intracellular organelles resulting in an alteration in vascular tone (Akira and Joji, 1993)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis \r\n•\tThrombosis\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Akira, K. and Joji, A. (1993) 'Vascular Endothelial Cell Functions and Biomechanics', Endothelium, 1(2), pp. 127-130.\r\nDorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The cervical line (cementoenamel junction) is a thin, irregular margin that forms the boundary between the enamel covering the crown and the cementum covering the root. Therefore, the cervical line separates the crown and root of the tooth."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Smooth muscle is a type of muscle without transverse striations in its constituent fibers; it is found in the walls of the viscera and blood vessels and in the dermis and is not under voluntary control (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure",
    "text" : "Smooth muscle is a type of non-striated involuntary muscle, which generally forms the supporting tissue of blood vessels and hollow internal organs, such as the stomach, intestine, and bladder. Smooth muscle is found within the tunica media, the middle layer, of a blood vessel. There is a larger amount of smooth muscle in the walls of arteries than veins, hence their greater wall thickness.\r\n\r\nThere are no striations in vascular smooth muscle, this is due to their lack of sarcomeres and unsymmetrical arrangement of cytoskeletal fibers when compared with skeletal muscle. Fibers in smooth muscle are smaller than those of striated muscle and their cells are spindle shaped with a single central nucleus.\r\n\r\nSmooth muscle of blood vessels is innervated predominantly by the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system (Mescher, 2013).\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Smooth muscle is found within the tunica media of blood vessels."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Smooth muscle is responsible for changing the volume of blood that passes through the vessel. This is achieved through vasoconstriction or vasodilation resulting in changes of lumen diameter. This vascular smooth muscle also provides the vessel with structural integrity giving support to the vessel at high pressure (Pawlina, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tHypertension\r\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
