[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "An artery is a vessel through which the blood passes away from the heart to the various parts of the body. The wall of an artery consists typically of an outer coat (tunica externa), a middle coat (tunica media), and an inner coat (tunica intima) (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "Arteries are types of blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart. They are characterized by their thick walls that can withstand the high blood pressure forces created by the heart. Arteries are classified into three types:\r\n•\telastic (large or conducting) arteries\r\n•\tmuscular (medium or distributing) arteries\r\n•\tarterioles (or small arteries).\r\n\r\nThe walls of arteries consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe three layers of the arterial wall vary in composition. \r\n•\tThe tunica interna is composed of a single layer of compacted endothelial cells, known as the endothelium. The endothelium is one cell thick and lines the lumen. The tunica interna is enclosed by the basement membrane which is made of loose connective tissue to provide support to the endothelium. The tunica interna also consists of an outer subendothelial layer of connective tissue. A fenestrated layer of elastic tissue, the internal elastic lamina, separates the tunica interna from the tunica media. \r\n•\tThe tunica media is the thickest layer of arteries and consists mainly of smooth muscle that controls the diameter of the lumen to regulate blood flow and blood pressure. Separating the tunica media from the tunica externa is a layer of elastic tissue called the external elastic lamina. \r\n•\tThe tunica externa encapsulates the vessel in the surrounding tissue. This layer consists of connective tissue with varying amounts of elastic and collagenous fibers.\r\n\r\nThe lumen of an artery (the central hollow region of a blood vessel through which blood flows) is smaller than that of a vein of comparable size. It is this smaller lumen that enables arteries to ensure the pressure needed to maintain blood flow is constant. The thick walls of arteries and the smaller diameter lumens mean arteries have a round appearance in cross section compared to veins.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Arteries carry blood away from the heart and to all body organs and tissues. Their thick muscular walls facilitate this function, enabling blood to be pumped under high pressure around the body. Blood that is transported through arteries is typically oxygen-rich, with the exception of the pulmonary arteries, which carry deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs. \r\n\r\nAnother function of arteries is the regulation of systemic blood flow. Through vasodilation and vasoconstriction, an arteries’ lumen can be increased or decreased in diameter, respectively. This change in the lumen allows greater or lesser blood flow through the vessel. \r\n\r\nArteries play an important role in the transport of vital molecules around the body. These molecules include dissolved gases, metabolic waste, nutrients, cells of the immune system and hormones.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica externa of a blood vessel is the outer, fibroelastic coat of vessels, usually in contact with surrounding tissues (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nRecent studies have suggested that the tissue arrangement of tunica externa is not a densely packed wall-like structure, commonly seen in alcohol fixed tissue. In fact, it is composed of a collagen lattice surrounded by fluid filled interstitial spaces (Benias et al., 2018). This has also been described in other tissues, such as lung, renal, dermis, and fascia.\r\n\r\nThe tunica externa possesses its own network of smaller blood vessels, known as the vasa vasorum, which provide the blood vessel with the molecules and nutrients necessary for cellular activity (Pawlina, 2016). Along with this small network of supportive blood vessels, the tunica externa is innervated by unmyelinated autonomic nerve fibers, the nervi vasorum (Mescher, 2013). The nervi vasorum are efferent autonomic fibers that play a role in vasodilation and vasoconstriction. \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa of elastic arteries is relatively thin and usually less than half the thickness of the tunica media. It is primarily composed of thick bundles of collagen running in a circular manner. Elastin in this layer forms a loose network and is not well organized like that seen in the tunica media.\r\n\r\nIn muscular arteries, the tunica externa is a relatively thick (about the same thickness as the tunica media) collagenous tissue and often separated from the tunica media by a discernable external elastic membrane. \r\n\r\nIn arterioles, the tunica externa is thin and poorly defined with loosely arranged elastin and collagen. It blends with the surrounding connective tissue the vessels travel within.\r\n \r\nThe tunica externa is the most prominent part of veins. In medium veins, the externa is almost entirely collagenous, but also contains longitudinally arranged smooth muscle. In large veins, the tunica externa is the thickest layer and is composed mainly of longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle surrounded by collagenous connective tissue. The tunica externa of postcapillary venules is absent but is thicker than the media in typical (muscular) venules."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica externa is the outermost layer of the blood vessel. It surrounds the tunica media."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The tunica externa acts as an anchor, securing the blood vessel to the surrounding body tissue. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Benias, P. C., Wells, R. G., Sackey-Aboagye, B., Klavan, H., Reidy, J., Buonocore, D., Miranda, M., Kornacki, S., Wayne, M., Carr-Locke, D. L. and Theise, N. D. (2018) 'Structure and Distribution of an Unrecognized Interstitium in Human Tissues', Scientific Reports, 8(1), pp. 4947.\r\nDorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica media a blood vessel is made up of transverse elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe tunica media is generally the largest layer within arteries and much thinner in corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of elastic arteries is modified by having multiple concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae wrapped around the vessel. The fenestrations allow nutrients to pass to the cells buried deep within the arteries walls. Smooth muscle cells are interspersed between the elastic laminae and synthesize and secrete collagen and elastin. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media is the thickest layer of muscular arteries and is dominated by concentric layers of smooth muscle, with a small number of thin elastic sheets and collagen fibers between them. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of arterioles and venules contain one or two layers of helically arranged smooth muscle. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of the good-sized medium veins (such as the brachial or basilic veins) has significant amounts of smooth muscle separated by sheets of collagen, although it’s much thinner than a corresponding muscular artery.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of large veins is not well developed and is relatively narrow. The presence of circumferentially arranged smooth muscle in this layer depends on the location of the vein, e.g., uterine veins have several layers of smooth muscle, while retinal veins have none (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021).\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica media separates the tunica interna from the tunica externa. In arteries, the external elastic membrane separates the tunica media from the tunica externa, while the internal elastic membrane divides the tunica media from the tunica interna."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The mechanical strength of the tunica media comes from structural collagen and smooth muscle. The smooth muscle coiled around the vessel can contract or relax, altering the diameter of the vessel’s lumen, which causes the lumen to decrease or increase. This is known as vasoconstriction and vasodilation, respectively.\r\n\r\nThe function of the tunica media of veins is distinctly different from that of arteries. Veins maintain the tone in their walls to regulate the volume of blood that is held in them. Veins can hold approximately 70% of the blood volume and can alter this reserve by contraction.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

