[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "A ribosome is a large molecular structure, approximately 12 nm wide and 25 nm long, having two dissociable subunits, that is the site of protein synthesis. Ribosomes found in the cytosol of eukaryotes have a sedimentation coefficient of 80S; the subunits have coefficients of 60S and 40S and together contain four different ribosomal RNA (rRNA) chains and about 80 different proteins. Ribosomes found in prokaryotes and mitochondria are smaller (70S, with 50S and 30S subunits containing a total of three rRNAs and 55 proteins) and also differ from eukaryotic ribosomes in their sensitivity to certain antibiotics (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Features",
    "text" : "Ribosomes are protein complexes and consist of rRNA synthesized in the nucleolus. They appear in electron micrographs as small, electron-dense particles 20–30 nm in two different forms. \n\nFirstly, they could be loosely lying within the cytoplasm. Sometimes, they're combined with a messenger RNA to produce a chain of ribosomes called polyribosomes. \n\nSecondly, ribosomes (and polyribosomes) can be attached to the membrane of the endoplasmic reticulum.\n\nBecause of the phosphate groups of rRNA act as anions, ribosomes are intensely basophilic and so will stain with basic dyes and hematoxylin (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Firstly, ribosomes position loosely within the cytoplasm or as a chain of ribosomes called polyribosomes. Secondly, ribosomes (and polyribosomes) can be attached to the membrane of the endoplasmic reticulum."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Individual ribosomes or polyribosomes in the cytoplasm of the cell are freely associated, meaning they are involved with making proteins that the cell uses for itself.\n\nProteins synthesized by those ribosomes in collaboration with the endoplasmic reticulum is destined to be exported from the cell or incorporated into the plasmalemma. In some cases, the proteins are stored within the cell e.g., in lysosomes and in the granules of white blood cells (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMcKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nOvalle, W. K. and Nahirney, P. C. (2021) Netter's Essential Histology: with Correlated Histopathology. 3rd edn.: Elsevier Health Science.\r\nRoss, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.\r\n"
  }
