[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Vascular endothelium is the layer of epithelial cells that lines the lumina of blood vessels; it originates from the mesoderm (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium is a key component of the tunica interna of a blood vessel. It forms the innermost layer surrounding the lumen, which separates the various cells and molecules within the blood from the surrounding tissue. The endothelium is therefore in direct contact with the bloodstream.\r\n\r\nThe endothelium is composed of a single layer of simple squamous endothelial cells. These cells form a continuous and uninterrupted sheet, or lining, around the lumen of the artery. \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The endothelium lies on the basement membrane of the blood vessel. This membrane affixes the endothelium to the connective tissue as well as providing the vessel wall with strength. The basement membrane is attached to the subendothelial connective tissue layer of the tunica interna.  "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium presides over the movement of cells in and out the blood as well as the exchange of molecules between the blood and surrounding tissues. Endothelium also influences blood flow by acting as a mechanoreceptor when shear stress is applied to the vessel and transmitting the signal to intracellular organelles resulting in an alteration in vascular tone (Akira and Joji, 1993).\r\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis \r\n•\tThrombosis\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica externa of a blood vessel is the outer, fibroelastic coat of vessels, usually in contact with surrounding tissues (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nRecent studies have suggested that the tissue arrangement of tunica externa is not a densely packed wall-like structure, commonly seen in alcohol fixed tissue. In fact, it is composed of a collagen lattice surrounded by fluid filled interstitial spaces (Benias et al., 2018). This has also been described in other tissues, such as lung, renal, dermis, and fascia.\r\n\r\nThe tunica externa possesses its own network of smaller blood vessels, known as the vasa vasorum, which provide the blood vessel with the molecules and nutrients necessary for cellular activity (Pawlina, 2016). Along with this small network of supportive blood vessels, the tunica externa is innervated by unmyelinated autonomic nerve fibers, the nervi vasorum (Mescher, 2013). The nervi vasorum are efferent autonomic fibers that play a role in vasodilation and vasoconstriction. \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa of elastic arteries is relatively thin and usually less than half the thickness of the tunica media. It is primarily composed of thick bundles of collagen running in a circular manner. Elastin in this layer forms a loose network and is not well organized like that seen in the tunica media.\r\n\r\nIn muscular arteries, the tunica externa is a relatively thick (about the same thickness as the tunica media) collagenous tissue and often separated from the tunica media by a discernable external elastic membrane. \r\n\r\nIn arterioles, the tunica externa is thin and poorly defined with loosely arranged elastin and collagen. It blends with the surrounding connective tissue the vessels travel within.\r\n \r\nThe tunica externa is the most prominent part of veins. In medium veins, the externa is almost entirely collagenous, but also contains longitudinally arranged smooth muscle. In large veins, the tunica externa is the thickest layer and is composed mainly of longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle surrounded by collagenous connective tissue. The tunica externa of postcapillary venules is absent but is thicker than the media in typical (muscular) venules. \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica externa is the outermost layer of the blood vessel. It surrounds the tunica media."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The tunica externa acts as an anchor, securing the blood vessel to the surrounding body tissue. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Benias, P. C., Wells, R. G., Sackey-Aboagye, B., Klavan, H., Reidy, J., Buonocore, D., Miranda, M., Kornacki, S., Wayne, M., Carr-Locke, D. L. and Theise, N. D. (2018) 'Structure and Distribution of an Unrecognized Interstitium in Human Tissues', Scientific Reports, 8(1), pp. 4947.\r\nDorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The vasa vasorum are the small nutrient arteries and the veins in the walls of the larger blood vessels (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure",
    "text" : "The vasa vasorum are a network of small blood and lymphatic vessels surrounding arteries and veins (Phillippi, 2022). Veins tend to have more extensive and well-developed vasa vasorum compared to arteries (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021).\r\n\r\nThe vessels of the vasa vasorum are composed of a single layer of endothelium surrounded by a very thin basement membrane.\r\n"
