[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica media a blood vessel is made up of transverse elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe tunica media is generally the largest layer within arteries and much thinner in corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of elastic arteries is modified by having multiple concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae wrapped around the vessel. The fenestrations allow nutrients to pass to the cells buried deep within the arteries walls. Smooth muscle cells are interspersed between the elastic laminae and synthesize and secrete collagen and elastin. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media is the thickest layer of muscular arteries and is dominated by concentric layers of smooth muscle, with a small number of thin elastic sheets and collagen fibers between them. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of arterioles and venules contain one or two layers of helically arranged smooth muscle. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of the good-sized medium veins (such as the brachial or basilic veins) has significant amounts of smooth muscle separated by sheets of collagen, although it’s much thinner than a corresponding muscular artery.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media of large veins is not well developed and is relatively narrow. The presence of circumferentially arranged smooth muscle in this layer depends on the location of the vein, e.g., uterine veins have several layers of smooth muscle, while retinal veins have none (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021).\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica media separates the tunica interna from the tunica externa. In arteries, the external elastic membrane separates the tunica media from the tunica externa, while the internal elastic membrane divides the tunica media from the tunica interna."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The mechanical strength of the tunica media comes from structural collagen and smooth muscle. The smooth muscle coiled around the vessel can contract or relax, altering the diameter of the vessel’s lumen, which causes the lumen to decrease or increase. This is known as vasoconstriction and vasodilation, respectively.\r\n\r\nThe function of the tunica media of veins is distinctly different from that of arteries. Veins maintain the tone in their walls to regulate the volume of blood that is held in them. Veins can hold approximately 70% of the blood volume and can alter this reserve by contraction.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica intima of a blood vessel is made up of endothelial cells surrounded by longitudinal elastic fibers and connective tissue (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is formed by an endothelium with a supporting basement membrane, a subendothelial connective tissue, and an internal elastic membrane in larger arteries. \r\n\r\nThe endothelial layer arises from a single continuous layer of endothelial cells. These cells are in direct contact with blood in the lumen. The endothelium sits on the basement membrane, which is composed of the basal lamina and the reticular lamina. \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is relatively thick in elastic arteries, making up to 20% of its wall thickness (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021). Its subendothelial layer of connective tissue composed of elastin and collagen embedded in a ground substance, all of which are secreted by scattered smooth muscle cells within the layer. An internal elastic membrane is present on the outer aspect of the tunica interna, although difficult to identify as it merges with the numerous elastic layers of the tunica media.\r\n\r\nThe tunica interna of muscular arteries is thin with a sparse underlying connective tissue. Immediately beneath, a thick band of elastic fibers forms the internal elastic lamina. Its contraction gives a folded appearance to the interna (Ross and Pawlina, 2016). \r\n\r\nIn arterioles, the endothelial cells of the tunica interna are smaller and the internal elastic membrane is often absent in the smallest arterioles (Standring, 2016). \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna of veins is thin. In medium veins, it contains small bundles of circularly arranged smooth muscle. Medium veins often contain valves, which are projections of the tunica interna. Valves permit blood flow in one direction and prevent the backflow, or retrograde flow, of blood.\r\n\r\nThe boundary of the tunica interna of large veins with the tunica media is not easily discernable. There is a single layer of endothelium resting on an incomplete basement membrane. The subendothelial layer is thin and contains elastic fibers and some smooth muscle cells.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "In smaller arterioles or venules, the tunica interna is usually a single coating of cells, although thickness can differ in larger vessels such as the aorta. Surrounding the tunica interna is a narrow membrane of elastic fibers running parallel to the vessel. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The tunica interna acts as a barrier between the blood in the lumen and the vessel walls. This barrier function results in the tunica interna partly assisting in the regulation of capillary exchange. Through release of endothelins from the endothelium, the tunica interna can alter blood pressure (Pawlina, 2016). These local chemicals can cause constriction of the smooth muscle within the walls of the vessel. The tunica interna also impedes thrombosis through hindering attachment of blood cells to the vessel wall."
  },
