  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The external elastic membrane (or lamina) is considered to be part of the tunica externa, separating it from the tunica media. The external elastic membrane is composed of sheets of fenestrated elastic connective tissue. The fenestrations allow nutrients to pass to the cells buried deep within the arteries walls.\r\n\r\nThe external elastic membrane is most prominent in muscular arteries, thin in elastic arteries (if present), and absent in arterioles and veins.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The external elastic membrane lies between the tunica media and the tunica externa."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The external elastic lamina serves to provide a readily deformable, compliant structure surrounding the lumen of the vessel to enable diameter changes when needed."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The internal elastic membrane is a fenestrated elastic membrane that constitutes the outermost component of the tunica intima of arteries (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The internal elastic membrane (or lamina) is a layer found in the tunica interna of the blood vessel. It is composed of elastic fibers that are arranged concentrically to form laminae.\r\n\r\nAn internal elastic membrane is difficult to identify in elastic arteries as it merges with the numerous elastic layers of the tunica media. In muscular arteries, the internal elastic lamina if much more conspicuous. Its contraction gives a folded appearance to the tunica interna (Ross and Pawlina, 2016). It internal elastic lamina is absent in smaller arterioles and not present in veins. \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The internal elastic membrane is found separating the tunica media from the tunica interna."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The internal elastic membrane acts as a physical barrier. The fenestrations within the sheath are thought to act as low-resistance pathways for heightened communication between vascular endothelial and smooth muscle cell layers."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tChronic allograft nephropathy\r\n•\tFungal rhinosinusitis\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."
