[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "A microtubule is a tubular cytoplasmic structure, 25 nm in diameter and of variable length, composed of α- and β-tubulin dimers, whose orientation imparts polarity; microtubules are involved in maintenance of cell shape and in the movements of organelles and inclusions and form the spindle fibers of mitosis. In cilia and flagella, they are constantly arranged with two single microtubules in the center and nine pairs of doublets arrayed around the central two. A neural microtubule is a microtubule occurring in a neuron (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)", "text": "Microtubules are dynamic tubes of polymerized tubulin molecules that can rapidly form and then disassemble. Microtubules are 20-25 nm in diameter with the tube wall about 5 nm thick and radiate out to the cell periphery from a microtubule organizing center (MTOC), or centrosome, which is usually located close to the cell nucleus. Therefore, they maybe several micrometers in length (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "Microtubules radiate out to the cell periphery from a microtubule organizing center (MTOC)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Microtubules are involved in:

- maintaining cell shape;

- facilitating or creating a scaffold or guide for vesicular transport, and movement of organelles;

- attachment of chromosomes to the spindle and chromosome movements during mitosis and meiosis;

- motility of cilia and also the tail piece of the mature sperm (flagellum);

- cell migration."}, {"name": "Clinical Correlates", "text": "Any defect in the protein components (or the assembly) of microtubules can affect cilia motility, and therefore non-clearance of secretions on respiratory tract epithelium, impaired ciliary transport of the ovum in the uterine tube and so infertility in women, and sperm motility and so male sterility (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

McKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.

Ovalle, W. K., Nahirney, P. C. and Netter, F. H. (2013) Netter's Essential Histology. ClinicalKey 2012: Elsevier Saunders.

Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Location: Vertebral column.

Bone Type: Irregular bone.

