  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Elastic arteries are arterial trunks characterized by large size and elasticity, such as the aorta, subclavian artery, common carotid artery, brachiocephalic trunk, and pulmonary trunk (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "Elastic arteries (conducting or large arteries), such as the aorta, are located close to the heart and must withstand the pressure pulses generated by the heart. To cope, elastic arteries contain large amounts of elastin, allowing them to function as a temporary pressure reservoir. Their resilient walls are thick, however, in proportion to the size of the lumen, the walls are thinner than that of muscular arteries. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of elastic arteries is modified by having multiple concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae wrapped around the vessel. The fenestrations allow nutrients to pass to the cells buried deep within the arteries walls. Smooth muscle cells are interspersed between the elastic laminae and synthesize and secrete collagen and elastin. \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is relatively thick in elastic arteries, making up to 20% of its wall thickness (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021). The luminal surface of the interna is lined by endothelium, supported by an underlying basement membrane. A subendothelial layer of connective tissue composed of elastin and collagen embedded in a ground substance, all of which are secreted by scattered smooth muscle cells within the layer. An internal elastic membrane is present on the outer aspect of the tunica interna, although difficult to identify as it merges with the numerous elastic layers of the tunica media.\r\n\r\nThe tunica externa is primarily composed of thick bundles of collagen running in a circular manner. Elastin in this layer forms a loose network and is not well organized like that seen in the tunica media. The tunica externa also contains small blood vessels, the vasa vasorum and lymphatic capillaries that also extend into the tunica media. They deliver nutrients and oxygen into the vessel wall and remove waste products via corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThere are also nervi vasorum, commonly called vasoconstrictor nerves. These are unmyelinated postsynaptic sympathetic nerve fibers that release norepinephrine/noradrenaline resulting in vasoconstriction, i.e., narrowing of the lumen of the blood vessel.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Elastic arteries are those that are closest to the heart, including the aorta and its initial branches. They deliver blood to muscular arteries."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Elastic arteries must withstand the pressure pulses generated by the heart. To cope, elastic arteries contain large amounts of collagen and elastin. Collagen provides tensile strength to the walls of elastic arteries preventing them from expanding beyond their limit under high blood pressures. Elastin allows distensibility of the arterial wall."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis \r\n•\tAortic Aneurysms\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nOvalle, W. K. and Nahirney, P. C. (2021) Netter's Essential Histology: with Correlated Histopathology. 3rd edn.: Elsevier Health Science.\r\n"
  }
]

[{"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "The dorsal carpometacarpal ligaments are a series of fibrous bands that attach the bases of the metacarpal bones to the carpal bones on the dorsal aspect of the hand. The ligaments are arranged as follows:

—the second metacarpal has two ligaments attaching to the trapezium and trapezoid;

—the third metacarpal had two ligaments attaching to the trapezoid and capitate;

—the fourth metacarpal had two ligaments attaching to the capitate and hamate;

—the fifth metacarpal has a single ligament attaching to the hamate.

The first carpometacarpal joint also contains a dorsal carpometacarpal ligament which extends obliquely from the ulnar side of the base of the first metacarpal to the dorsal surface of the trapezium."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "The dorsal carpometacarpal ligaments stabilize the carpometacarpal joints and permit limited gliding movements of the joints produced by the long flexor and extensor muscles of the digits."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Muscular arteries are most of the arteries except the elastic arteries; of muscular type, they extend from the large vessels to the arterioles (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "Muscular (distributing or medium) arteries, such as the brachial and femoral arteries, have more smooth muscle than elastic arteries. This allows muscular arteries to regulate blood flow to various parts of the body by contraction and relaxation of the smooth muscle in their walls. Muscular arteries have thick walls relative to the lumen.\r\n\r\nThe tunica media is the thickest layer of muscular arteries and is dominated by concentric layers of smooth muscle, with a small number of thin elastic sheets and collagen fibers between them. \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna of muscular arteries is thin and is lined with a squamous endothelium with a sparse underlying connective tissue. Immediately beneath, a thick band of elastic fibers forms the internal elastic lamina. Its contraction gives a folded appearance to the interna (Ross and Pawlina, 2016). \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa is a thick layer of dense, collagenous connective tissue that typically blends with surrounding connective tissue. Its external elastic membrane consists of elastic fibers that run along the length of the vessel. \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa also contains small blood vessels, the vasa vasorum and lymphatic capillaries that also extend into the tunica media. They deliver nutrients and oxygen into the vessel wall and remove waste products via corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThere are also nervi vasorum, commonly called vasoconstrictor nerves. These are unmyelinated postsynaptic sympathetic nerve fibers that release norepinephrine/noradrenaline resulting in vasoconstriction, i.e., narrowing of the lumen of the blood vessel.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Muscular arteries receive blood from elastic arteries and distribute blood to arterioles."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The smooth muscle of muscular arteries contracts tonically to regulate blood flow to various parts of the body."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis "
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nRoss, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2016) Histology: A Text and Atlas. Wolters Kluwer Health.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nervi vasorum are the nerve branches that supply the adventitia of the blood vessels (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure",
    "text" : "Efferent autonomic fibers innervate blood vessels via the nervi vasorum (Mescher, 2013). These nervi vasorum are composed of unmyelinated axons in little parcels. These axons are predominantly postganglionic fibers originating from sympathetic ganglionic neurons."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The nervi vasorum branch and connect within the tunica externa of vessels, forming a meshwork around it. The density of innervation varies in different vessels and in different areas of the body."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The nervi vasorum regulate muscular tone, and therefore, blood vessel diameter."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Large veins, such as the vena cava, have a relatively large lumen and narrow walls, they extend from the medium vessels to the heart."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "Large veins, such as the superior and inferior vena cavae, return blood to the heart under low pressure. \r\n\r\nThe tunica media of large veins is not well developed and is relatively narrow. The presence of circumferentially arranged smooth muscle in this layer depends on the location of the vein, e.g., uterine veins have several layers of smooth muscle, while retinal veins have none (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021).\r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is thin and its boundary with the tunica media is not easily discernable. There is a single layer of endothelium resting on an incomplete basement membrane. The subendothelial layer is thin and contains elastic fibers and some smooth muscle cells.\r\n\r\nThe tunica externa is the thickest layer of large veins and is composed mainly of longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle surrounded by collagenous connective tissue. \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa also contains small blood vessels, the vasa vasorum, and lymphatic capillaries that also extend into the tunica media. They deliver nutrients and oxygen into the vessel wall and remove waste products via corresponding veins.\r\n\r\nThere are also nervi vasorum, commonly called vasoconstrictor nerves. These are unmyelinated postsynaptic sympathetic nerve fibers that release norepinephrine/noradrenaline resulting in vasoconstriction, i.e., narrowing of the lumen of the blood vessel.\r\n"
