    "text" : "The hip region and leg is innervated by nerves that arise from the lumbosacral plexus. The lumbosacral plexus itself is made up of the lumbar plexus, the sacral plexus, and the coccygeal nerve.

The lumbar plexus is comprised of the ventral divisions of the first four lumbar spinal nerves which contribute towards the makeup of the femoral, genitofemoral, lateral femoral cutaneous, superior cluneal, obturator, ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves. The lumbar plexus also contributes a tract called the lumbosacral trunk, made up of the anterior divisions of the fifth and fourth lumbar nerves, to the sacral plexus.

The sacral plexus is a nervous plexus comprised of the anterior divisions of the first four sacral spinal nerves, along with a contribution from the fourth and fifth lumbar nerves. The sacral plexus gives rise to the common fibular and tibial nerve roots that converge, and descend as the sciatic nerve. The common fibular and tibial nerves separate from the sciatic nerve in the thigh. 

Other branches of the sacral plexus include the posterior femoral cutaneous, medial cluneal, pudendal, and superior and inferior gluteal nerves.

The fifth sacral nerves innervate the coccygeal muscles, and contribute branches to the coccygeal nerve to form the anococcygeal nerve, which innervates the skin of the back of the coccyx."
