[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ascending branch is one of the two terminal branches of the colic branch of ileocolic artery, the other being the descending branch.

From its origin, it travels superiorly along the ascending colon. Along its path, it gives off the straight arteries of colon, and it ends by anastomosing with the descending branch of right colic artery. 

Overall, the ascending branch of colic branch of ileocolic artery and its branches provide an arterial supply to the ascending colon.

The ascending branch of colic branch of ileocolic artery is just one of the multiple arteries that contribute to the formation of the marginal artery of colon. The marginal artery of colon is an anastomotic channel that is formed by the combination of multiple arteries that are arranged in series along the large intestine. It travels adjacent to the ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, and ends at the rectosigmoid junction. As it travels along the colon, the arteries that make up the marginal artery of colon give off the straight arteries of colon, which provide an arterial supply to the colon."
  }
]

[
