[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior mesenteric lymph nodes are a group of lymph nodes situated around the superior mesenteric artery that receive lymph from the large and small intestines. \n\nSpecifically, the superior mesenteric lymph nodes receive lymph efferents from the mesenteric lymph nodes of the ileum and jejunum, and the ileocolic, mesocolic, right colic, and middle colic lymph nodes of the large intestine. The superior mesenteric lymph nodes drain to the pre-aortic lymph nodes and intestinal trunk, and then to the cisterna chyli."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Origin: Spinal nerves from the first thoracic down to the second lumbar nerves.\n\nCourse: Anteriorly to the sympathetic trunk.\n\nBranches: No branches.\n\nSupply: Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the entire body."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Origin",
    "text" : "The white communicating branches of the spinal nerves, or white ramus communicans, are short myelin rich axonal fasciculation’s that branch off from each spinal nerve, from the first thoracic nerve down to and including the second lumbar nerve."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Course",
    "text" : "The white communicating branches run a short distance from a spinal nerve anteriorly to the sympathetic trunk."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Branches",
    "text" : "There are no named branches."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Supplied Structures & Function",
    "text" : "Each white communicating branch carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers that will do one of four things:\n\n- synapse at a sympathetic chain ganglion at the same level; \n\n- ascend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a higher vertebral level;\n\n- descend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a lower vertebral level;\n\n- exit the sympathetic trunk without synapsing (splanchnic nerve). \n\nPostganglionic neurons that synapse with the preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the white rami will target blood vessels, sweat glands, and arrector pili muscles throughout the body wall, head, neck, and limbs, or in the case of splanchnic nerves, visceral organs."
  }
]

[
  {
