[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior gastric plexus (aka left gastro-epiploic plexus) is the autonomic plexus on the left gastric artery, and is one of the sympathetic plexuses of the abdominal viscera. \n\nIt is one of the ten secondary plexuses of the celiac ganglion, and thus receives innervation primarily from the greater splanchnic nerve. \n\nIt distributes these fibers along the lesser curvature of the stomach, and joins with some branches of the left vagus nerve, and some fibers that run with the right gastric artery."
