[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cell membrane is the structure enveloping a cell, enclosing the cytoplasm and forming a selective permeability barrier; it consists of a lipid bilayer with integral and peripheral proteins and contains channels and pumps to transport substances across the membrane, as well as receptors to receive stimuli from the external environment and transduce them into intracellular signals (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)",
    "text" : "The cell membrane (or plasmalemma) is a bi-layered membrane, containing primarily phospholipids, cholesterol, and protein molecules. About 7.5-10 nm in width, it can have integral proteins attached to either the internal or the external surface of the bilayer. \n\nThe external surface of the cell membrane contains a carbohydrate-rich region termed the glycocalyx which links to cell membrane proteins and lipids (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The cell membrane is the external boundary of the cell."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The cell membrane is the barrier between the very stable internal environment of the cell and the external environment. It allows transfer of materials across the cell membrane. Oxygen, nutrients, waste products, and other materials, pass in and out of the cell through the cell membrane using a very detailed set of transport processes. Bulk transfer of material occurs through the cell membrane. The bulk intake of materials is called endocytosis and the release of bulk materials is exocytosis. Both of these processes involve different proteins and other molecules that associate with the cell membrane.\n\nThe integral proteins perform very important functions:\n\n- form intercellular junctions;\n\n- create pumps or channels to allow ions and molecules to go in and out of the cell;\n\n- serve as receptors;\n\n- act as enzymes on the surface of the cell;\n\n- act in cell recognition. \n\nThe glycocalyx plays a role in cell recognition and attachments to other cells and extracellular molecules (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disease and is partly due to a defect in the proteins that affect chloride ion (Cl-) channels within the cell membrane. \n\nMany glycoproteins and glycolipids in the cell membrane act as receptors that mediate various cell activities, cell adhesions, and responses to chemical messengers. A number of diseases are a result of defective receptors e.g., target cells of some hormones do not respond because they lack the relevant, competent receptors (McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nMcKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\n\nOvalle, W. K., Nahirney, P. C. and Netter, F. H. (2013) Netter's Essential Histology. ClinicalKey 2012: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nRoss, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "A microtubule is a tubular cytoplasmic structure, 25 nm in diameter and of variable length, composed of α- and β-tubulin dimers, whose orientation imparts polarity; microtubules are involved in maintenance of cell shape and in the movements of organelles and inclusions and form the spindle fibers of mitosis. In cilia and flagella, they are constantly arranged with two single microtubules in the center and nine pairs of doublets arrayed around the central two. A neural microtubule is a microtubule occurring in a neuron (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)", "text": "Microtubules are dynamic tubes of polymerized tubulin molecules that can rapidly form and then disassemble. Microtubules are 20-25 nm in diameter with the tube wall about 5 nm thick and radiate out to the cell periphery from a microtubule organizing center (MTOC), or centrosome, which is usually located close to the cell nucleus. Therefore, they maybe several micrometers in length (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "Microtubules radiate out to the cell periphery from a microtubule organizing center (MTOC)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Microtubules are involved in:

