Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The diploic veins are veins of the skull, including the frontal, occipital, anterior temporal, and posterior temporal diploic veins, that form sinuses in the cancellous tissue between the laminae of the cranial bones. They send branches to the external and the internal lamina, the periosteum, and the dura mater, and empty in part inside and in part outside the skull, communicating with meningeal veins, dural sinuses, pericranial veins, and each other (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Description", "text": "The diploic veins are a valveless intraosseous network of veins draining the skull itself. These are situated in the diploe, the spongy layer of bone in the cranial vault, and run inside the diploic channels.

The diploic veins communicate with the dural venous sinuses and meningeal veins along the compact inner table of the skull and with the pericranial veins on the outer table via emissary veins (Jivraj et al., 2009; Garcia-Gonzalez et al., 2009).

The diploic veins can be categorized into four groups based on their location in the human skull:

—frontal; 

—anterior temporal;

—posterior temporal;

—occipital."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Garcia-Gonzalez, U., Cavalcanti, D. D., Agrawal, A., Gonzalez, L. F., Wallace, R. C., Spetzler, R. F. and Preul, M. C. (2009) 'The diploic venous system: surgical anatomy and neurosurgical implications', Neurosurg Focus, 27(5), pp. E2.

Jivraj, K., Bhargava, R., Aronyk, K., Quateen, A. and Walji, A. (2009) 'Diploic venous anatomy studied in-vivo by MRI', Clin Anat, 22(3), pp. 296-301."}]

