    "text" : "Periapical radiograph shows normal dental and periodontal anatomy. The periodontal ligament (PDL) space is a thin radiolucent line that surrounds the root of the tooth. The lamina dura is a thin radiopaque line that surrounds the tooth socket radiographically. Healthy alveolar bone crests (crestal laminae dura) are corticated. Nutrient canals may appear as small corticated canals within the bone connected to the apical foramen."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Imaging Technique",
    "text" : "A radiograph, or X-ray, is a 2Dimensional image produced by passing high-energy ionizing radiation (X-rays) through the body. As the radiation passes through the body, different tissue types absorb different amounts of radiation. The remaining unabsorbed radiation hits a detector behind the body, thus generating an image. Conventional radiographs are limited to five basic densities. These include (from least to most dense):

- air, which appears blackest;

- fat, which is a lighter shade of grey than air;

- soft tissue or fluid;

- calcium (within bone);

- metal (present in prosthetic implants), which appears the whitest.

 

Bone tends to absorb more radiation than soft tissue or fat, thus bones produce a higher contrast on an x-ray detector and appear white. X-rays travelling though less dense tissues, like fat, or air-filled cavities passes more easily and display different shades of grey on a radiograph.

A periapical radiograph is used to obtain a radiograph of individual teeth and their surrounding tissue. Each radiograph generally shows two to four teeth. Periapical radiography may be used to identify apical infections or cysts, assessing trauma to that area, evaluating the position of unerupted teeth, among others."
  },
