[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Sustentocytes, or Sertoli cells, are cells elongated cells in the seminiferous tubules, to which the spermatids become attached; they provide support, protection, and possibly nutrition until the spermatids become transformed into mature spermatozoa (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure/Morphology", "text": "Sustentocytes, more commonly known as Sertoli cells, are non-dividing, support cells found in the seminiferous tubules and may occupy 25% of epithelial volume. Each Sertoli cell partly or completely surrounds several dozen germ cells (Standring, 2020).

Adjacent Sertoli cells have long cytoplasmic processes that extend above the basal spermatogonia and interface with each other, forming specialized tight cell junctions. This organization essentially creates the blood testis barrier, which creates basal and adluminal compartments within the seminiferous tubules and prevents intercellular transport of substances. The tight junctions can disassemble, enabling spermatogonia that lose their connection with the basement membrane to migrate to the adluminal compartment.

Sertoli cells are variable in shape although mostly appear as a pyramidal cell. Their basal ends sit on the basement membrane, while their apical ends extend into the lumen of the seminiferous tubule."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "In the adult testis, Sertoli cells occupy up to 25% of the volume of the seminiferous epithelium."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Sertoli cells are the support for spermatogenesis. They have complex recesses in their cell membranes which envelop spermatogonia, spermatids, and spermatozoa.

Sertoli cells secrete protein-rich fluid that facilitates transport of spermatozoa from the seminiferous tubules to excurrent ducts. They also secrete hormones, such as inhibin B and androgen-binding protein (ABP). ABP concentrates testosterone in the adluminal compartment of the seminiferous tubule which is essential for spermatogenesis.

The position of supporting sustentocytes (Sertoli cells) on the basement membrane of the seminiferous epithelium, and the interconnecting tight junctions between cytoplasmic processes from adjacent Sertoli cells, subdivides the epithelium of the convoluted seminiferous tubules into basal and adluminal compartments, thereby dividing immature and mature progenitors into distinct anatomical and functional compartments. This is necessary for:

—protecting meiotic and post-meiotic germ cells from immunological risks from peripheral tissues;

—maintaining a basal stem cell environment;

—preventing intercellular fluid and macromolecular flow into the seminiferous epithelium;

—controlling exposure of basal germ cells to regulatory factors produced by Leydig and myoid cells."}, {"name": "List of Clinical Correlates", "text": "The blood testis barrier can be disrupted by trauma or inflammatory events, leading to subfertility."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Standring, S. (2020) Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice. 42nd edn.: Elsevier Health Sciences."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pituitary gland is a heterogeneous body located inferior to the base of the brain in the sella turcica, attached by a stalk to the hypothalamus, from which it receives important neural and vascular outflow. It consists of two lobes of differing embryonic origin, the anterior lobe (pars distalis adenohypophysis), which secretes most of the hormones, and the posterior lobe (pars nervosa neurohypophysis), which stores and releases neurohormones that it receives from the hypothalamus, and an intervening part (pars intermedia adenohypophysis) (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The infundibular stalk is the superior part of the neurohypophysis; it contains the neural connections of the pituitary gland and is continuous with the tuber cinereum and infundibulum of the hypothalamus (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The supraoptic nucleus is a sharply defined nucleus of nerve cells in the anterior hypothalamic region, immediately above the lateral part of the optic chiasm; many of its cells are neurosecretory in function, secreting vasopressin, which is carried to the neurohypophysis posterior pituitary) by the fibers of the supraopticohypophysial tract; other cells are osmoreceptors, which respond to increased osmotic pressure to signal the release of vasopressin by the neurohypophysis (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Origin: Connects the two cavernous sinuses on either side of sella turcica.\r\n\r\nCourse: Lies within the anterior attached border of the diaphragma sellae.\r\n\r\nTributaries: Small irregular sinuses inferior to the pituitary gland drain into the intercavernous sinuses.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus is a group of nerve cell bodies found in the inferior portion of the intermediate hypothalamic region; it is involved in diverse functions, e.g., food intake and sexual behavior (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The neurohypophysis is the neural part of the pituitary gland, which originates as an evagination from the floor of the embryonic diencephalon and is the neural portion of the gland. It consists of the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland, which is its main body, and the infundibular stalk, which is continuous with the hypothalamus. The infundibulum hypothalami and median eminence are sometimes considered to be part of the neurohypophysis. It serves as a reservoir for the hypothalamic neurohormones vasopressin, oxytocin, and the neurophysins, releasing them as needed (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica externa of a blood vessel is the outer, fibroelastic coat of vessels, usually in contact with surrounding tissues (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nRecent studies have suggested that the tissue arrangement of tunica externa is not a densely packed wall-like structure, commonly seen in alcohol fixed tissue. In fact, it is composed of a collagen lattice surrounded by fluid filled interstitial spaces (Benias et al., 2018). This has also been described in other tissues, such as lung, renal, dermis, and fascia.\r\n\r\nThe tunica externa possesses its own network of smaller blood vessels, known as the vasa vasorum, which provide the blood vessel with the molecules and nutrients necessary for cellular activity (Pawlina, 2016). Along with this small network of supportive blood vessels, the tunica externa is innervated by unmyelinated autonomic nerve fibers, the nervi vasorum (Mescher, 2013). The nervi vasorum are efferent autonomic fibers that play a role in vasodilation and vasoconstriction. \r\n\r\nThe tunica externa of elastic arteries is relatively thin and usually less than half the thickness of the tunica media. It is primarily composed of thick bundles of collagen running in a circular manner. Elastin in this layer forms a loose network and is not well organized like that seen in the tunica media.\r\n\r\nIn muscular arteries, the tunica externa is a relatively thick (about the same thickness as the tunica media) collagenous tissue and often separated from the tunica media by a discernable external elastic membrane. \r\n\r\nIn arterioles, the tunica externa is thin and poorly defined with loosely arranged elastin and collagen. It blends with the surrounding connective tissue the vessels travel within.\r\n \r\nThe tunica externa is the most prominent part of veins. In medium veins, the externa is almost entirely collagenous, but also contains longitudinally arranged smooth muscle. In large veins, the tunica externa is the thickest layer and is composed mainly of longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle surrounded by collagenous connective tissue. The tunica externa of postcapillary venules is absent but is thicker than the media in typical (muscular) venules, \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The tunica externa is the outermost layer of the blood vessel. It surrounds the tunica media."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The tunica externa acts as an anchor, securing the blood vessel to the surrounding body tissue. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Benias, P. C., Wells, R. G., Sackey-Aboagye, B., Klavan, H., Reidy, J., Buonocore, D., Miranda, M., Kornacki, S., Wayne, M., Carr-Locke, D. L. and Theise, N. D. (2018) 'Structure and Distribution of an Unrecognized Interstitium in Human Tissues', Scientific Reports, 8(1), pp. 4947.\r\nDorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer.\r\n"
  }
]

