—a “brush border” characterized by long, straight and closely packed microvilli;

—a junctional complex that prevents continuity between the intercellular space and tubule lumen;

—zonula adherens, which establish strong adhesion between adjacent cells;

—significant folding and interdigitations of both lateral and basal plasma membrane of the adjacent cell;

—basal striations, which appear as elongated, vertically oriented mitochondria accumulate at the basal surface of the cells (Ross and Pawlina, 2006).

Basal interdigitating processes of the adjacent cells contain numerous microfilaments 6 nm in diameter."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "Simple cuboidal epithelium lines the proximal convoluted tubule."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Cuboidal cells of the proximal convoluted tubule reabsorb about 65% of the fluid from the glomerular filtrate due to the NA+/K+-ATPase pumps and aquaporin-1 (AQP-1) transmembrane proteins. Most small polypeptides, amino acids, and glucose are also reabsorbed across the proximal convoluted tubules. The microfilaments regulate fluid movement from the basolateral extracellular space across the basal lamina to a nearby capillary (Ross and Pawlina, 2006)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "A neuron is any of the excitable, conducting cells of the nervous system. A typical neuron consists of a cell body, containing the nucleus and the surrounding cytoplasm (perikaryon); several short radiating processes (dendrites); and one long process (the axon), which terminates in twiglike branches (telodendrons) and may have branches (collaterals) projecting along its course. The axon together with its covering or sheath forms the nerve fiber (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Neurons, or nerve cells, are the functional units of the nervous system. The functional components of all neurons are the dendrites, cell body, axon, and synaptic junctions.\n\nNeuron cell bodies in the central nervous system are either dispersed within the gray matter or are clustered into nuclei, columns or layers. In contrast, neuron cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system are confined within ganglia (Mancall and Brock, 2011). \n\nNeurons cannot divide or replicate as they lack centrioles that are required for cell division. Despite this, neural stem cells are present in some areas of the brain and are responsible for replacing damaged nerve cells by differentiating into neurons (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Splittgerber, 2018)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Neuronal Classification",
    "text" : "Neurons are generally classified into three functional categories which are the sensory neurons, motor neurons, and interneurons.\n\nNeurons can also be classified into three structural categories depending on the number of processes, i.e., dendrites and axons, that extend from the neuronal cell body. These three categories are the unipolar, bipolar, and multipolar neurons. \n\nSome neurons are often classified, depending on the size of their axon, into Golgi type I neurons and Golgi type II neurons. Golgi type I neurons have a very long axon (up to one meter or more in length). They are motor neurons in the CNS e.g., Purkinje cells in the cerebellar cortex and pyramidal cells in the cerebral cortex, or motor nerve fibers in the PNS. Golgi type II neurons have a short axon, or may even be absent, and are numerous interneurons in the cerebral and cerebellar cortex (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Splittgerber, 2018)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Pseudounipolar Neurons",
    "text" : "A pseudounipolar (or unipolar) neuron has a single short axonal process that emerges from the cell body. It divides into two long axonal branches, one runs centrally into the central nervous system, the other extends peripherally to the receptors. The receptive ending of the peripheral axon receives sensory stimuli and transmits signals towards the cell body. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Bipolar Neurons",
    "text" : "A bipolar neuron has two processes, a single fused dendrite and an axon, which extend from opposite sides of the cell body. Bipolar neurons are rare and can be found in special sensory organs such as the olfactory epithelium of the nose, the inner ear, and retina of the eye, where they predominantly function as sensory neurons."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Multipolar Neurons",
    "text" : "A multipolar neuron usually has numerous dendrites and a single axon. More than 99% of neurons are multipolar neurons (Marieb, Wilhelm and Mallatt, 2012). \r\n\r\nMost multipolar neurons are found in the central nervous system and include motor neurons and interneurons. Multipolar neurons are also found in the peripheral nervous system in autonomic ganglia of the autonomic nervous system. \r\n\r\nAt the synaptic end of a multipolar neuron, various neurotransmitters are released and can affect other neurons to cause a muscle contraction or can act on the epithelium of glands to cause glandular secretion.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Neurons are located within the central and peripheral nervous systems. They also form the enteric nervous system. Neuroendocrine cells are located in the brain and elsewhere in the body."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Neurons are electrically excitable cells that process and transmit information. Neurons receive stimuli via their dendrites which conduct electrical nerve impulses towards the cell body. The axon of the neuron transmits electrical nerve impulses away from the cell body towards other neurons, cells, or glands. The functional components of all neurons are the dendrites, cell body, axon, and synaptic junctions."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
