[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The dentoperiosteal fibers are gingival fibers that pass from the lamina propria of the gingiva to the alveolar bone."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)", "text": "Gingival connective tissue is comprised of 60% fibroblasts, 5% vessels and nerves, and 35% matrix. There are four types of gingival fibers: 

—dentogingival group; 

—alveologingival group; 

—circular group;

—dentoperiosteal fibers (Rajendran and Selvam, 2015)."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "Dentoperiosteal fibers extend from the cement, travel over the crest, and incline apically between the lamina propria of the gingiva to the periosteum of the alveolar bone (Rajendran and Selvam, 2015)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Dentoperiosteal fibers, as part of the gingival fiber group, anchor the marginal gingiva to the teeth and provide sufficient rigidity to the teeth to withstand the forces of mastication (Rajendran and Selvam, 2015)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Rajendran, M. and Selvam, U. A. (2015) 'Transseptal fibers - crosslinking convolutes: A review', Int J Contemp Dent Med Rev."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The adenohypophysis is the glandular epithelial part of the pituitary gland, which originates in the roof of the stomodeum of the embryo; it secretes hormones that regulate the functioning of the thyroid gland, gonads, suprarenal cortex, and other endocrine organs. The adenohypophysis comprises the pars distalis (anterior lobe of the pituitary gland), which is its main body; the pars intermedia; and the pars tuberalis (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Neuroglia are the cells of the supportive tissue of the central nervous system; these nonneural cells are of three kinds: astrocytes, oligodendrocytes (collectively termed macroglia), and microglia (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Description", "text": "Neurons in the central nervous system are supported by non-excitable cells collectively referred to as neuroglia or neuroglial cells. They are smaller cells than most neurons, 5–10 times more numerous, and account for half the volume of the central nervous system (Ross and Pawlina, 2006).

In the central nervous system, there are four types of neuroglial cells:

—astrocytes;

—oligodendrocytes;

—microglia;

—ependymal cells.

The two supporting cells in the peripheral nervous system are the Schwann cells and satellite cells (Ross and Pawlina, 2006)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tunica intima of a blood vessel is made up of endothelial cells surrounded by longitudinal elastic fibers and connective tissue (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The walls of blood vessels consist of three main layers:\r\n•\tthe inner layer, the tunica interna (or intima)\r\n•\tthe middle layer, the tunica media\r\n•\tthe outer layer, the tunica externa (or adventitia).\r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is formed by an endothelium with a supporting basement membrane, a subendothelial connective tissue, and an internal elastic membrane in larger arteries. \r\n\r\nThe endothelial layer arises from a single continuous layer of endothelial cells. These cells are in direct contact with blood in the lumen. The endothelium sits on the basement membrane, which is composed of the basal lamina and the reticular lamina. \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna is relatively thick in elastic arteries, making up to 20% of its wall thickness (Ovalle and Nahirney, 2021). Its subendothelial layer of connective tissue composed of elastin and collagen embedded in a ground substance, all of which are secreted by scattered smooth muscle cells within the layer. An internal elastic membrane is present on the outer aspect of the tunica interna, although difficult to identify as it merges with the numerous elastic layers of the tunica media.\r\n\r\nThe tunica interna of muscular arteries is thin with a sparse underlying connective tissue. Immediately beneath, a thick band of elastic fibers forms the internal elastic lamina. Its contraction gives a folded appearance to the interna (Ross and Pawlina, 2016). \r\n\r\nIn arterioles, the endothelial cells of the tunica interna are smaller and the internal elastic membrane is often absent in the smallest arterioles (Standring, 2016). \r\n\r\nThe tunica interna of veins is thin. In medium veins, it contains small bundles of circularly arranged smooth muscle. Medium veins often contain valves, which are projections of the tunica interna. Valves permit blood flow in one direction and prevent the backflow, or retrograde flow, of blood.\r\n\r\nThe boundary of the tunica interna of large veins with the tunica media is not easily discernable. There is a single layer of endothelium resting on an incomplete basement membrane. The subendothelial layer is thin and contains elastic fibers and some smooth muscle cells.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "In smaller arterioles or venules, the tunica interna is usually a single coating of cells, although thickness can differ in larger vessels such as the aorta. Surrounding the tunica interna is a narrow membrane of elastic fibers running parallel to the vessel. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The tunica interna acts as a barrier between the blood in the lumen and the vessel walls. This barrier function results in the tunica interna partly assisting in the regulation of capillary exchange. Through release of endothelins from the endothelium, the tunica interna can alter blood pressure (Pawlina, 2016). These local chemicals can cause constriction of the smooth muscle within the walls of the vessel. The tunica interna also impedes thrombosis through hindering attachment of blood cells to the vessel wall."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tThrombosis"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\nPawlina, W. 2016. Histology: A text and atlas with correlated cell and molecular biology. 7th ed. Philadelphia: Wolters Kluwer."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nucleolus is a rounded, dense body, present in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells during interphase, that is the site of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) synthesis and ribosomal subunit assembly. It consists of (a) a fibrillar center, containing the rRNA genes, surrounded by (b) the pars fibrosa, a dense fibrillar component where transcription occurs, and (c) the pars granulosa, a granular component where the ribosomal subunits are assembled. Multiple nucleoli occur in some cells (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)",
    "text" : "The most predominant, non-membranous structure in the nucleus is the spherical nucleolus. It surrounds rRNA genes that are transcriptionally active. The nucleolus is the site of synthesis of ribosomal ribonucleic acid (rRNA), ribosomal production and assembly. The diameter of the nucleolus is 1–2 mm, but this varies within different populations of cells. In cells that are most active in synthesizing proteins, the nucleolus is well developed and prominent. It is common for some cells to contain more than one nucleolus (Mescher, 2013).\r\n\r\nLike heterochromatin, the nucleolus stains with basic dyes and hematoxylin.\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The nucleolus is located near the center of the nucleus."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The nucleolus synthesizes and assembles ribosomal RNA (rRNA) (Ross and Pawlina, 2006)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "Nucleoli house proteins for ribosome synthesis and oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes that control cell proliferation. Structural and functional faults in nucleoli have been the basis of a number of disease processes including some types of cancers (McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nMcKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\r\n"
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The distal convoluted tubule is a distal, convoluted part of the renal tubule, extending from the distal straight tubule to the collecting tubule (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)", "text": "The distal convoluted tubule is a continuation of the distal straight tubule (thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle). The tubule is much less tortuous than the proximal convoluted tubule. The fluid in the distal convoluted tubule passes into a collecting tubule which in turn empties the fluid into a collecting duct in the medullary ray."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "The distal convoluted tubule is a continuation of the distal straight tubule (thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "The distal convoluted tubule:

—reabsorbs Na+ and so conserves Na+ and secretes of K+ into the glomerular filtrate;

—reabsorbs bicarbonate ions and so secretion of H+ ions which acidifies the urine;

—reabsorbs chloride (CL-) ions;

—secretes ammonium ions (Ross and Pawlina, 2006)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The efferent glomerular arteriole is an arteriole that arises from a renal glomerulus and breaks up into capillaries to supply renal tubules (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Features", "text": "Efferent glomerular arterioles carry blood away from the glomerulus. The walls of efferent glomerular arterioles are thicker than their afferent counterparts, and therefore the lumen of efferent arterioles tends to be narrower. This narrow lumen creates resistance which increases the pressure of blood traveling through the glomerulus."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "Upon leaving the glomerulus, efferent arterioles divide into:

—peritubular capillaries surround the cortical portions of the renal tubule;

—straight arterioles surround tubules that lie in the outer and inner zones of the medulla."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Efferent arterioles are responsible for transporting blood away from the glomerulus."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."}]

