[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The epidermis is the superficial, nonvascular layer of the skin, varying in thickness from 0.07 to 0.12 mm, except on the palms and soles where it may be 0.8 to 1.4 mm. This keratinized stratified squamous epithelium comprises, from within outward, five layers: the stratum basale (basal layer); stratum spinosum (prickle cell or spinous layer); stratum granulosum (granular layer); stratum lucidum (clear layer); and stratum corneum (horny layer). In the thinner epidermis of the general body surface, the stratum lucidum is usually absent (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure/Morphology", "text": "The epidermis is the outermost layer of skin and is composed of a stratified squamous keratinized epithelium. A number of cell types can be found in the epithelium: 

—keratinocytes, which make up the vast majority of cells;

—Langerhans cells, the antigen-presenting cells; 

—Merkel cells that are responsible for tactility; 

—melanocytes, which produce melanin. 

While the epidermis is avascular i.e., containing no blood vessels, it is tightly bound to the underlying, highly vascularized dermis. This connection between the two layers is known as the dermoepidermal border. At this border, the epidermis has many downward projections into the dermis, known as epidermal ridges. Likewise, the dermis has alternated upward projections between the epidermal ridges, the dermal papillae. This organization at the dermoepidermal border increases the surface area of contact between the two layers, thus strengthening their connection.

The epidermis can be subdivided into two layers, a superficial layer and a deep layer. These two layers can be further subdivided into cellular layers. From superficial to deep, the superficial layer of the epidermis is composed of a stratum corneum, stratum lucidum, and stratum granulosum, while the deep layers of the epidermis is composed of a stratum spinosum and stratum basale. These cellular layers, or strata, undergo mitosis, differentiation, maturation, and keratinization, which results in cells being pushed from the deep layer into the superficial layer, where they can be shed from the skin's surface. These properties ensure that the skin is continuously renewing and replacing itself. The turnover of cells from the stratum basale to the stratum corneum varies from 20 to 75 days, however, in some diseases, such a psoriasis, the transit time can be as little as 8 days (Baroni et al., 2012).

It is important to note that skin is described as either thick or thin skin. Thick skin is only found in palmar skin of the hand and plantar skin, or sole, of the foot. The thickness of the epidermis of plantar skin can reach up to 1.4 mm. Thin skin, on the other hand, lacks the stratum lucidum in the superficial layer of the epidermis and the number of cells in the other strata is greatly reduced. Epidermal thickness of thin skin can range from 0.07-0.12 mm (Arda, Goksugur and Tuzun, 2014)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "The epidermis is continuously renewing its epithelial layers. This ensures that the epidermis acts as the body's first line of defense, acting as a barrier against environmental hazards, such as physical, chemical, biochemical, and immunological threats."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Arda, O., Goksugur, N. and Tuzun, Y. (2014) 'Basic histological structure and functions of facial skin', Clin Dermatol, 32(1), pp. 3-13.

Baroni, A., Buommino, E., De Gregorio, V., Ruocco, E., Ruocco, V. and Wolf, R. (2012) 'Structure and function of the epidermis related to barrier properties', Clin Dermatol, 30(3), pp. 257-62.

Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."}]

