[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "A multipolar neuron is a neuron with several to many processes; such neurons vary in shape, depending on the arrangement of the processes, with pyramidal and stellate (star) shapes being common (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/\r\nMorphology\r\n",
    "text" : "A multipolar neuron has one axon and one or more dendrites. The terminal ends of the axons contain neurotransmitters which are released at the synapse to affect other neurons, muscle cells, or glandular epithelial cells. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Most multipolar neurons are found in the central nervous system and include motor neurons and interneurons. Multipolar neurons are also found in the peripheral nervous system in autonomic ganglia of the autonomic nervous system. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Most of the multipolar neurons are motor neurons or interneurons. Impulses pass from dendrites to the cell body then along the axon or only from the cell body along the axon. This is because the receptor portions of the neuron receiving the stimulus are the dendrites or the cell body, and the axon is the conducting portion of the neuron (Ross and Pawlina, 2016)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\nRoss, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2016) Histology: A Text and Atlas. Wolters Kluwer Health.\r\n"
  }
