[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The blood-air barrier is a thin tissue barrier between pulmonary alveoli and adjacent capillaries, the site of gas exchange between alveolar air and capillary blood (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure", "text": "The air within the alveoli is separated from the blood within neighboring capillaries by several components collectively called the blood-air barrier:

—a thin layer of surfactant;

—plasma membrane and the cytoplasm of the Type I pneumocytes;

—basal laminae (often fused) of both the Type I pneumocytes and the capillary endothelial cell;

—plasma membrane and cytoplasm of the capillary endothelial cell.

The total thickness varies but is usually around 0.1 to 1.5 µm (Mescher, 2013).

Between the two basal laminae, there is often connective tissue and fibers present that widen the blood-air barrier producing a thin portion and a thick portion of the barrier."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Most gas exchange occurs across the thin portion of the blood-air barrier. Tissue fluid tends to accumulate in the thick portion. This fluid drains into lymph vessels within the connective tissue elements accompanying the terminal bronchioles (Ross and Pawlina, 2016)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Mescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.

Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2016) Histology: A Text and Atlas. Wolters Kluwer Health."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The terminal bouton is a button-like terminal enlargement of an axon that ends in relation to another neuron at a synapse (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Features",
    "text" : "Usually axons branch profusely at their termination. This is known as terminal arborization. Each branch of this arborization ends in a dilation called a terminal bouton or axon terminal.\r\n\r\nTerminal boutons are unmyelinated, specialized, presynaptic endings that are in close proximity with the postsynaptic plasmalemma or axolemma of target cells. This close relationship is referred to as synapses, which are junctions between neurons and other neurons or effector cells. Axon terminals can form:\r\n—axodendritic synapses, which are connections between axons and dendrites:\r\n—axosomatic synapses, which are connections of axons with cell bodies;\r\n—axo-axonic synapses, which are connections between other axons;\r\n—neuromuscular junctions, which are connections between nerve cells and muscles (Standring, 2016).\r\n\r\nTerminal boutons of the presynaptic neuron contain numerous small clear synaptic vesicles and large dense-core vesicles. These synaptic vesicles are membranous sacs filled with neurotransmitters. Most neurotransmitters are synthesized in the nerve cell body and transported to the axon terminals, although some neurotransmitters can be synthesized at axon terminals if the necessary precursors are provided by anterograde axonal transport. As nerve impulses end at the terminal boutons, neurotransmitters are released into the synaptic cleft by exocytosis and the added presynaptic membrane are recycled by endocytosis to form new synaptic vesicles (Mescher, 2013). \r\n\r\nTerminal boutons contain numerous mitochondria since energy is required for the release of neurotransmitters.\r\n\r\nPresynaptic axon terminals contain smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which synthesizes new cell membrane since, during exocytosis of the neurotransmitters, the cell membrane does not remain intact (Mescher, 2013).\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Terminal boutons form functional junctions between other neurons or effector cells. As nerve impulses end at the terminal boutons, neurotransmitters are released into the synaptic cleft and can either excite or inhibit other neurons or effector cells."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nMescher, A. (2013) Junqueira's Basic Histology: Text and Atlas. 13th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\n\nStandring, S. (2016) Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice. Gray's Anatomy Series 41 edn.: Elsevier Limited."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cranial dura is the dura mater covering the brain, composed of two mostly fused layers: an outer periosteal layer (endocranium) adherent to the inner aspect of the cranial bones, analogous to the periosteum of the bony skeleton, and an inner meningeal layer. Venous sinuses and the trigeminal ganglion are located between the layers (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The cell membrane is the structure enveloping a cell, enclosing the cytoplasm and forming a selective permeability barrier; it consists of a lipid bilayer with integral and peripheral proteins and contains channels and pumps to transport substances across the membrane, as well as receptors to receive stimuli from the external environment and transduce them into intracellular signals (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)", "text": "The cell membrane (or plasmalemma) is a bi-layered membrane, containing primarily phospholipids, cholesterol, and protein molecules. About 7.5-10 nm in width, it can have integral proteins attached to either the internal or the external surface of the bilayer. 

The external surface of the cell membrane contains a carbohydrate-rich region termed the glycocalyx which links to cell membrane proteins and lipids (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "Anatomical Relations", "text": "The cell membrane is the external boundary of the cell."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "The cell membrane is the barrier between the very stable internal environment of the cell and the external environment. It allows transfer of materials across the cell membrane. Oxygen, nutrients, waste products, and other materials, pass in and out of the cell through the cell membrane using a very detailed set of transport processes. Bulk transfer of material occurs through the cell membrane. The bulk intake of materials is called endocytosis and the release of bulk materials is exocytosis. Both of these processes involve different proteins and other molecules that associate with the cell membrane.

The integral proteins perform very important functions:

- form intercellular junctions;

- create pumps or channels to allow ions and molecules to go in and out of the cell;

- serve as receptors;

- act as enzymes on the surface of the cell;

- act in cell recognition. 

The glycocalyx plays a role in cell recognition and attachments to other cells and extracellular molecules (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013; McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "Clinical Correlates", "text": "Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disease and is partly due to a defect in the proteins that affect chloride ion (Cl-) channels within the cell membrane. 

Many glycoproteins and glycolipids in the cell membrane act as receptors that mediate various cell activities, cell adhesions, and responses to chemical messengers. A number of diseases are a result of defective receptors e.g., target cells of some hormones do not respond because they lack the relevant, competent receptors (McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016)."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

McKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.

Ovalle, W. K., Nahirney, P. C. and Netter, F. H. (2013) Netter's Essential Histology. ClinicalKey 2012: Elsevier Saunders.

Ross, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."}]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The paraventricular hypothalamic nucleus is a sharply defined band of cells in the wall of the third ventricle in the anterior hypothalamic region; many of its cells are neurosecretory in function, secreting oxytocin, which is carried to the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) by the fibers of the paraventriculohypophysial tract (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pars intermedia of the hypophysis is an ill-defined region between the anterior and posterior lobes of the pituitary gland. In humans a defined structure develops during embryogenesis, but only scattered cells are retained when the gland matures. It secretes β-endorphin, melanotropins, and other regulators (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

