[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "A pseudounipolar neuron is a unipolar neuron, almost always a primary sensory neuron, that was originally bipolar but whose two processes fused during development to form a single process that bifurcates at a distance from the cell body. The branches are called central and peripheral processes (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/\r\nMorphology\r\n",
    "text" : "A pseudounipolar (or unipolar) neuron, as the name suggests, has only one process, the axon. The axon divides into two long conducting processes close to the cell body (or soma). One process extends out to the periphery (to a sensory receptor) and the other extends into the central nervous system (brain or spinal cord)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "As most pseudounipolar neurons are sensory neurons, the cell body is located in a dorsal root ganglion (or spinal ganglion) or a cranial nerve nucleus."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "Most pseudounipolar neurons are sensory neurons. "
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nucleus is a spheroid body within a eukaryotic cell, separated from the cytoplasm by the nuclear envelope, and containing chromatin, a nucleolus, and nucleoplasm. In the nucleus, the cell's genetic information is stored on the chromosomes and RNA transcription and processing occur (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Features",
    "text" : "The nerve cell body contains a large, spherical, euchromatic nucleus with at least one prominent nucleolus. The nuclear membrane is a double membranous envelope that surrounds the nucleus. However, the nuclear membrane is not continuous as it fuses at locations all around the nucleus to form nuclear pores. Between the two nuclear membranes is a perinuclear cisternal space."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The nucleus is located centrally within the cell body. In cases of cell injury, the nucleus becomes displaced to the periphery (Splittgerber, 2018)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The nucleus is the control center of the cell and houses the genome (genetic information) in eukaryotic cells. It enables DNA replication and synthesizes ribosomal (rRNA), messenger (mRNA), and transfer (tRNA) ribonucleic acid."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nSplittgerber, R. (2018) Snell's Clinical Neuroanatomy. Wolters Kluwer Health."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Vascular endothelium is the layer of epithelial cells that lines the lumina of blood vessels; it originates from the mesoderm (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium is a key component of the tunica interna of a blood vessel. It forms the innermost layer surrounding the lumen, which separates the various cells and molecules within the blood from the surrounding tissue. The endothelium is therefore in direct contact with the bloodstream.\r\n\r\nThe endothelium is composed of a single layer of simple squamous endothelial cells. These cells form a continuous and uninterrupted sheet, or lining, around the lumen of the artery. \r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The endothelium lies on the basement membrane of the blood vessel. This membrane affixes the endothelium to the connective tissue as well as providing the vessel wall with strength. The basement membrane is attached to the subendothelial connective tissue layer of the tunica interna.  "
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The vascular endothelium presides over the movement of cells in and out the blood as well as the exchange of molecules between the blood and surrounding tissues. Endothelium also influences blood flow by acting as a mechanoreceptor when shear stress is applied to the vessel and transmitting the signal to intracellular organelles resulting in an alteration in vascular tone (Akira and Joji, 1993)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "•\tAtherosclerosis \r\n•\tThrombosis\r\n"
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Akira, K. and Joji, A. (1993) 'Vascular Endothelial Cell Functions and Biomechanics', Endothelium, 1(2), pp. 127-130.\r\nDorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\r\n"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cell is the smallest living unit capable of independent function, consisting of cytoplasm containing various subcellular compartments (organelles and cytosol) and separated from the external environment by the plasma membrane. Eukaryotic cells also include a nucleus containing the genome and nucleolus; prokaryotic cells lack a nucleus and DNA is present as a naked chromosome. Cells may exist as independent organisms or, with specialization for specific functions, be subunits of more complex organisms (Dorland, 2011)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure and/or Key Feature(s)",
    "text" : "The cell is the basic structural, functional, and reproductive unit of all multicellular organisms. All mature human cells have a surrounding barrier called the plasmalemma (cell or plasma membrane); a nucleus, which regulates the functions of the cell; and cytoplasm, which contains many components called organelles that perform specific cell functions embedded in an aqueous gel-like matrix called the cytosol. “Cytology” is the study of cells (Ross and Pawlina, 2006; Ovalle, Nahirney and Netter, 2013)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "As it is the basic unit of life, the typical eukaryotic cell becomes specialized in various tissues and organs to perform body functions that sustain life."
  },
  {
    "name" : "List of Clinical Correlates",
    "text" : "Most cells of the body divide to replace damaged, aged, or dead cells. Some cells, such as blood cells and cells in epithelia, are replaced often. Other cells, such as nerve cells (neurons) or cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes), do not divide at all in the adult (McKinley, O'Loughlin and Pennefather-O'Brien, 2016).\n\nThe cell cycle has two phases:\n\n- interphase, in which most cells spend most of their functional life:\n\n- mitotic phase, when the cell divides into two cells. \n\nWhen some cell populations cannot properly control their cell cycle, they divide faster than they are dying and the resultant abnormal growth is called a neoplasm or tumor. A “benign” tumor is confined within a connective tissue boundary or capsule and is usually slow growing. A malignant tumor is not restricted by a capsule and so can easily spread to other tissues and organs. This is called metastasis. Cancer is the collective term used to describe malignant neoplasms (or tumors)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "References",
    "text" : "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nMcKinley, M. P., O'Loughlin, V. D. and Pennefather-O'Brien, E. E. (2016) Human Anatomy. 5th edn.: McGraw-Hill Education.\n\nOvalle, W. K., Nahirney, P. C. and Netter, F. H. (2013) Netter's Essential Histology. ClinicalKey 2012: Elsevier Saunders.\n\nRoss, M. H. and Pawlina, W. (2006) Histology: A text and atlas. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The hypophyseal cleft forms from a diverticulum from the ectodermal roof of the stomodeum of the embryo that lies adjacent to the floor of the diencephalon; from it the adenohypophysis is developed. Its lumen persists in adults as small colloid-filled cysts and clefts at the juncture of the pars distalis and the neurohypophysis (Dorland, 2011)."
  }
]

[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Origin: interlobar artery;

Branches: interlobular artery and arteriolae rectae;

Distribution: parenchyma of kidney (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Structure and/or Key Features", "text": "At the corticomedullary junction, interlobar arteries divide into arcuate arteries which diverge at right angles. Arcuate arteries are named so due to the fact they arch as they travel between the medulla and the cortex. The branches formed from the arcuate arteries are known as interlobular arteries. The ends of arcuate arteries do not anastomose, instead they terminate in the cortex as additional interlobular arteries (Standring, 2016)."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "Arcuate arteries facilitate the circulation of blood through the kidneys."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

