[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "The blood testis barrier is a barrier separating the blood from the convoluted seminiferous tubules, consisting of special junctional complexes between adjacent Sertoli cells near the base of the seminiferous epithelium (Dorland, 2011)."}, {"name": "Description", "text": "The blood testis barrier is the structural and physiological compartment formed by tight junctions between cytoplasmic processes of the sustentocytes (Sertoli cells). These junctions are located on the adluminal side of the basally positioned spermatogonia and primary spermatocytes.

The blood testis barrier first assembles during the induction of spermatogenesis in response to gonadotropic stimulation and the appearance of primary spermatocytes. Sertoli cells are absent until about eight years of age and begin to assemble in the early phase of puberty, thus establishing the basal and adluminal compartments of the seminiferous tubule. This compartmentalization separates immature and mature germ cells into distinct anatomical and functional groups.

The formation of tight junctions coincides with the appearance of spermatocytes. As developing spermatocytes mature, the tight junctions open allowing them to migrate to the adluminal compartment, after which the tight junction reforms. Disruption of tight junctions is controlled by transforming growth factor-β 2 [TGFβ2] produced by transiting spermatocytes and Sertoli cells."}, {"name": "Function", "text": "As mentioned above, the blood testis barrier separates the germ cells into two distinct anatomical and functional compartments. This separation is necessary for:

—protecting meiotic and post-meiotic germ cells from immunological risks from peripheral tissues;

—maintaining a basal stem cell environment;

—preventing intercellular fluid and macromolecular flow into the seminiferous epithelium;

—controlling exposure of basal germ cells to regulatory factors produced by Leydig and myoid cells."}, {"name": "List of Clinical Correlates", "text": "The blood testis barrier may be interrupted by trauma or infection. Immune responses may develop against sperm antigens, potentially leading to subfertility."}, {"name": "References", "text": "Dorland, W. (2011) Dorland's Illustrated Medical Dictionary. 32nd edn. Philadelphia, USA: Elsevier Saunders.

Standring, S. (2020) Gray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice. 42nd edn.: Elsevier Health Sciences."}]

