[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The body (shaft) of eighth rib is the long, curved part of the bone that extends from its neck to its costal end. It is flattened in appearance, where it has external and internal surfaces and superior and inferior borders.\n\nIt consists of the costal groove and the angle, and provides attachment sites for the intercostal muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spinous process is the single, posteriorly projecting process located at the posterior part of the vertebral arch of a thoracic vertebra. It extends from the junction between the two laminae, which itself is found at the midline.\n\nOverall, the spinous processes of thoracic vertebrae are generally long, thin, and tapered. They are obliquely oriented in the posteroinferior direction, resulting in adjacent spinous processes overlapping each other. They provide attachment sites for both intrinsic and extrinsic back muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior border of radius is the margin located along the anterolateral aspect of the body of the radius. It extends inferiorly from the anterior edge of the radial tuberosity to the radial styloid process.\n\nThe anterior border of radius separates the anterior and lateral surfaces of the radius and provides an origin site for the flexor digitorum superficialis muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The costal end (anterior end or sternal end) of fourth rib is located at the anterior end of the shaft. It has a small, rough, concave facet, which attaches to the fourth costal cartilage, forming the fourth costochondral joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
