[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Inferior to the esophageal hiatus of the respiratory diaphragm, the thoracic part of the esophagus continues as the abdominal part. The abdominal part of esophagus is short (1–2.5 cm in length) and becomes slightly wider as it reaches the cardia of the stomach. \n\nThe abdominal part of the esophagus is tethered to the margins of the esophageal hiatus (or aperture) by the phrenicoesophageal ligament, which helps anchor the esophagus to the respiratory diaphragm to limit the mobility of the esophagus within the hiatus. The ligament is composed of elasin-rich connective tissue with interspersed smooth muscle. Below the respiratory diaphragm, the ligament is an extension of the subperitoneal transversalis fascia. It is thin and only loosely connected to the esophagus. \n\nAs the abdominal part of the esophagus exits through the esophageal hiatus, it runs obliquely to the left to reach the cardiac orifice of the stomach, thus forming the gastroesophageal junction. The left lobe of the liver lies anteriorly, and the anterior and posterior vagal trunks lie on its anterior and posterior surface, respectively."
  }
]

