[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The cingulum is the smooth convexity found along the lingual surface of the mandibular lateral incisor tooth. It is located:

- within the cervical third of the lingual surface of the crown;

- superior to the cervical line;

- inferior to the lingual fossa.

The cingulum is continuous both mesially and distally with the marginal ridges."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The masseteric border (masseteric margin, inferior border, or inferior margin) is the thick, rough, posteroinferiorly located margin of the zygomatic bone. It extends posteriorly from the maxillary process to the temporal process of the zygomatic bone and is continuous posteriorly with the inferior margin of the zygomatic process of temporal bone.\n\nThe masseteric border of zygomatic bone provides an origin site for the masseter muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The body is the horizontal, quadrilateral, centrally located part of the hyoid bone. It is continuous:

—posterolaterally, with both the right and left greater horns of hyoid bone;

—superolaterally, with both the right and left lesser horns of hyoid bone.

The body of hyoid bone consists of anterior and posterior surfaces, and superior and inferior borders."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The buccal surface is the aspect of the mandibular first premolar tooth that faces towards the cheek and vestibule. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the lingual, mesial, distal, and occlusal surfaces. The buccal surface consists of three buccal lobes.

The buccal surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiobuccal angle;

- distal surface at the distobuccal angle;

- occlusal surface at the buccoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the buccal surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- occlusal third;

- middle third;

- cervical third.

Furthermore, the buccal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces."
  }
]

