[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior nasal spine (acanthion) is the pointed, anterosuperiorly projecting process found along the midline on the anterior aspect of the cranium. It is formed by both the right and left maxillae and is located at the superior end of the intermaxillary suture.\n\nThe anterior nasal spine provides an attachment site for the cartilaginous part of the nasal septum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The foramina transversaria are the small, oval, laterally located openings found on the transverse processes of the sixth second cervical vertebra.\n\nOverall, all seven cervical vertebrae have a pair of foramina transversaria, although they may be absent in the seventh cervical vertebra of some individuals. The foramina transversaria of the upper six cervical vertebrae allow for the passage of the vertebral arteries and veins, while the foramina transversaria of the seventh cervical vertebra only allows the passage of the accessory vertebral veins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spinous process is the single, posteriorly projecting process located at the posterior part of the vertebral arch of a thoracic vertebra. It extends from the junction between the two laminae, which itself is found at the midline.\n\nOverall, the spinous processes of thoracic vertebrae are generally long, thin, and tapered. They are obliquely oriented in the posteroinferior direction, resulting in adjacent spinous processes overlapping each other. They provide attachment sites for both intrinsic and extrinsic back muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments."
  }
]

