]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The medial surface of tibia is the smooth, slightly convex, subcutaneous area found along the anteromedial aspect of the bone. It is one of the three surfaces of tibia, the other two being the lateral and posterior surfaces, and is an area that is bounded:

—anteriorly, by the anterior border of tibia;

—posteriorly, by the medial border of tibia.

The medial surface of tibia provides insertion sites for the gracilis, sartorius and semitendinosus muscles, as well an attachment site for the tibial collateral ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The marginal ridges are the curved elevations found along the lingual surface of the maxillary central incisor tooth. They consist of:

- A mesial marginal ridge, which extends mesially from the cingulum and then curves to travel inferiorly towards the incisal margin.

- A distal marginal ridge, which extends distally from the cingulum and then curves to travel inferiorly towards the incisal margin.

The marginal ridges form the mesial and distal boundaries of the lingual fossa of the maxillary central incisor tooth."
  }
]

[{"name": "Description", "text": "The Sternoclavicular joint is a synovial sellar (or saddle) joint and is the only articulation between the axial skeleton and the upper limb. The joint is located between the clavicle, the clavicular notches of the sternum, and the superior surface of the first costal cartilage. The articulating surfaces of the clavicle and sternum are covered with fibrocartilage. The joint is reinforced by a number of ligament:

—anterior sternoclavicular ligament;

—posterior sternoclavicular ligament;

—interclavicular ligament;

—costoclavicular ligament. 

The synovial joint is completely separated by a fibrocartilaginous articular disc."}]

