]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior intervertebral surface is the flat, rough area on the lower aspect of the body of a thoracic vertebra. It is surrounded by a smooth outer rim, known as the inferior annular epiphysis.\n\nOverall, the intervertebral surfaces of vertebrae provide attachment sites for the intervertebral discs. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior annular epiphysis (inferior epiphyseal ring) is the raised, smooth, outer edge on the lower aspect of the body of a thoracic vertebra.\n\nOverall, the superior and inferior annular epiphyses provide attachment sites for the intervertebral discs. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sphenoidal crest (crest of sphenoid bone) is the vertical ridge found along the midline on the anterior surface of the body of sphenoid bone. The sphenoidal crest:

—is continuous superiorly with the ethmoidal spine and continuous inferiorly with the sphenoidal rostrum;

—is located medial to both the right and left sphenoidal conchae;

—contributes to the formation of the nasal septum;

—articulates with the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, contributing to the formation of the sphenoethmoidal suture."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sphenoparietal suture is a fibrous joint found along the lateral aspect of the cranium. It is the articulation site located between the parietal margin of the greater wing of sphenoid bone and the sphenoidal angle of the parietal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The base of third metacarpal bone is the expanded, proximal end of the bone. It is the proximal continuation of its body and contains the:

—styloid process of third metacarpal bone;

—proximal articular facet of third metacarpal bone;

—second metacarpal articular facet of third metacarpal bone;

—fourth metacarpal articular facet of third metacarpal bone.

The base of third metacarpal bone provides an origin site for the oblique head of adductor pollicis muscle, and insertion sites for the flexor carpi radialis and extensor carpi radialis brevis muscles. It also provides attachment sites for the:

—palmar metacarpal ligaments;

—dorsal metacarpal ligaments;

—palmar carpometacarpal ligaments;

—dorsal carpometacarpal ligaments."
  }
]

[
