[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lateral surface is the rough, convex area on the lateral aspect of the cuboid bone. It is one of the six surfaces of the cuboid bone, the other five being the plantar, dorsal and medial surfaces, and the proximal and distal articular surfaces.\n\nThe lateral surface of cuboid bone contains part of the groove for tendon of fibularis longus muscle and provides an attachment site for dorsal tarsometatarsal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sacral hiatus is the inverted U-shaped gap found between the sacral cornua on the dorsal surface of the sacrum. It is present due to the failure of fusion between the laminae of the fifth (and sometimes fourth) sacral vertebra at the midline.\n\nThe sacral hiatus forms the caudal opening of the sacral canal and transmits the fifth sacral and coccygeal nerves."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior border (anterior margin) is the long, thin, oblique margin of the cuneiform part of vomer. It is one of the four borders of the vomer, the other three being the posterior, superior and inferior borders.

The anterior border of vomer:

—consists of the vomerine groove;

—provides an attachment site for the septal nasal cartilage;

—articulates superiorly with the perpendicular plate of vomer;

—articulates inferiorly with the incisive crest of maxilla."
  }
]

