    "text" : "Spinous process of twelfth thoracic vertebra"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior surface of clavicle is the rough area on the lower aspect of the bone. It is one of the two surfaces of the clavicle, the other being the superior surface. It is an area that is bounded:

—anteriorly, by the anterior border of clavicle;

—posteriorly, by the posterior border of clavicle.

The inferior surface of clavicle contains the conoid tubercle, trapezoid line, impression for the costoclavicular ligament, subclavian groove, and a nutrient foramen."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The mesiolingual cusp is the superiorly located, pointed elevation found along the mesiolingual aspect of the crown of mandibular first molar tooth. It is one of the five cusps of this tooth, the other four being the mesiobuccal, distobuccal, distolingual, and distal cusps. The mesiolingual cusp consists of both cusp and triangular ridges."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The condylar fossae (condyloid fossae) are the two depressions found along the inferior aspects of the lateral parts of the occipital bone. They are located posterior to the occipital condyles and lateral to the foramen magnum and accommodate the posterior margins of the superior articular facets of the atlas (first cervical vertebra) during head extension.\n\nIn some individuals, the floor of each condylar fossa contains a condylar canal."
  }
]

