    "text" : "The external acoustic meatus (external auditory meatus or external auditory canal) is a tubular passage found in each temporal bone. It is formed by both the squamous and tympanic parts of the temporal bone and is located anterior to the mastoid process.\n\nThe external acoustic meatus extends medially from its external acoustic opening along the lateral aspect of the temporal bone to the tympanic membrane. It allows for the passage of sound waves from the external ear to the tympanic membrane."
