    "text" : "The foramina transversaria are the small, oval, laterally located openings found on the transverse processes of the axis (second cervical vertebra).\n\nOverall, all seven cervical vertebrae have a pair of foramina transversaria, although they may be absent in the seventh cervical vertebra of some individuals. The foramina transversaria of the upper six cervical vertebrae allow for the passage of the vertebral arteries and veins, while the foramina transversaria of the seventh cervical vertebra only allows the passage of the accessory vertebral veins."
