[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The mandibular symphysis (mental symphysis or symphysis menti) is the vertical, sometimes indistinct, line found along the midline on the body of the mandible. It marks the site of fusion between the right and left halves of the mandible that unite during its development. \n\nOn the external surface of the body of the mandible, the lower portion of the mandibular symphysis is continuous inferiorly with the mental protuberance. On the internal surface of the body of the mandible, the superior and inferior mental spines are found along the lower portion of the mandibular symphysis."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The medial lip of linea aspera is the edge of the linea aspera that lies closer to the midline of the body. It is one of two lips of the linea aspera, the other being the lateral lip, both of which surround the intermediate zone of linea aspera. The medial lip is continuous:

—superiorly, with the spiral line;

—inferiorly, with the medial supracondylar line.

The medial lip of linea aspera provides an origin site for the vastus medialis muscle and an attachment site for the medial femoral intermuscular septum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior mental spine (inferior genial spine or inferior genial tubercle) is a small, midline process found along the internal surface of the body of the mandible. It is the lower of the two mental spines that are found along the lower portion of the mandibular symphysis, the other being the superior mental spine. \n\nThe inferior mental spine provides an origin site for both the right and left geniohyoid muscles. In some individuals, the inferior mental spine may be split and present as a pair of inferior mental spines."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The vertebral foramen is the small, circular, centrally located opening of the tenth thoracic vertebra. Its margins are formed:

—anteromedially, by the vertebral body;

—anterolaterally, by both the right and left pedicles;

—posterolaterally, by both the right and left laminae;

—posteromedially, by the spinous process.

The vertebral foramen of the tenth thoracic vertebra contributes to the formation of the vertebral canal, which extends throughout the length of the vertebral column. The vertebral canal allows for the passage of the spinal cord, along with its accompanying meninges and vasculature.

Overall, the size and shape of the vertebral foramina in the different regions of the vertebral column are as follows:

—cervical vertebral foramina are the largest and triangular;

—thoracic vertebral foramina are the smallest and circular;

—lumbar vertebral foramina are intermediate in size and triangular."
  }
]

