[{"name": "Quick Facts", "text": "Origin: Spinal nerves from the first thoracic to the second lumbar nerves.

Course: Anteriorly to the sympathetic trunk.

Branches: No branches.

Supply: Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the entire body."}, {"name": "Origin", "text": "The white communicating branches of the spinal nerves, or white ramus communicans, are short myelin rich axonal fasciculations that branch off from each spinal nerve, from the first thoracic nerve down to and including the second lumbar nerve."}, {"name": "Course", "text": "The white communicating branches run a short distance from a spinal nerve anteriorly to the sympathetic trunk."}, {"name": "Branches", "text": "There are no named branches."}, {"name": "Supplied Structures & Function", "text": "Each white communicating branch carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers that will do one of four things:

—synapse at a sympathetic chain ganglion at the same level; 

—ascend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a higher vertebral level;

—descend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a lower vertebral level;

—exit the sympathetic trunk without synapsing (splanchnic nerve). 

