[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The alveolar process (alveolar part) is the thick, curved, inferior projection found along the inferior aspect of the maxilla. The alveolar process of maxilla:

—is continuous superiorly with the body of maxilla;

—is continuous both posteriorly and medially with the palatine process of maxilla;

—consists of the dental alveoli, interalveolar septa and interradicular septa of the maxilla;

—provides an origin site for the buccinator muscle;

—articulates with the alveolar process of the opposite maxilla, contributing to the formation of the intermaxillary suture.

The alveolar processes of both the right and left maxillae combine to form the maxillary alveolar arch, which lodges all of the maxillary (upper) teeth."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sphenoidal border (sphenoidal margin or anterior border) is the curved, beveled, anteriorly located margin of the squamous part of temporal bone. It is one of the two borders of the squamous part of temporal bone, the other being the parietal border. 

The sphenoidal border:

—extends superolaterally from the anterior border of the petrous part to the parietal border of the squamous part of temporal bone;

—articulates with the squamosal margin of the greater wing of sphenoid bone, forming the sphenosquamous suture."
  }
]

