[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The uncinate processes are the two curved, hook-like projections found along the inferolateral aspects of each of the ethmoidal labyrinths. On its corresponding side, each uncinate process articulates with the ethmoidal process of an inferior nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior vertebral notches are the indentations found on the upper aspects of the pedicles of a thoracic vertebra.\n\nThe inferior vertebral notches of thoracic vertebrae are generally deeper than the superior notches. This is due to the pedicles extending from the thoracic vertebral bodies at points that are above mid-height level.\n\nOverall, the superior and inferior vertebral notches of adjacent vertebrae contribute to the formation of the margins of the intervertebral foramina, which are found along the vertebral column. The vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs also contribute to these margins. Spinal nerves, and their accompanying vessels exit the vertebral canal by passing through these intervertebral foramina."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The alae are the two wing-like, lateral projections that form the posterosuperior part of the vomer. They are continuous anteroinferiorly with the cuneiform part of vomer and articulate with the inferior surface of the body of sphenoid bone, forming the sphenovomerine suture."
  }
]

[
