[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The zygomaticomaxillary suture (maxillozygomatic suture or infraorbital suture) is a fibrous joint found along the anterior aspect of the cranium. It is the articulation site located between the maxillary border of the zygomatic bone and the zygomatic process of the maxilla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The transverse processes are the two laterally projecting processes located at the lateral parts of the vertebral arch of a typical cervical vertebra. Their anterior roots extend from the cervical vertebral bodies, while their posterior roots extend from the junctions between the pedicles and laminae.\r\rOverall, the transverse processes of typical cervical vertebrae are generally small, and they each consist of a foramen transversarium, a groove for spinal nerve, and anterior and posterior tubercles. They provide attachment sites for muscles of the neck and upper back."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The ulnar notch is the smooth, concave, articular indentation found along the medial aspect of the distal part of radius. Its inferior margin provides an attachment site for the articular disc of the distal radioulnar joint.\n\nThe ulnar notch articulates with the articular circumference of the head of the ulna, forming the distal radioulnar joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The subarcuate fossa is a depression found along the posterior surface of the petrous part of temporal bone. It is located superolateral to the internal acoustic meatus and lodges a portion of dura mater."
