    "text" : "The superior annular epiphysis (superior epiphyseal ring) is the raised, smooth, outer edge on the upper aspect of the body of a thoracic vertebra.\n\nOverall, the superior and inferior annular epiphyses provide attachment sites for the intervertebral discs. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sphenosquamous suture (sphenosquamosal suture) is a fibrous joint found along the lateral aspect of the cranium. It is the articulation site located between the squamosal margin of the greater wing of sphenoid bone and the sphenoidal border of the squamous part of temporal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The body (shaft) of fourth rib is the long, curved part of the bone that extends from its neck to its costal end. It is flattened in appearance, where it has external and internal surfaces and superior and inferior borders.\n\nThe body of fourth rib consists of the costal groove and the angle, and provides attachment sites for the intercostal muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The perpendicular plate (perpendicular part) is the vertical, flat, inferiorly projecting bony plate found along the midline on the ethmoid bone. It is continuous superomedially with the crista galli and continuous superolaterally with the cribriform plate. The perpendicular plate: 

—consists of anterior, posterior and inferior borders and right and left lateral surfaces;

—contributes to the formation of the nasal septum;

—provides an attachment site for the septal nasal cartilage;

—articulates with the vomer, frontal, and sphenoid bones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The crown (anatomical crown) is the inferior portion of the maxillary lateral incisor tooth that is covered by enamel. It is separated from the root by the cervical line and consists of four surfaces (labial, lingual, mesial, and distal) and an incisal margin.\n\nThe clinical crown is a term used to describe the inferior portion of a maxillary tooth that is not covered by gingiva when viewed in the oral cavity."
  }
]

