[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The inferior articular facet is the lower, larger, smooth area located on the medial aspect of the head of the fourth rib. It is one of two articular facets on the head of the fourth rib, the other being the superior articular facet, and these two facets are separated from each other by the crest of the head of the rib.\n\nThis inferior articular facet articulates with the superior costal facet of the fourth thoracic vertebra, forming the lower part of the joint of the head of the fourth rib. The upper part of this joint is formed by the articulation between the superior articular facet on the head of the fourth rib and the inferior costal facet of the third thoracic vertebra.\n\nOverall, the first, tenth, eleventh, and twelfth ribs have a single articular facet on their heads, while the remaining ribs have two articular facets, known as the superior and inferior articular facets on heads of ribs."
