[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The laminae are the two broad, flat, bony plates that project posteromedially from the posterior ends of the pedicles of a lumbar vertebra. They form the posterolateral parts of the vertebral arch, the posterolateral walls of the vertebral foramen, and their posterior ends unite at the midline.\n\nThe transverse and superior articular processes extend from the junctions between the pedicles and laminae, the spinous process extends from the junction between the two laminae, and the inferior articular processes extend inferiorly from the laminae.\n\nOverall, the laminae of the vertebral column provide attachment sites for intrinsic back muscles and the ligamenta flava."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sublingual fossae are the two shallow depressions found along the internal surface of the body of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each sublingual fossa is located superior to the anterior portion of a mylohyoid line and lodges a sublingual gland."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The base is the expanded proximal end of the distal phalanx of second toe. It is the proximal continuation of its body and contains an articular facet, which articulates with the distal articular facet of the middle phalanx of second toe, forming the distal interphalangeal joint of the second toe.\n\nThe base of distal phalanx of second toe provides insertion sites for the flexor digitorum longus and extensor digitorum longus muscles. It also provides attachment sites for the collateral and plantar ligaments of the distal interphalangeal joint of the second toe."
  }
]

