  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior intervertebral surface is the flat, rough area on the upper aspect of the body of a thoracic vertebra. It is surrounded by a smooth outer rim, known as the superior annular epiphysis.\n\nOverall, the intervertebral surfaces of vertebrae provide attachment sites for the intervertebral discs. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lingual fossae are the two smooth, shallow, concave areas found along the lingual surface of the mandibular canine tooth.

The mesial lingual fossa is an area that is bounded:

- inferiorly, by the cingulum;

- superiorly, by the incisal margin;

- mesially, by the mesial marginal ridge;

- distally, by the lingual ridge.

The distal lingual fossa is an area that is bounded:

- inferiorly, by the cingulum;

- superiorly, by the incisal margin;

- mesially, by the lingual ridge;

- distally, by the distal marginal ridge."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The pharyngeal tubercle is the small prominence found along the midline on the inferior aspect of the basilar part of occipital bone. It provides an attachment site for the pharyngeal raphe."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS) is the blunt process that marks the posterior end of the iliac crest. It cannot be palpated but a small skin dimple often marks its location.\n\nThe posterior superior iliac spine provides an attachment site for the sacrotuberous and posterior sacroiliac ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The groove (sulcus) for subclavian vein is a shallow depression located on the middle part of the superior surface of the first rib. It lies immediately anterior to the scalene tubercle, while the groove for the subclavian artery is located posterior to this tubercle.\n\nThis groove lodges the subclavian vein."
  }
]

[
