[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The marginal ridges are the curved elevations found along the occlusal surface of the mandibular first molar tooth. They consist of:\n\n- A mesial marginal ridge, which extends lingually from the mesial end of the mesiobuccal cusp to the mesial end of the mesiolingual cusp.\n\n- A distal marginal ridge, which extends lingually from the distal cusp to the distolingual cusp."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior articular processes (superior zygapophyses) are the two upwardly projecting processes located at the lateral parts of the vertebral arch of a lumbar vertebra. They extend from the junction between the pedicles and laminae.\n\nOverall, the superior articular processes of lumbar vertebrae consist of mammillary processes and posteromedially facing superior articular facets. They articulate with the inferior articular processes of the vertebra above, forming zygapophyseal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lesser horns (lesser cornua) are the two small, conical projections found along the superior aspect of the hyoid bone. On its corresponding side, each lesser horn of hyoid bone:

—extends posterosuperiorly from the junction between the body and greater horn of the hyoid bone; 

—provides origin sites for middle pharyngeal constrictor and chondroglossus muscles;

—provides an attachment site for the stylohyoid ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The ethmoidal groove (ethmoidal sulcus) is the longitudinal indentation found along the internal surface of the nasal bone. It extends anteroinferiorly from the superior border to the inferior border of the nasal bone and lodges the anterior ethmoidal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The spinous process is the single, posteriorly projecting process located at the posterior part of the vertebral arch of a lumbar vertebra. It extends from the junction between the two laminae, which itself is found at the midline.\n\nOverall, the spinous processes of lumbar vertebrae are generally short, thick, and blunt. They are more horizontally oriented than other vertebrae and provide attachment sites for intrinsic back muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The distobuccal cusp is the superiorly located, pointed elevation found along the distobuccal aspect of the crown of mandibular third molar tooth. It is one of the four cusps of this tooth, the other three being the mesiobuccal, mesiolingual, and distolingual cusps. The distobuccal cusp consists of both cusp and triangular ridges."
  }
]

[
  {
