[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The costal groove (costal sulcus) is the short, shallow depression found along the body of the eighth rib. It begins lateral to the tubercle and travels along the internal surface of the body, adjacent to the inferior border, and gradually becomes indistinguishable as it travels to the more anterior part of the body of the eighth rib.\n\nThe intercostal vein, artery and nerve travel along the costal groove, with the vein lying most superior and the nerve most inferior. The upper edge of the costal groove provides an attachment site for the innermost intercostal muscle.\n\nOverall, only the second to eleventh ribs have a costal groove, while the first and twelfth ribs lack this bony feature."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The fourth metatarsal articular facet is the quadrilateral, medially located area found on the distal articular surface of the cuboid bone. It is one of the two facets found on the distal articular surface of the cuboid bone, the other being the fifth metatarsal articular facet.\n\nThe fourth metatarsal articular facet articulates with the proximal articular facet of the fourth metatarsal bone, contributing to the formation of the tarsometatarsal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The base is the expanded proximal end of the distal phalanx of little (fifth) toe. It is the proximal continuation of its body and contains an articular facet, which articulates with the distal articular facet of the middle phalanx of little toe, forming the distal interphalangeal joint of the little toe.\n\nThe base of distal phalanx of little toe provides insertion sites for the flexor digitorum longus and extensor digitorum longus muscles. It also provides attachment sites for the collateral and plantar ligaments for the distal interphalangeal joint of the little toe."
  }
]

[
