[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The vertebral foramen is the large, triangular, centrally located opening of the fifth cervical vertebra. Its margins are formed:

—anteromedially, by the vertebral body;

—anterolaterally, by both the right and left pedicles;

—posterolaterally, by both the right and left laminae;

—posteromedially, by the spinous process.

The vertebral foramen of the fifth cervical vertebra contributes to the formation of the vertebral canal, which extends throughout the length of the vertebral column. The vertebral canal allows for the passage of the spinal cord, along with its accompanying meninges and vasculature.

Overall, the size and shape of the vertebral foramina in the different regions of the vertebral column are as follows:

—cervical vertebral foramina are the largest and triangular;

—thoracic vertebral foramina are the smallest and circular;

—lumbar vertebral foramina are intermediate in size and triangular."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The dental alveoli (alveolar cavities or tooth sockets) are the small cavities found along the inferior aspect of the alveolar process of each maxilla. They lodge the roots of teeth and can vary in morphology depending on the type of tooth they each accommodate.\n\nIn adults, there are normally eight dental alveoli on each maxilla, giving a total of sixteen dental alveoli found across the entire maxillary alveolar arch. Adjacent dental alveoli are separated from each other by interalveolar septa and the dental alveoli for some teeth are subdivided by interradicular septa in order to accommodate teeth that have more than one root."
  }
]

[
  {
