[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The obturator foramen is the large, oval opening found in the anteroinferior part of each coxal bone (hip bone). Its margins are formed:

—superiorly, by the inferior border of superior pubic ramus;

—medially, by both the body of pubis and the lateral border of superior pubic ramus;

—inferiorly, by the superior border of ramus of ischium;

—laterally, by the anterior border of body of ischium.

The obturator membrane attaches to the margins of the obturator foramen and, closes closing it in all areas except for its superolaterally where there is area, leaving a small space. This space is combined with the obturator groove of the pubis to form the obturator canal. The obturator canal allows for the passage of obturator nerves and vessels between the pelvic and thigh femoral regions."
