[{"name": "Description", "text": "The brachial plexus may be divided into infraclavicular and supraclavicular parts depending on its relationship with the clavicle.

The infraclavicular part of the brachial plexus includes the lateral, medial, and posterior cords and their terminal branches. 

Infraclavicular branches arising from the lateral cord include: 

—lateral pectoral nerve (C5—C7); 

—musculocutaneous nerve (C5—C7); 

—a lateral root for the median nerve. 

Infraclavicular branches arising from the medial cord include: 

—medial pectoral nerve (C8, T1); 

—medial brachial cutaneous nerve (C8, T1);

—medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve (C8, T1);

—a medial root for the median nerve.

Note that the median nerve has two roots from the lateral (C5, 6, 7) and medial (C8, T1) cords, which embrace the third part of the axillary artery, and unite anterior or lateral to it.

Infraclavicular branches arising from the posterior cord include: 

—upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5, C6);

—thoracodorsal nerve (C6, C8);

—axillary nerve (C5, 6); 

—Radial nerve, which is the largest branch of the brachial plexus (C5—C8, T1)."}, {"name": "List of Clinical Correlates", "text": "—Infraclavicular brachial plexus block"}]

