[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ileum is the final portion of the small intestine that arises as a continuation of the jejunum, and ends in the cecum at the ileocecal valve. 

Like the other components of the small intestine, the ileum transports its contents through the coordinated peristaltic contraction of its embedded smooth muscle layers. The pH value of the ileum is slightly alkaline, and it functions primarily to absorb electrolytes, water, vitamins, fatty acids and bile salts that were not absorbed by the jejunum or duodenum. 

The absorptive properties of the ileum arise from the compact folded nature of the ileal wall. The ileal wall is made up of many villi, whose internal finger-like projections are further convoluted by the presence of epithelial microvilli that serve to further increase the surface area exposed to the tract contents. 

The ileum is also responsible, to a lesser degree, for the digestion of proteins and carbohydrates which is accomplished by the release of proteases and carbohydrases, as well as other hormones such as gastrin, secretin and cholecystokinin. 

The nutrients collected by the blood capillary network surrounding the ileal villi are transported to the liver via the hepatic portal vein for filtration and cleansing before being circulated."
  }
