[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The hepatic portal vein (aka portal vein) is formed by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. It receives blood from the spleen and gastrointestinal tract and distributes it to the liver for filtration before systemic circulation. 

Immediately before entering the liver, the hepatic portal vein divides into the left and right branches.

The hepatic portal vein is not by definition a true vein, as it does not conduct blood straight to the heart. Because the blood returning from the gastrointestinal tract is nutrient-rich from food, it must be filtered and processed before being circulated to remove toxins and metabolize biologically active compounds. 

The left and right hepatic portal veins are major components of the hepatic portal system. While some smaller tributaries of the portal vein contain valves, the larger veins comprising the portal vein have no valves to prevent the back-flow of blood."
