[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The circular layer of muscular layer of stomach (aka circular layer of muscular coat of stomach) is the middle smooth muscular layer of the stomach wall. It is one of the three smooth muscular layers of the stomach wall, the other two being the oblique and longitudinal muscular layers.\n\nThe circular muscular layer is the most complete layer, covering the entirety of the stomach and being continuous rostrally with the circular layer of the esophagus, and thickening caudally to form the the pyloric sphincter. \n\nThe smooth muscle fibers of the circular layer run concentric to the longitudinal axis of the stomach, and functions in controlling the movement of food and chyme along this axis. Collectively, the muscular layers of the stomach are responsible for its wave-like contractions, known as peristalsis, which involuntarily moves contents along the stomach."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "There are three Intermuscular Septae of the lower leg: The Transverse Intermuscular Septum, Anterior Intermuscular Septum and Posterior Intermuscular Septum. They are continuous with the deep and crural fascia of the lower leg, and separate the muscles of the lower leg into functional compartments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The longitudinal layer of muscular layer of duodenum (aka longitudinal layer of muscular coat of duodenum) is the outer smooth muscular layer of the duodenal wall. \n\nCollectively, the circular and longitudinal muscular layers of duodenum are responsible for its wave-like contractions, known as peristalsis, which involuntarily moves contents along the duodenum. The circular layer of muscle contracts and reduces the diameter of the duodenum, aiding the digestion of chyme and absorption of its nutrients, while the longitudinal layer contracts to facilitate the movement of material within the duodenum towards the next part of the small intestine (i.e. the jejunum)."
  }
]

