[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Abdominal fascia consists of the subcutaneous layers, Camper’s fascia (or superficial fatty layer of superficial fascia of abdomen), and the deeper layers, Scarpa’s fascia (or membranous layer of superficial fascia of abdomen). \n\nUnderneath these layers, a deep membranous layer envelops the abdominal muscles. It is divided into three thin layers of investing fascia; the superficial, intermediate, and deep layers which envelop the abdominal muscles and forms the rectus sheath. \n\nBehind the rectus sheath is the transversalis fascia. It is separated from the parietal peritoneum by the extraperitoneal fat layer."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The arcades of sigmoid arteries are the anastomotic loops formed between the distal ends of adjacent sigmoid arteries. \n\nFrom their origins, they travel within the sigmoid mesocolon towards the sigmoid colon, where they give off the straight arteries of colon. Superiorly, the arcades of sigmoid arteries anastomose with the descending branch of left colic artery; inferiorly, they anastomose with the superior rectal artery.\n\nOverall, the arcades of sigmoid arteries and their branches provide an arterial supply to the sigmoid colon and the distal end of the descending colon."
  }
]

[
  {
