    "text" : "Location: Distal end of the colon, from the anorectal junction to the anus.\n\nArterial Supply: Superior and inferior anorectal arteries.\n\nVenous Drainage: Superior and inferior anorectal veins.\n\nInnervation: Parasympathetic: pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4), inferior hypogastric plexus, rectal plexuses, and inferior rectal nerve; Sympathetic: sacral splanchnic nerves, inferior hypogastric and rectal plexuses; pudendal nerve (S2 -4). \n\nLymphatic Drainage: Common and internal iliac lymph nodes."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The anal canal begins at the anorectal junction, at the level of the pelvic floor. It is 2–5cm in length with the posterior wall being slightly longer than the anterior and lies approximately 2–3cm anterior to the distal tip of the coccyx (Standring, 2016). At rest, it does not retain its circular cross-sectional appearance and is oval or slit-like in an anteroposterior direction.\n\nInternally, the middle third of the anus has approximately six to ten vertically orientated mucosal folds, the anal columns. These often contain the terminal branches of the superior rectal vessels. The distal ends of the columns form small semilunar folds that extend from each to its neighbors. These are called anal valves. The valves form small pockets between anal wall and valve called anal sinuses. The combination of distal anal columns and it's the anal valves/sinuses between them form a circle around the inner wall of the anal canal called the pectinate line. \n\nThe pectinate line defines the junction of the upper two thirds and lower third of the anal canal. It is an important landmark that defines the end of the hindgut and is also the site of transition of vascular supply and epithelial appearance. Specifically, at this point columnar epithelium changes to squamous epithelium that is continuous with that of the skin.\n\nThe external wall of the anal canal contains the anal sphincter complex that consists of, from inside to out, the internal anal sphincter, the longitudinal muscle layer, which in the anal canal fans out to form the conjoint longitudinal muscle, and the external anal sphincter. \n\nThe internal anal sphincter is the terminal portion of the inner circular smooth muscle layer of the large intestine. It is characterized by a tight spiral arrangement that shortens and widens when relaxed during defecation. The internal sphincter does not extend beyond the superior two- thirds of the anal canal. \n\nExternal to the circular, the longitudinal, smooth muscle layer of the rectum continues as the conjoining longitudinal muscle of the anal canal.\n\nFinally, superficial to this, the external anal sphincter forms the bulk of the complex. It consists of striated muscle, primarily type 1 slow twitch fibers, and thus is suited to sustained contractions (Schrøder and Reske-Nielsen, 1983). Its upper portion surrounds the internal sphincter."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "Its proximal end is continuous with the rectum. It projects posteriorly due to the puborectalis muscle pulling the rectum anteriorly, thus forming the puborectal angle. At its distal end, the epithelium becomes continuous with the skin of the perineum. \n\nThe middle one third of the anal canal is firmly attached to the perineal body of the perineum. In males, the urethra sits anterior to the anal canal; however, in females, the vagina sits anterior to the anal canal. \n\nPosterolaterally are the ischioanal fossae. They are filled with adipose tissue and permit distension. Posteriorly, the anococcygeal ligament attaches the external anal sphincter to the coccyx in the midline."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The anal canal provides a conduit for feces as it is excreted from the rectum to the anus. It has internal and external sphincters that provide subconscious and conscious control over the process of defecation. Tonic contraction of the internal anal sphincter as well as contraction of the external anal sphincter, caused by the conscious awareness of fecal matter passing into the anal canal, prevent defecation until a socially appropriate time (Koeppen and Stanton, 2009)."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Arterial Supply",
    "text" : "The upper two thirds of the anal canal (above the pectinate line) is vascularized by the termination of the superior anorectal artery, the inferior anorectal artery and a small contribution from the median sacral artery. The lower third of the anal canal is supplied by the inferior rectal arteries, a branch arising from the inferior pudendal arteries."
