    "text" : "A rectal venous plexus surrounds the anal canal (Standring, 2016). The plexus drains into the systemic circulation via the inferior and middle anorectal vein (through the internal pudendal and internal iliac veins, respectively), and the portal venous circulation via the superior anorectal vein (through the inferior mesenteric vein).\n\nDue to its drainage into both the system and portal venous system, the rectal venous plexus acts as an area of portacaval anastomosis."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Innervation",
    "text" : "Parasympathetic innervation comes from pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4) and are distributed via the inferior hypogastric plexus. \n\nSympathetic innervation is derived from sacral splanchnic nerves and distributed via the inferior hypogastric plexus. In addition, the superior rectal plexus supplies innovation to the internal anal sphincter. \n\nA branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-4), the inferior rectal nerve, innervates the external anal sphincter. Below the pectinate line it also provides sensory innervation (Standring, 2016)."
