[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The duodenal ampulla (aka duodenal bulb, duodenal cap) is the superior portion of the duodenum that is also closest to the stomach, from which it is separated by the pyloric sphincter.\n\nThis part of the duodenum is located superior to the head of the pancreas and posterior to the liver. The gallbladder sits between the duodenal ampulla and the posterior surface of the liver.\n\nThe duodenal ampulla is particularly susceptible to ulceration, most of which develop on the anterior portion of the ampulla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The trunk contains most of the vital organs and is, by necessity, heavily vascularized. A series of veins drain the respiratory, urogenital and digestive systems, together with the muscular and skeletal tissues of the region, emptying into the venae cavae or their major tributaries. \n\nThe superior and inferior venae cavae, formed by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins and of the right and left common iliac veins respectively, enter the corresponding regions of the right atrium of the heart, delivering deoxygenated blood for transport to the lungs. \n\nThe major veins of the trunk drain the organs and tissues of the trunk, and receive deoxygenated blood from the head, neck and limbs, carrying it into the right atrium for transport to the lungs."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ascending branch is one of the two terminal branches of the right colic artery, the other being the descending branch.

From its origin, it travels superiorly along the ascending colon to the right colic flexure, where it ends by anastomosing with the right branch of middle colic artery.

Overall, the ascending branch of right colic artery and its branches provide an arterial supply to the distal end of the ascending colon and the right colic flexure.

The ascending branch of right colic artery is just one of the multiple arteries that contribute to the formation of the marginal artery of colon. The marginal artery of colon is an anastomotic channel that is formed by the combination of multiple arteries that are arranged in series along the large intestine. It travels adjacent to the ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, and ends at the rectosigmoid junction. As it travels along the colon, the arteries that make up the marginal artery of colon give off the straight arteries of colon, which provide an arterial supply to the colon."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Structure",
    "text" : "The spinal dura mater is a tough fibrous membrane that protects and encloses the spinal cord from the foramen magnum down to the conus medullaris at roughly L1-L2, the dural sac down to S2, and the coccygeal ligament down to the posterior coccyx. The spinal dura mater is the caudal continuation of the cranial dura mater and runs from the foramen magnum down to the posterior coccyx. It inserts with the filum terminale as the coccygeal ligament. This is the dense fibrous covering that contains the spinal cord and cerebrospinal fluid."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Key Features/Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The spinal dura mater forms the dural sac, running from the foramen magnum rostrally to the S2 vertebrae caudally. At the level of C1 and C2, the dural sac is generally adhered to the surrounding bone. Between the C2 and S2 vertebrae, the spinal dura mater no longer adheres to bone and forms the dural sac. Below the level of S2, the dural sac collapses and together with the filum terminale, runs down to the posterior coccyx as the coccygeal ligament. Dura mater extends laterally with nerve roots to cover nerves as they emerge from the spinal canal. Roughly at the level of nerve trunks, the dura mater blends with the epineurium."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Autonomic innervation to the viscera in this region is both sympathetic and parasympathetic.\nSympathetic innervation is provided by the Splanchnic nerves which provide innervation to the viscera in parts of the abdominal, thoracic and pelvic regions. \nParasympathetic innervation is provided by the vagus nerve.\nThere are many autonomic plexus’ which have both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibres."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Structure/Morphology",
    "text" : "The free tenia is one of three distinct bands of longitudinal smooth muscle that are collectively termed the teniae coli. These strips of longitudinal smooth muscle cells that run along the large intestine and they reside beneath its outer serosal surface."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The three teniae coli are termed the mesocolic tenia, the free tenia, and the omental tenia. The free tenia is located opposite to the mesentery, the omental tenia is located on the posterolateral aspect of the colon and the mesocolic tenia is located on the posteromedial aspect of the colon.\n\nDue to the hepatic flexure at the junction of the transverse colon the teniae coli have a rotated position. Thus, the anterior free tenia becomes inferior, the posteromedial mesocolic tenia becomes posterior, and the posterolaterally placed omental tenia coli becomes superior. \n\nOnce they reach the sigmoid colon, the teniae expand to take up a greater portion of the circumference of the colon where they form distinct bands located anteriorly and posteriorly. Both of these bands eventually converge and unite to form a complete longitudinal muscle layer, a feature characteristic of the rectum."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The longitudinal and circular layers of the muscular layer of the colon are responsible for producing peristaltic contractions of the gut, thus propelling the ingested substance through the lumen of the gut."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ascending branch of lateral circumflex femoral vein travels along the intertrochanteric line of the femur and receives tributaries from the retinacular veins of the head of femur. It drains into the lateral circumflex femoral vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The descending branch is one of the two terminal branches of the right colic artery, the other being the ascending branch.

