Overall, the descending branch of right colic artery and its branches provide an arterial supply to the ascending colon.

The descending branch of right colic artery is just one of the multiple arteries that contribute to the formation of the marginal artery of colon. The marginal artery of colon is an anastomotic channel that is formed by the combination of multiple arteries that are arranged in series along the large intestine. It travels adjacent to the ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, and ends at the rectosigmoid junction. As it travels along the colon, the arteries that make up the marginal artery of colon give off the straight arteries of colon, which provide an arterial supply to the colon."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Structure",
    "text" : "The lumbosacral enlargement is the area of the spinal cord which corresponds to the neural levels contributing to the lower limbs (L1-S3). Because of the large number of neuronal cell bodies associated with lower limb motor and sensory control, the lumbosacral region is visibly thicker in diameter than thoracic spinal cord levels. The spinal cord levels corresponding to the lumbosacral enlargement have a similar structure to that of the rest of the spinal cord. The primary difference is the lumbosacral levels have more neuronal cell bodies, resulting in significantly more gray matter than the thoracic levels. In cross section this appears as a thicker butterfly shape with more prominent dorsal and ventral horns."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Key Features/Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The enlargement is due primarily to the increased thickness of the dorsal and ventral horns of gray matter found at lumbosacral spinal cord levels. These levels correspond to the neural levels serving the lower limbs. The lumbosacral enlargement is found roughly from vertebral levels T9-T12. Posterior to the lumbosacral enlargement, the spinal cord quickly tapers to an end at the conus medullaris."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The arterial supply to the head comes from the distal branches of the two common carotid arteries and the two vertebral arteries."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The omental venous arc compliments the omental arterial arc and drains the inferior border of the omentum into the omental veins, which in turn drain into the left and right gastroomental veins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anus is an aperture at the final portion of the gastrointestinal tract through which the solid wastes of the digestive system, called feces, are removed from the body. \n\nThis aperture is guarded by the involuntary internal and the voluntary external anal sphincter muscles. These muscles are in a tonic state of contraction, storing the contents of the rectum until they are excreted through the anus. Excretion occurs when the two anal sphincter muscles relax."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior mesenteric lymph nodes are a group of lymph nodes situated around the superior mesenteric artery that receive lymph from the large and small intestines. \n\nSpecifically, the superior mesenteric lymph nodes receive lymph efferents from the mesenteric lymph nodes of the ileum and jejunum, and the ileocolic, mesocolic, right colic, and middle colic lymph nodes of the large intestine. The superior mesenteric lymph nodes drain to the pre-aortic lymph nodes and intestinal trunk, and then to the cisterna chyli."
  }
]

[
  {
