    "text" : "The left lobe of liver is the smaller of the two halves of the liver as seen from an anterior view. It is approximately one sixth of the volume of the right lobe of the liver. \n\nThe left lobe of the liver contains the esophageal and colic impressions on the posterior surface, and the cranial portion is rounded to fit snugly under the diaphragm over the stomach, when the stomach is not distended."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "There are normally 31 pairs of spinal nerves: eight cervical, twelve thoracic, five lumbar, five sacral, and one coccygeal. \n\nSpinal nerves are connected to the spinal cord via two roots. The posterior (dorsal) root, contains sensory fibers, in which their cell bodies are located in spinal ganglia. The anterior (ventral) root contains motor fibers and unlike the posterior roots, their cell bodies are located in anterior regions of the spinal cord. \n\nThe anterior and posterior roots divide into multiple, smaller rootlets before entering the spinal cord."
  }
]

[
  {
