[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ascending colon extends upwards from the cecum towards the transverse colon, the transition to which is typically in line with the inferior surface of the right lobe of the liver. This transition point is known as the right colic flexure, or hepatic flexure due to its proximity to the liver. \n\nThe ascending colon is fixed to the posterior abdominal wall by the peritoneum which covers its anterior, medial and lateral surfaces. Like the other components of the large intestine, the ascending colon is responsible for the absorption of water, and to a lesser degree, nutrients and solutes from the fecal material."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The circular folds of jejunum are a continuation of those in the lower parts of the duodenum. They are large and dense to aid in the absorption of nutrients and increase the mucosal surface area in contact with chyme. \n\nAs the folds continue down the jejunum, they become less pronounced and less dense until they become almost non-existent at the level of the ileum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "At the level of filum terminale of the spinal cord, the anterior spinal arteries anastomose with the posterior branches, forming loops which curve around the base of the spinal cord."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The greater omentum is a large fold of visceral peritoneum that hangs down from the greater curvature of the stomach and the free section of the transverse colon, passing in front of the small intestines. 

It is the largest of all the peritoneal folds, and consists of a double layer that repeats on itself, making four layers over the small intestines, extending as far as the pelvis. Upon reaching back up to the large intestine, it becomes continuous with the transverse mesocolon. 

The greater omentum contains a significant amount of adipose tissue, and it also plays a role in immune function.

The left and right gastroepiploic arteries, which run along the inferior border of the stomach, supply the greater omentum with oxygenated blood via its branches, and an anastomosis of these vessels called the epiploic arterial arc lies along its inferior peritoneal fold."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The central canal is a tubular structure containing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) that runs through the center of the entire spinal cord. It is continuous with the fourth ventricle and is lined with a single layer of ependymal cells involved in the production of CSF."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The omental veins (epiploic veins) are the venae comitantes of the omental arteries. They drain the omentum into the left and right gastroomental veins that lie along the greater curvature of the stomach."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The autonomic plexus adjacent to the suprarenal arteries is called the suprarenal plexus. It distributes sympathetic fibers to the suprarenal gland, in particular its medullary portion. \n\nThese fibers arise from the celiac plexus, the celiac ganglion, the phrenic nerve, and the greater splanchnic nerve (via an intermediate ganglion). It is noteworthy in that its fiber tracts are of large size relative to the size of the suprarenal gland."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pylorus is the narrowed part of the stomach that joins the stomach to the duodenum, to which it is separated by the pyloric sphincter. \n\nIt is composed of the pyloric antrum and the pyloric canal. The pylorus is heavily folded and is itself responsible for the production of digestive enzymes which aid in the breakdown of food that has entered the digestive tract. \n\nFood that has been sufficiently acted upon by the digestive juices is passed into the duodenum through the pyloric sphincter muscle that guards the duodenal entrance."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The gastric folds (aka gastric rugae, or rugae of stomach) are the folds of the stomach that serve to increase its surface area without increasing pressure when food enters, and facilitate absorption of nutrients through the columnar epithelium."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cystic vein is the vena comitans of the cystic artery. It drains the gallbladder and the cystic duct into the hepatic portal vein via its anterior and posterior cystic tributaries."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The prepancreatic artery is one of the two terminal branches of the dorsal pancreatic artery, the other being the inferior pancreatic artery. \n\nFrom its origin, it travels laterally to the right, along the neck and head of pancreas. It ends by anastomosing with one of the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. \n\nThe prepancreatic artery provides an arterial supply to the neck and head of the pancreas."
  }
]

