    "text" : "The pyloric antrum (aka antrum of stomach, or gastric antrum) is one of the two components that make up the pylorus of the stomach, the other one being the pyloric canal. \n\nThe pyloric antrum makes up the inferior portion of the stomach, and can be demarcated superiorly by the notch called the incisura angularis between the lesser curvature of the stomach and the pyloric antrum. \n\nFunctionally, the pyloric antrum is importantly involved in the production of digestive enzymes such as gastrin and somatostatin that aid in the breakdown of food upon entry into this initial part of the digestive tract."
