[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The metatarsophalangeal and proximal and distal interphalangeal joints are hinge joints that are surrounded by joint synovial joint capsules which allows their fluid movement. \n\nThe collateral ligaments of these joints extend from the lateral and medial sides of the metatarsal bones and the phalanges on either side of these joints. They serve to connect and stabilize these joints during articulation.\n\nSupporting the metatarsophalangeal and interphalangeal joints, along with the collateral ligaments, are the accessory collateral ligaments. The accessory collateral ligaments extend from the palmar surface of the metatarsal and phalangeal bones, blending fibres with the collateral ligaments, and attach to the plantar plates, securing them."
