    "text" : "The cecum is the first portion of the large intestine. It is a pouch, usually peritoneal, and is about 6 cm long and 7.5 cm broad. It receives fecal material from the ileum, from which it is separated by the ileocecal valve.\n\nIt is continuous with the ascending colon of the large intestine, from which it is separated by the cecocolic junction. The appendix is also connected to the cecum.\n\nThe cecum serves to collect the chyme from the small intestine, to mix it further with mucous, and to absorb builds and salts from the ileal contents. Mucous is secreted by the internal wall of the cecum, through which fluids and salts are also absorbed. The contents of the cecum are churned by contraction of a layer of deep muscle beneath the mucosal lining of the cecum. They also aid the passage of the contents to the ascending colon."
