  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The mastoid notch, sometimes also referred to as the mastoid incisure, is a deep notch between the styloid and the mastoid processes of the temporal bone. It provides the insertion for the posterior belly of the digastric muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Temporal bone part of temporal fossa"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lateral surface of the palatine bone is located on the posterior part of the perpendicular plate of the bone. It is directed posterolaterally and forms part of the infratemporal fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The occipital lymph nodes are located at the back of the head at the lateral border of the trapezius, and proximal to the semispinalis capitis insertion. These nodes, often around two or three, drain occipital region and upper neck into the superior deep cervical lymph glands, which in turn drain lymph to the jugular lymphatic trunk."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial lymph vessels of the leg lie within the superficial fascia of the leg, and can be divided into two groups - a medial group and a lateral group. \n\nThe medial group of lymph vessels largely accompany the great saphenous vein from the tibial side of the foot to the hip, while the lateral group accompanies the small saphenous vein from the fibular side of the foot to the knee where the small saphenous vein anastomoses with the popliteal vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The palatine process of the maxilla is a horizontal projection of bone that extends posteriorly from the body of the maxilla. The palatine process of the maxilla makes up the anterior part of the hard plate which separates the oral cavity and the nasal cavity. The horizontal plate of the palatine bone makes up the remainder of the hard plate."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The zygomaticomaxillary suture is a line which marks the point of articulation of the zygomatic process of the maxilla with the zygomatic bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The olfactory tract travels posteriorly before the fibres divide into the lateral stria and medial stria, as well as the intermediate stria. The junction between these striae forms the olfactory trigone, located in front of the anterior perforated substance."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The capitohamate ligament belongs to the palmar intercarpal group of ligaments, which are formed by several short fibrous bands that extend transversely across the palmar surface of the carpal bones. The capitohamate ligament, as the name suggests, attaches from the capitate to the hamate."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The palmar intercarpal ligaments serve to stabilize the carpal bones in the midcarpal joint and to allow limited movement (gliding) between them."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Mylohyoid line of mandible"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The first costal notches are the superiorly located, laterally facing articular areas found along the lateral borders of the manubrium of sternum. They are one of the seven pairs of articular facets that are found along the length of the sternum.\n\nThe first costal notches articulate with the first costal cartilage on its respective side, forming the first sternocostal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial anterior cervical lymph nodes (aka anterior jugular lymph nodes) are located close to the anterior jugular vein, accompanying them superficially along the front of the neck. \n\nThese nodes drain lymph from the superficial surfaces of the front of the neck."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Medial part of orbital margin"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Tuberosity of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The transverse cervical lymph nodes comprise a group of lymph nodes that lie superficially in the neck, and deep to the insertion of the platysma muscle. \n\nThese superficial lymph nodes drain lymph to the supraclavicular lymph nodes (also known as the inferior deep cervical lymph nodes), which themselves drain lymph to the jugulosubclavian junction."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Pelvic surface of sacrum"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The deep branch arises from the medial plantar artery as it travels through the sole of foot.\n\nFrom its origin, it travels laterally, deep to the tendon of the fibularis longus muscle, and ends by anastomosing with the deep plantar arch.\n\nThe deep branch of medial plantar artery provides an arterial supply to the medial side of the sole of foot, including the muscles of the great toe."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The jugular fossa is a rounded depression on the inferior surface of the temporal bone. Part of the internal jugular vein is contained in this fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The bregma marks the point at which the sagittal and coronal sutures meet. In a baby, this area is membranous and is called the anterior fontanelle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The maxillary process of the palatine bone is a forward directed projection from the perpendicular plate of palatine bone. It forms part of the wall of the maxillary sinus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular surface for cuboid (anterior surface of calcaneus) is the saddle-shaped, articular area on the anterior aspect of the anterior process of talus. \n\nIt is one of the six surfaces of the calcaneus, the other five being the posterior, dorsal, plantar, medial, and lateral surfaces.\n\nThe articular surface for cuboid articulates with the proximal articular surface of cuboid, forming the calcaneocuboid joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior trochlear surface of the talus bone articulates with the tibia. The medial malleolar facet of the talus is the surface of the talus that articulates with the medial malleolus of the tibia, and the lateral malleolar facet of the talus is the surface of the talus that articulates with the lateral malleolus of the fibula. \n\nThe medial and lateral malleolar facets, together with the superior trochlear surface of the talus make up the trochlea of the talus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The medial facial muscles are one of the two subgroups of the facial muscles, the other being the lateral facial muscles. This group consists of the single muscles that lie along the midline of the face. They attach to the skin and subcutaneous structures of the face on one end, and the bones and fascia of the face on the other. They are involved in facial expressions. \r\rThe medial facial muscles consist of the:\r\r- Nasal muscles\r- Orbicularis oris"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The maxillary surface of the palatine bone is on the lateral side of the perpendicular plate of the bone. It is directed anterolaterally towards the maxilla and forms part of the wall of the maxillary sinus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular tubercle is an eminence located between the mandibular fossa and the external acoustic meatus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The frontal process of the maxilla is an extension of the bone which articulates with the frontal bone superiorly."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The fundiform ligament of the penis comprises a thickened extension of the lower aspect of Scarpa’s fascia, which itself is adherent to, and blends with fibers of the linea alba, the pubic symphysis, and the external oblique aponeurosis.\n\nThis ligament extends from the pubic bone and wraps around the dorsal and lateral aspects of the penis."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The intermediate nerve (aka Wrisberg nerve) is a branch of the facial nerve. From its origin, it travels between the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves. It provides parasympathetic innervation to the sublingual and submandibular glands, carries taste information from the anterior part of the tongue and palate, and carries sensory input from the mucous membranes of the nasopharynx and nose."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The second costal notches are one of the seven pairs of laterally facing articular areas found along the lateral borders of the sternum. 

The second costal facets are formed by the combination of two demifacets:

- the upper demifacet is located at the inferior end of the lateral border of the manubrium of sternum;

- the lower demifacet is located at the superior end of the lateral border of the body of sternum.

The second costal notches articulate with the second costal cartilage on its respective side, forming the second sternocostal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The petrous apex is the anteromedially facing tip of the petrous part of the temporal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The orbital margin marks the anterior border of the eye socket. The lower part of this margin is called the infraorbital margin and is formed by the maxilla and zygomatic bones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The erector spinae (sacrospinalis) muscles are the collective name for a group of muscles found in the intermediate layer of the intrinsic muscles of the back. Overall, the erector spinae muscles are involved in movements of the head, neck and trunk.

The erector spinae muscle consists of three individual, vertically oriented muscles that lie parallel to the vertebral column. From medial to lateral, these muscles are the:

- spinalis;

- longissimus;

- iliocostalis.

This group of muscles is known as the ‘erector spinae’ because they are responsible for straightening the vertebral column."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lateral to the nasal notch of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The intercalated lymph node is a lymph node belonging to the posterior lateral superficial cervical lymph nodes.\n\nThe posterior lateral superficial cervical lymph nodes comprise a collection of lymph nodes situated superficially in the neck, typically between the sternocleidomastoid muscle and the deeper muscles of the neck, including the splenius capitis, splenius cervicis, levator scapulae and middle scalene muscles.\n\nThese nodes drain lymph from the more superficial portions of the neck to the jugulosubclavian junction where lymph drains into the venous system."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Zygomatic process of the temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the parietal bone is a large curved plate of bone which forms on either side, the walls of the summit of the skull. The body of the bone is bound on all sides by sutures with other bones of the skull."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the maxilla is part of the internal surface of the maxilla which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The inferior nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the inferior nasal concha and the floor of the nasal cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The extrinsic laryngeal muscles are one of the two subgroups of the laryngeal muscles, the other being the intrinsic laryngeal muscles. \r\rThe extrinsic laryngeal muscles are located external to the larynx, and move the entire larynx when they contract. These muscles are the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles, whose contraction moves the larynx superior and inferiorly, respectively."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular capsule is also known as the joint capsule. It is a sac enclosing the articulating ends of bones which participate in a synovial joint. It contains an outer fibrous layer and an inner synovial membrane. The inner layer secretes synovial fluid and is composed of loose connective tissue with a free smooth surface that lines the joint cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior surface of the oral part of the tongue is covered in papillae. These include filiform papillae, fungiform papillae, foliate papillae and vallate papillae. \n\nVallate papillae are the largest of the papillae and are dome-shaped invaginations in the tongue’s surface. There are about eight to twelve vallate papillae, which are organised into a single V-shaped line, and are immediately anterior to the terminal sulcus of the tongue. They are surrounded by a wall, or vallum, and contain special taste organs."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The facial surface or anterior surface of the maxilla is the external surface of the bone which is directed anteriorly and laterally."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial portions of the anterior part of the neck are drained by lymph vessels that drain into the superficial cervical, superior deep cervical, supraclavicular (inferior deep cervical) and jugular lymph nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This surface lies on the lateral side of the triquetral carpal and articulates with the hamate carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pancreaticoduodenal lymph nodes are lymph nodes that accompany the pancreaticoduodenal arteries. They drain the pancreas and duodenum to the cisterna chyli via the celiac group of preaortic lymph nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The occipital groove is a groove on the temporal bone along which the occipital artery travels. It begins medial to the mastoid notch and extends posteriorly."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The circumorbital muscles are a group of facial muscles that surround each orbit. They are involved in facial expressions, close the eyelids, and compress the lacrimal sac.\r\rThe circumorbital muscles consist of the:\r\r- Orbicularis oculi\r- Depressor supercilii\r- Corrugator supercilii"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Mastoid notch of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The styloid process of the temporal bone is a projection from the base of the temporal bone. The stylomandibular ligament and three muscles that aid in moving the hyoid bone attach here."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The mylohyoid branch of the inferior alveolar vein drains the mylohyoid muscle before merging with the inferior alveolar vein and emptying into the maxillary vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The clavicular notches are the oval indentations found along the superior border of the manubrium of sternum. They are superolaterally facing articular areas located on either side of the jugular notch of sternum. Each clavicular notch articulates with the sternal facet of the clavicle on its respective side, forming the sternoclavicular joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The artery of precentral sulcus (aka precentral sulcal artery, or pre-Rolandic artery) arises from the superior terminal branch of the middle cerebral artery. \r\rFrom its origin, it travels superiorly, along the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere, towards the precentral sulcus, which it also travels along.\r\rIt provides an arterial supply to the precentral sulcus and the areas of the cerebral cortex that lie adjacent to it."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior temporal line runs mostly parallel to the inferior temporal line. The temporal fascia attach along this line."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The horizontal plate of the palatine bone is a horizontal projection of bone that extends medially from the lateral plate of the palatine bone. It is continuous anteriorly with the palatine process of the maxilla. The palatine process of the maxilla makes up the anterior part of the hard plate and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone makes up the posterior part of the hard plate. The hard plate separates the oral cavity from the nasal cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The dura mater is the tough outer layer of the meninges, and it is made up of two parts; the cranial dura mater and the spinal dura mater.\n\nThe cranial dura mater (aka dura mater of brain) is the outermost part covering of the brain. It consists of the periosteal outer layer and meningeal inner layer. \n\nThe dural venous sinuses lie between layers of the cranial dura. The meningeal layer forms folds that help to support brain. These include the cerebral falx, cerebellar falx and the cerebellar tentorium."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior cerebellar veins pass anteromedially from their origin on the superior surfaces of the cerebellar hemisphere, draining into the straight sinus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the nasal bone is thin, elongated and slightly curved. Its upper margin articulates with the frontal bone, its lateral margin with the maxilla, its medial margin with the opposing nasal bone and the lateral cartilage of the nose is attached to its inferior border. The medial border is also thickened on its posterior side and extends into a crest which articulates with the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone. The two nasal bones together form the bridge of the nose."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The muscular branches radiate from the ophthalmic artery. They travel within the orbit, along the extra-ocular muscles. Along their paths, these muscular branches give off anterior ciliary arteries.\n\nThe muscular branches of the ophthalmic artery provide an arterial supply to the extra-ocular muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lacrimal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "There are four paranasal air sinuses - the ethmoidal, sphenoidal, maxillary and frontal sinuses. The sinuses are are named according to the bone in which they are found. The nasal sinuses are lined with respiratory mucosa which secretes mucus into the nasal cavity. The paranasal sinuses receive nerve innervation from branches of the trigeminal nerve [V]."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Head of the 5th metatarsal"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lacrimal tubercle is a small eminence at the junction of the lacrimal crest with the margin of the orbit. It marks the location of the lacrimal sac."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the spinal nerve is found on the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae. The transverse processes exhibit a deep sulcus on their upper surfaces for passage of the corresponding cervical spinal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cerebral veins consist of either superficial cerebral veins or internal cerebral veins depending on which area of the hemisphere they drain into."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pterygoid lateral vein is a tributary of the maxillary vein that runs laterally around and within the pterygoid muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior border of the vomer articulates with the nasal crest of the maxilla and palatine bones. The posterior part articulates with the palatine bone and the anterior part with the maxilla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Superior head - infratemporal surface of greater wing of sphenoid"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Inferior head - lateral surface of lateral pterygoid plate"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Infra-orbital margin of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The auricle (also known as the pinna) is part of the external part of the ear. It collects sounds waves and directs them along the external auditory canal towards the eardrum, the tympanic membrane. \n\nThe auricle is made up of components such as the helix, anti-helix, scapha, lamina of tragus and spine of helix."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Petrous part of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The muscles of the deep perineal pouch lie in the urogenital triangle of the perineum, superior to the perineal membrane, and inferior to the levator ani muscles and urogenital hiatus. They support the pelvic floor and assist with urinary continence. \r\rThe muscles of the deep perineal pouch consist of the:\r\r- Deep transverse perineal\r- External urethral sphincter"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "ORIGIN: Oblique line of the thyroid cartilage posteriorly; the cricoid cartilage between the origin of the cricothyroideus muscle and the inferior cornu of the thyroid cartilage.

INSERTION: Pharyngeal raphe.

ACTION: This muscle contracts to push the bolus of food downwards into the esophagus during swallowing.

NERVE SUPPLY: Pharyngeal branch of vagus nerve; pharyngeal plexus of vagus nerve; external branch of superior laryngeal nerve

ARTERIAL SUPPLY: Ascending pharyngeal artery; superior thyroid artery"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior vein of vermis originates on the superior surface of the vermis and runs anteriorly into the internal cerebral vein draining the tentorial region of the cerebellum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The orbital surface of the maxilla forms the lower surface of the eye socket. It is continuous with the orbital surfaces of adjacent bones. It contains a groove anteriorly, the infraorbital groove, through which the infraorbital vessels and nerve pass."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The corpus cavernosum of penis (aka cavernous body of penis) comprises one of a pair of erectile bodies along the dorsal aspect of the penis. \n\nThese two bodies are sponge-like in consistency, and become engorged with blood during erection. The spongy tissues of these bodies contains the blood, maintaining a more dense form during erection. The dorsal arteries of the penis run between these two bodies within the deep fascia of penis, supplying them with blood. The deep fascia of penis serves to put pressure on the deep dorsal vein of the penis, also lying between the cavernosa, thereby preventing blood return from the penis, and maintaining erection.\n\nThe corpora cavernosa attach to the pubic bones proximally, and to the head of the penis distally. Situated beneath the two cavernosa is the corpus spongiosum, within which the urethra runs."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal crest of the palatine bone is a ridge of bone along the superior surface of the palatine process above the median palatine suture, where the two palatine bones meet. The upper border of this ridge articulates with the vomer. The posterior of the nasal crest forms a pointed end which is termed the posterior nasal spine and which gives origin to the musculus uvulae."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The perforator veins of the lower leg are those veins which pass from the superficial veins of the lower leg to the deeper anterior tibial, posterior tibial and fibular veins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The gingivae (aka gums) are the vascular connective tissues and mucous membranes that cover the alveolar processes of the jaws and surround the base of the teeth. \n\nThey can be divided into marginal, attached, and interdental areas. These refer to the terminal edge of the membrane surrounding the teeth, the tissue continuous with this edge that covers the mandible and maxilla, and the tissue forming the gingival embrasure between teeth, respectively. The gingivae are considered part of the buccal mucosa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The head of the talus bone points anteriorly. It is covered in articular cartilage and articulates with the navicular tarsal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The branch of middle cerebral artery to angular gyrus arises from the inferior terminal branch of the middle cerebral artery. It travels posterolaterally, through the lateral sulcus, and then further posteriorly along the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere towards the angular gyrus, which it also travels along.\n\nThe branch of middle cerebral artery to angular gyrus provides an arterial supply to the angular gyrus and adjacent areas along the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the spinal nerve is found on the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae. The transverse processes exhibit a deep sulcus on their upper surfaces for passage of the corresponding cervical spinal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The parietomastoid suture is a line which unites the parietal bone to the mastoid part of the temporal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Temporal process of the zygomatic bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Tubercle of iliotibial tract of tibia"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Anterior aspect of body of pubis"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasalis muscle is the facial muscle that lies superficial to the nasal bones and nasal cartilage. It is involved in facial expressions and regulates the size of the nostrils.\r\rThe nasalis muscle consists of transverse and alar parts."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "NERVE SUPPLY: Perineal Nerve\n\nIn males, the external urethral sphincter (aka external sphincter of male urethra, or striate sphincter) is located just inferior to the prostate gland. This muscle consists of voluntarily controlled skeletal muscle. It is responsible for the conscious control of the expulsion of urine through the urethra. \n\nIn addition to the external urethral sphincter, the lower portion of the bladder is guarded by an internal urethral sphincter, which is located at the junction of the urethra with the bladder. This muscle consists of involuntarily controlled smooth muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular surface of the distal phalanx of the first digit articulates with the proximal phalanx of the same digit. The articular surface of the distal phalanx of the second to fifth digits articulates with the intermediate phalanx of the same digit. These are called distal interphalangeal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the palatine bone is part of the medial surface of the bone which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The superior nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the superior nasal concha and the middle nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The carpal articular surface of radius is the smooth, concave area on the inferior aspect of the distal part of the bone. It is divided, by a ridge, into medial and lateral portions.

- The medial portion articulates with the lunate bone and is known as the lunate articular facet of radius.

- The lateral portion articulates with the scaphoid bone and is known as the scaphoid articular facet of radius.

Collectively, these two articulations form the radiocarpal (wrist) joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior ciliary arteries arise from the muscular branches of the ophthalmic artery. They travel anteriorly within the orbit along the rectus muscles of the eye. Two arteries travel with the superior, inferior and medial rectus muscles each, while only one travels with the lateral rectus muscle.\n\nAfter reaching the point at which the rectus muscles insert into the sclera, the anterior ciliary arteries pierce the sclera and anastomose with the major arterial circle of the iris, which encircles the iris.\n\nThe anterior ciliary arteries provide an arterial supply to the rectus muscles, the sclera, conjunctiva and iris."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Left transverse process of first lumbar vertebra"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior surface is the smooth, convex area on the anterior aspect of the manubrium of sternum. It is one of the two surfaces of the manubrium of sternum, the other being the posterior surface. It is an area that is bounded by the superior, inferior, and two lateral borders of the manubrium of sternum.\n\nThe anterior surface of manubrium of sternum provides an origin site for the pectoralis major muscle and the sternal head of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. It also provides an attachment site for the sternocostal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "A communicating branch often exists between the facial vein and the anterior jugular vein. It runs down the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle to join the lower part of the anterior jugular vein. It also occasionally communicates with the external jugular vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The incisive foramen is located just behind the incisor teeth on the inferior surface of the palatine plate. It allows passage of vessels and nerves into the incisive canal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lacrimal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The palatomaxillary suture is a line which marks the union of the palatine and maxillary bones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Alveolar process of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "Systemic arteries are one of the three types of blood vessels found within the systemic circulation, the other two being systemic veins and systemic capillaries. They are generally described as the blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart to all the tissues of the body, except the lungs. Systemic arteries are all distal branches of the aorta, and carry oxygenated blood from the left ventricle of the heart to the capillaries of all tissues of the body, except the lungs. \n\nSystemic capillaries are located within the tissues of the body; they are the sites where the exchange of nutrients and waste products occurs between the blood and these tissues."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Fascia covering the auricular muscle that is continues with the fascia covering the rest of the head."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Posterior medullary veins are transverse connections between the longitudinal medullary veins at the inferior aspect of the medulla oblongata and drain the lateral group of pontine veins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Xiphoid process"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The deep cervical fascia of the neck lies under the platysma and invests the neck. The deep cervical fascia is divided into layers. An investing layer surrounds all structures in the neck (excluding the platysma). It is attached posteriorly to the ligamentum nuchae and splits anteriorly to surround the sternocleidomastoid muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Apex of coccyx"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The oral mucosa of palate covers the inferior surface of both the hard and soft palates.\n\nOverall, the palate is the structure that separates the oral cavity from the nasal cavity. It consists of two parts; the anteriorly located hard palate, and the posteriorly located soft palate. The hard palate forms the anterior two-thirds of the palate, and is formed by the maxillary and palatine bones. The soft palate forms the posterior one-third of the palate, and is formed by the palatine muscles and associated muscles. \n\nThe superior aspect of the palate is covered by nasal mucosa, while its inferior aspect is covered by oral mucosa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The sternal angle (angle of Louis, Ludwig, or Ludovicus) is the inclined junction between the anterior surfaces of the manubrium and body of sternum. It is found subcutaneously and marks the location of the manubriosternal joint."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the palatine bone is part of the medial surface of the bone which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The inferior nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the inferior nasal concha and the floor of the nasal cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The vocal fold (aka true vocal fold, or true vocal cord) consists of folded mucous membranes stretched horizontally across the larynx. \n\nIt is attached to the arytenoid cartilages posteriorly, and to the thyroid cartilage anteriorly. Its inner margins are free to oscillate to produce the sounds for speech, and its outer margins are attached to laryngeal muscles, such as the vocalis muscle, to modify the sounds. \n\nControl of the muscles during phonation is provided by the vagus nerve. The difference in vocal pitch between adult males and females is due to the larger vocal fold in males."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pancreaticoduodenal lymph nodes are lymph nodes that accompany the pancreaticoduodenal arteries. They drain the pancreas and duodenum to the cisterna chyli via the celiac group of preaortic lymph nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The lunotriquetral ligament belongs to the palmar intercarpal group of ligaments, which are formed by several short fibrous bands that extend transversely across the palmar surface of the carpal bones. The lunotriquetral ligament, as the name suggests, attaches from the lunate to the triquetrum."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Function",
    "text" : "The palmar intercarpal ligaments serve to stabilize the carpal bones in the midcarpal joint and to allow limited movement (gliding) between them."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sacrum and coccyx together constitute the most inferior region the vertebral column. The sacrum is formed by 5 fused sacral vertebrae, with the most superior of these articulating with the most inferior of the lumbar vertebra (L5) via an intervertebral disc. The coccyx, also referred to as the tailbone, is the terminal segment of the spinal column and consists of between 3 and 5 fused coccygeal vertebrae.\n\nThe cauda equina passes through a hollow canal in the sacrum, with the sacral nerves exiting the sacrum posteriorly and anteriorly via the posterior and anterior sacral foramina. \n\nThere are no intervertebral discs between the vertebrae that form the sacrum or the coccyx. A fibrocartilagenous joint does, however, attach the coccyx to the sacrum, permitting very limited movement between the two. The coccyx provides attachment for several important ligaments and muscles of the pelvic floor."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pterygopalatine groove is a narrow depression which runs vertically along the lateral side of the bone just posterior to the lateral pterygoid plate."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The perforator veins of the lower leg are those veins which pass from the superficial veins of the lower leg to the deeper anterior tibial, posterior tibial and fibular veins."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The posterior parietal artery arises from the inferior terminal branch of the middle cerebral artery. It travels posterosuperiorly along the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere towards the posterior part of the parietal lobe, which it also travels along.\n\nIt provides an arterial supply to the posterior part of the parietal lobe."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This articular surface lies on the distal aspect of the lunate carpal for articulation with the capitate carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial portions of the neck are drained by the superficial cervical lymph nodes via efferents that pass inferiorly to the jugular trunk via the transverse cervical chain of lymph nodes and the supraclavicular lymph nodes (also known as inferior deep cervical lymph nodes)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Most of the joints of the skull are sutures. Sutures are immovable fibrous joints where the edges of the bones firmly fit together and are united by connective tissue. This thin connective tissue begins to fully ossify in the late twenties and is normally complete by around the age of 50. \n\nSome cranial fibrous joints are moveable, called syndesmoses, where bones or bone parts are joined together by connective tissue. Cranial syndesmoses include the stylohyoid ligament and pterygospinous ligament. The final type of fibrous joint found in the skull are gomphosis which are joints between the roots of the teeth and the sockets of the maxilla or mandible. \n\nThere are three joints in the skull which are synovial joints. These include the temporomandibular joint, and the two small joints between the three bones in the middle ear (the malleus, incus and stapes)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "There are three joints in the skull which are synovial joints. These include the temporomandibular joint, and the two small joints between the three bones in the middle ear (the malleus, incus and stapes)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The palmar surface is the rough, non-articular, anterior aspect of the hamate bone. 

It contains the hook of hamate bone, and provides origin sites for the flexor digiti minimi brevis and opponens digiti minimi muscles. It also provides attachment sites for the:

- Flexor retinaculum of hand;

- Pisohamate ligament;

- Palmar carpometacarpal ligaments;

- Capitohamate ligament;

- Triquetrohamate ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The terminal branch of the recurrent laryngeal nerve is known as the inferior laryngeal nerve. This nerve arises from the recurrent laryngeal nerve, generally as two branches. The anterior branch supplies the intrinsic muscles of the larynx, while the posterior branch forms Galen’s anastomosis by communicating with the superior laryngeal nerve via its passage through the thyrohyoid membrane."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial branch arises from the medial plantar artery as it travels through the sole of foot.

From its origin, close to the base of the 1st metatarsal, it travels medially in an arch like manner along the plantar surfaces of the 2nd and 3rd metatarsals. 

Along its course, it gives off three branches, which anastomose with the 1st to 3rd plantar metatarsal arteries. 

The superficial branch of medial plantar artery provides an arterial supply to the medial side of the sole of foot, and the toes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The superior border is the thick, irregular upper margin of the manubrium of sternum. It is one of the four borders of the manubrium of sternum, the other three being the inferior border and the two lateral borders.\n\nThe superior border of manubrium of sternum contains a medially located jugular notch, which is located between two laterally located clavicular notches. The superior border of manubrium of sternum provides an attachment site for the interclavicular ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal notch of the maxilla marks the anterior margin of the opening to the nasal cavity. It runs along the anteromedial margin of the maxillae."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lymph from the head and face is gathered in the lymph nodes located around the face, tongue, parotid glands and temporal and occipital regions of the scalp.\n\nFrom these nodes, lymph flows inferiorly to the group of deep cervical collecting lymph nodes of the neck, which in turn drain to the left and right jugular lymph trunks, proximal to the junctions of the internal jugular vein with the subclavian vein on each side of the body. \n\nLymph from the right jugular trunk drains into the right lymphatic trunk, which opens into the venous system at the junction of the right internal jugular vein with the right subclavian vein. Lymph from the left jugular trunk drains into the thoracic duct, which opens into the venous system at the junction of the left internal jugular vein with the left subclavian vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The posterior lateral superficial cervical lymph nodes comprise a collection of lymph nodes situated superficially in the neck, typically between the sternocleidomastoid muscle and the deeper muscles of the neck, including the splenius capitis, splenius cervicis, levator scapulae and middle scalene muscles.\n\nThese nodes drain lymph from the more superficial portions of the neck to the jugulosubclavian junction where lymph drains into the venous system."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lateral medullary venous plexus is a network of veins reaching the superficial veins of the lateral medullary fossa, which are relatively straight and may be longitudinal and transverse. The lateral veins of the medulla collect blood from the posterior median vein of the medulla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The deep plantar arch (aka plantar arterial arch) is the collective term used for the deep branch of lateral plantar artery, the deep plantar artery, and the anastomosis that is formed between them:

- The deep branch is one of the two terminal branches of the lateral plantar artery, the other being the superficial branch. From its origin, close to the base of the 5th metatarsal, the deep branch of lateral plantar artery travels medially, in an arch like manner, across the plantar surfaces of the bases of the 2nd to 4th metatarsals. It anastomoses with the deep plantar artery at the plantar aspect of the proximal part of the 1st intermetatarsal space.

- The deep plantar artery is one of the two terminal branches of the dorsalis pedis artery, the other being the 1st dorsal metatarsal artery. From its origin, at the dorsal aspect of the proximal part of the 1st intermetatarsal space, the deep plantar travels inferiorly through the 1st intermetatarsal space, and then anastomoses with the deep branch of lateral plantar artery.

Along its course, the deep plantar arch gives off the plantar metatarsal arteries, as well as perforating branches. 

Overall, the deep plantar arch and its branches provide an arterial supply to the to the sole of foot and toes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The muscles of the deep perineal pouch lie in the urogenital triangle of the perineum, superior to the perineal membrane, and inferior to the levator ani muscles and urogenital hiatus. They support the pelvic floor and assist with urinary continence. \r\rThe muscles of the deep perineal pouch consist of the:\r\r- Deep transverse perineal\r- External urethral sphincter"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lymph vessels communicating with the parotid and auricular lymph nodes drain the lateral parts of the face and head into the superficial parotid nodes, and then to the jugular trunks via the superior deep cervical lymph nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior ethmoidal vein drains the frontal sinus and the anterolateral nasal septum. It passes through the anterior ethmoidal foramen, emptying into the superior ophthalmic vein, via the ethmoidal vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This articular surface lies on the lateral aspect of the lunate carpal for articulation with the scaphoid carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
