    "text" : "The circular and longitudinal layers of the muscular layer of esophagus forms the muscularis externa. The muscularis externa can be up to 300um thick and is composed of different types of muscle throughout its length. In the upper third, of the esophagus it is composed of skeletal muscle; the middle third is a mix of both skeletal and smooth muscle fibers; while the lower third is composed of smooth muscle only."
  },
  {
    "name" : "Key Features/Anatomical Relations",
    "text" : "The circular layer of muscle fibers is thinner than the longitudinal layer and form the inner layer of the muscularis externa. Superiorly, the circular layer of fibers blend with the inferior pharyngeal constrictor. Inferiorly, is it continuous with the oblique layer of muscle in the stomach wall."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Ramus and coronoid process of mandible"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Together with the maxilla, the lacrimal sulus forms the nasolacrimal canal which allows passage of the nasolacrimal duct."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This articular surface lies on the medial side of the lunate carpal for articulation with the triquetral carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The squamous part of the temporal bone is the broad, flat and thin part of the bone which forms part of the lateral wall of the cranial vault, and the temple."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The incisive foramen is located just behind the incisor teeth on the inferior surface of the palatine plate. It allows passage of vessels and nerves into the incisive canal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Most of the joints of the skull are fixed fibrous joints, called sutures. Sutures are where the edges of the bones firmly fit together and are united by connective tissue. In fetal skulls the sutures are membranous and wide (called fontanelles) to allow slight movement during birth. Most of these fontanelles close during the first year of life, leaving thin connective tissue ligaments separating the bones at the suture line. This thin connective tissue begins to fully ossify in the late twenties and is normally complete by around the age of 50. \n\nAnother type of fibrous joint of the skull are called syndesmoses where bones or bone parts are joined together by connective tissue. Unlike other fibrous joints, syndesmoses are moveable. Cranial syndesmoses include the stylohyoid ligament, which joins the temporal bone to the hyoid bone, and the pterygospinous ligament, which forms a bridge between parts of the sphenoid bone. \n\nThe final type of fibrous joint found in the skull are gomphosis which are joints between the roots of the teeth and the sockets of the maxilla or mandible."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The bones of the head and neck consist of the bones of that form the skull, auditory ossicles, and the hyoid bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sphenoidal process of the palatine bone is a small process from the posterior of the perpendicular plate of the palatine bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The zygomatic process of the temporal bone articulates with the temporal process of the zygomatic bone to form the zygomatic arch."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The conchal crest of the palatine bone is a ridge on the nasal surface of the palatine bone which marks the point at which the palatine bone articulates with the inferior nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal alar branch arises from the superior labial artery.  It provides an arterial supply to structures that form the alar part of the nose."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular tubercle of the temporal bone is an eminence located anterior to the mandibular fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The infrahyoid lymph nodes are nodes of the deep cervical group of lymph nodes that are located between the hyoid bone and the thyroid cartilage. They drain into the internal jugular vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the spinal nerve is found on the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae. The transverse processes exhibit a deep sulcus on their upper surfaces for passage of the corresponding cervical spinal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lateral facial muscles are one of the two subgroups of the facial muscles, the other being the medial facial muscles. This group consists of the paired muscles that lie on both sides of the face. They attach to the skin and subcutaneous structures of the face on one end, and the bones and fascia of the face on the other. They are involved in facial expressions. \r\rThe lateral facial muscles consist of the:\r\r- Circumorbital muscles\r- Depressor anguli oris\r- Risorius\r- Zygomaticus major\r- Zygomaticus minor\r- Levator labii superioris \r- Levator labii superioris alaeque nasi\r- Depressor labii inferioris \r- Levator anguli oris \r- Buccinator \r- Mentalis"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior angle of the occipital bone joins the sphenoid bone. In the adult this part of the bone is fused to the sphenoid bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular circumference of the radius is the portion of the head of the radius that articulates with the radial notch of the ulna."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This surface lies on the proximal aspect of the triquetral carpal and articulates with the lunate carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sensory root of the trigeminal nerve (aka 5th cranial nerve, or CN V) emerges from the trigeminal ganglion and the motor root from the trigeminal motor nucleus in the pons. The nerve divides into the ophthalmic, mandibular and maxillary divisions at the trigeminal ganglion. \n\nThe sensory fibers convey general sensations from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, skin of the face and mucous membranes of the nasal and oral cavities. \n\nThe motor fibers supply the mylohyoid, tensor veli palatini, tensor tympani and anterior belly of the digastric muscles, as well as muscles of mastication which include the: masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid and lateral pterygoid muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The temporozygomatic suture is the line that marks the union of the zygomatic process of the temporal bone with the temporal process of the zygomatic bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superior deep lateral cervical lymph nodes are a group of lymph nodes that lie deep to the sternocleidomastoid muscle, close to the carotid sheath. Lymph from the structures of the head and neck drain into these lymph nodes, which in turn, drain into the inferior deep cervical lymph nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Medial aspect of base of the proximal phalanx of little finger"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The zygomatic orbital vein drains the muscles of the zygomatic region, running posteriorly into the superficial temporal vein and merging to form the retromandibular vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The seventh costal notches one of the seven pairs of laterally facing articular areas found along the lateral borders of the sternum. Each seventh costal facet is formed by the combination of two demifacets:

- the upper demifacet is located at the inferior end of the lateral border of the body of sternum;

- the lower demifacet is located at the superior end of the lateral border of the xiphoid process.

The seventh costal notches articulate with the seventh costal cartilage on its respective side, forming the seventh sternocostal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The arterial supply to the face, cranium, and exterior of the cranium mostly comes from the distal branches of the external carotid artery."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Three perforating branches arise from the deep plantar arch as it travels across the plantar aspect of the proximal parts of the intermetatarsal spaces.\n\nFrom their origin, they travel superiorly through the 2nd, 3rd and 4th intermetatarsal spaces, and then anastomoses with the 2nd, 3rd and 4th dorsal metatarsal arteries. \n\nThe perforating branches of deep plantar arch provide an arterial supply to the structures of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th intermetatarsal spaces, and adjacent structures."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior lacrimal crest is a ridge of bone which runs along the posterior margin of the frontal process. It marks the anterior border of the lacrimal fossa and contains a small tubercle, the lacrimal tubercle, at the point at which it is continuous with the orbital margin."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior nasal concha are curved bones which project medially from the maxillae. They form part of the lateral wall of the inferior nasal meatus and act to clean, heat and add moisture to air as it is inhaled. On their superior borders, they contain a number of processes for articulation with adjacent bones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the greater petrosal nerve is a depression on the anterior surface of the petrous part of the temporal bone. It runs more medially than the groove for the lesser petrosal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The circular pharyngeal muscles are one of the two subgroups of the pharyngeal muscles, the other being the longitudinal pharyngeal muscles. Within the pharyngeal wall, their muscle fibers are oriented in a circular manner, and lie external to the longitudinal pharyngeal muscles. Contraction of the circular pharyngeal muscles causes constriction of the pharynx, which is necessary for deglutition (i.e. swallowing).\r\rThe circular pharyngeal muscles consist of the:\r\r- Superior pharyngeal constrictor\r- Middle pharyngeal constrictor\r- Inferior pharyngeal constrictor"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the sigmoid sinus is a wide depression in the posterior cranial fossa. The sinus is placed on the lateral portion of the occipital bone where it curves on to the mastoid portion of the temporal bone and rests on the parietal bone. It is continuous with the transverse groove."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Anterior part of the styloid process"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The pharyngeal raphe is the median tendinous seam on the posterior pharyngeal wall where the muscular fibres of the pharyngeal constrictors meet and interlace. The pharyngeal raphe extends from the pharyngeal tubercle on the occipital bone to the posterior wall of the esophagus."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Grooves on the ethmoid bone and on the frontal bone either edge of the ethmoidal notch form the anterior ethmoid canal through the articulation of the two bones. The openings to this canal are called the anterior ethmoidal foramina and are found on the medial wall of the orbit. The anterior ethmoidal vessels are transmitted through this canal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the parietal bone is a large curved plate of bone which forms on either side, the walls of the summit of the skull. The body of the bone is bound on all sides by sutures with other bones of the skull."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Base of the middle phalanx of third toe"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior vertebral notch is the inferior concavity below the pedicle of the transverse process. The inferior notch of one vertebra connects to the superior intervertebral notch of the vertebra below to form the intervertebral foramen."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the maxilla is part of the internal surface of the maxilla which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The middle nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the middle and inferior nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Posterior aspect of the mastoid process of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The carotid groove marks the route of passage of the internal carotid artery."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Fascia is a layer of dense, fibrous connective tissue which covers most of the body, for the exception of the face. It consists of tendinous fibers that are placed parallel with one another. \n\nIt can extend deep to surround individual muscles (investing fascia), and also divide groups of muscles into compartments that share the same nerve supply and function (intermuscular septa).\n\nThis is the fascia covering the head and neck region."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Sternal head: anterior aspect of the manubrium"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Located on the posterior surface of the middle pharyngeal constrictor muscle is a plexus of nerves called the pharyngeal plexus of the vagus nerve. While the primary contribution to this nervous plexus is derived from vagus nerve fibers, a portion is also contributed by the superior cervical ganglion and the glossopharyngeal nerve. \n\nThe pharyngeal branch of the vagus nerve supplies motor fibers to the plexus, while the pharyngeal branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve contribute sensory fibers that also innervate the oropharynx and laryngopharynx."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Right transverse process of first lumbar vertebra"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The mucosa of larynx is the mucous membrane that lines the internal surfaces of the laryngeal cavity. It contains glands and contributes to the formation of folds within the larynx."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lacrimal tubercle is a small eminence at the junction of the lacrimal crest with the margin of the orbit. It marks the location of the lacrimal sac."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The superficial lymph vessels of the leg lie within the superficial fascia of the leg, and can be divided into two groups - a medial group and a lateral group. \n\nThe medial group of lymph vessels largely accompany the great saphenous vein from the tibial side of the foot to the hip, while the lateral group accompanies the small saphenous vein from the fibular side of the foot to the knee where the small saphenous vein anastomoses with the popliteal vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sphenosquamosal suture is a line which unites the squamous part of the temporal bone with the sphenoid bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The temporal branches radiate from the central retinal artery at the posterior part of the eye. These branches radiate along the temporal (or lateral) half of the internal surface of the retina, and can be subdivided into superior and inferior parts.\n\nThe temporal branches of the central retinal artery provide an arterial supply to the retina of the temporal half of the eye."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The posterior surface of xiphoid process is the smooth, slightly concave area on the posterior aspect of the xiphoid process. It is one of the two surfaces of the xiphoid process, the other being the anterior surface. It is an area that is bounded by the superior and two lateral borders of the xiphoid process. \n\nThe posterior surface of xiphoid process provides an origin site for the diaphragm and provides an attachment site for the sternocostal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The trigeminal impression is an impression for the trigeminal ganglion of the trigeminal nerve. It is located posterior to the petrous apex."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The zygomatic process of the maxilla is the part of the maxilla that articulates with the zygomatic bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sphenoidal sinus (aka sphenoid sinus) is a mucous membrane lined cavity located within the body of the sphenoid bone. It communicates with the superior nasal cavity via apertures on the posterior wall of the spheno-ethmoidal recess. The sphenoid sinuses drain into the nasal cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This cartilaginous flap normally projects obliquely upwards and dorsally from the larynx, but during swallowing, elevation of the hyoid bone causes it to depress, preventing food from entering the trachea and guiding food instead to the oesophagus. Of note is the presence of taste buds on the epiglottis."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The arterial supply to the eye and the surrounding structures of the orbit mostly comes from the distal branches of the ophthalmic artery."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Posterior surface of the manubrium"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lateral aspect of the mastoid process of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal retinal arterioles radiate from the central retinal artery at the posterior part of the eye. These branches radiate along the nasal (or medial) half of the internal surface of the retina, and can be subdivided into superior and inferior parts.\n\nThe nasal branches of the central retinal artery provide an arterial supply to the retina of the nasal half of the eye."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This articular surface which lies at the distal end of the ulna articulates with the articular disc of the wrist."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The spheno-ethmoidal synchrondrosis is where the ethmoidal bone joins the sphenoid bone. In the adult these bones are fused by a cartilaginous union."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The canine eminences mark the locations of the roots of the canine teeth."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The sphenopetrosal suture marks the area between the sphenoid bone and the petrous part of the temporal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ary-epiglottic fold is the triangular opening that forms the entrance of the larynx. It consists of both ligaments and muscle tissue joining the lateral border of the epiglottis and the superior border of the arytenoid cartilage."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Mastoid process of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Inferior head - lateral surface of lateral pterygoid plate"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Anterolateral portion of the iliac crest"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The olfactory tract travels posteriorly before the fibres divide into the lateral stria and medial stria. These form the olfactory trigone, located in front of the anterior perforated substance. The fibres of the medial stria travel to the contralateral olfactory bulb via the anterior commissure."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the medulla oblongata is an impression for the brainstem found on the posterior cranial fossa. It is on the inner surface of the occipital bone anterior to the foramen magnum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Pyramidal process of palatine bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The canine fossae are two small depressions, one left and one right, which lie just lateral to the canine eminence. The caninus muscle has its origin at this point."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The eyeball is the organ of sight and is comprised principally of the iris, the lens, the pupil and the cornea. The eye measures approximately 1 inch or 2.5 cm. in diameter. If someone's eyeball is larger than this, they will be nearsighted (myopic); if it is smaller than this, they will be farsighted (hyperopic). Having two eyes gives us binocular vision - (depth perception). This is due to the fusing of both images in the visual cortex."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The arterial supply to the hip region of the lower limb mostly comes from the distal branches of the internal and external iliac arteries and the femoral artery."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Head of fourth metatarsal"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The deep connective tissue in the anterior popliteal fossa. See Fascia of the Popliteal fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The alveolar process is a thick projection on the inferior part of the body of the maxilla. It contains the sockets for the teeth of the upper dental arch. It also gives attachment to the buccinator muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "This articular surface lies on the distal aspect of the scaphoid carpal for articulation with the trapezoid carpal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The groove for the spinal nerve is found on the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae. The transverse processes exhibit a deep sulcus on their upper surfaces for passage of the corresponding cervical spinal nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior articular surface is a curved facet on the anterior aspect of the dens of the axis. It articulates with the facet on the anterior arch of atlas."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The seventh costal notches one of the seven pairs of laterally facing articular areas found along the lateral borders of the sternum. Each seventh costal facet is formed by the combination of two demifacets:

- the upper demifacet is located at the inferior end of the lateral border of the body of sternum;

- the lower demifacet is located at the superior end of the lateral border of the xiphoid process.

The seventh costal notches articulate with the seventh costal cartilage on its respective side, forming the seventh sternocostal joints."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior nasal spine is a pointed anterosuperior projection at the anterior end of the intermaxillary suture. It marks the point at which the septal nasal cartilage attaches to the maxilla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Anteromedial surface of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The petrous part of the temporal bone is pyramid in shape and is located at the base of the skull. It contains the structures of the inner ear."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Pyramidal process of palatine bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lateral borders are the irregular lateral margins of the manubrium of sternum. They form two of the four borders of the manubrium of sternum, the other two being the superior and inferior borders.\n\nEach lateral border of the manubrium of sternum contains a superior first costal notch and an inferiorly located demifacet that forms the upper part of the second costal notch.\n\nThe lateral borders of the manubrium provide attachment sites for the sternocostal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Xiphoid process"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the zygomatic bone forms the prominence of the cheekbones. It extends into temporal, maxillary, frontal and orbital processes which articulate with the temporal, maxilla, frontal and sphenoid bones respectively. The orbital surface of the body is perforated by a small foramen, the zygomaticofacial foramen which allows passage of the zygomaticofacial nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "ORIGIN: Frontal process of maxilla. 

INSERTION: Alar cartilage of the nose and skin of the upper lip. 

ACTION: Elevation of the upper lip and assists in flaring of the nares. 

NERVE SUPPLY: Zygomatic branches of facial nerve; buccal branches of facial nerve

ARTERIAL SUPPLY: Superior labial artery; angular artery; infra-orbital artery"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The dental branches arise along the length of the distal half of the inferior alveolar artery. They provide an arterial supply to the mandibular teeth."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lateral nasal vein receives tributaries from the alar and septal veins, draining the tissue of the lateral external nose into the facial vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The anterior surface of xiphoid process is the smooth, flat area on the anterior aspect of the xiphoid process. It is one of the two surfaces of the xiphoid process, the other being the posterior surface. It is an area that is bounded by the superior and two lateral borders of the xiphoid process. \n\nThe anterior surface of xiphoid process provides an insertion site for the rectus abdominis muscle and provides an attachment site for the sternocostal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal septum is made up primarily of the vomer bone, the cartilage of the septum and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone. Inferiorly the crests of the maxillae and palatine bone also form part of the structure of the nasal septum. \n\nThe nasal septum separates the nasal cavity into left and right forming the left and right nostrils."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The vestibular fold (aka false vocal fold, false vocal cord, or ventricular fold) is a thick mucous membrane folds that lie over the vestibular ligaments. It has a less significant role in phonation than the vocal fold. Instead, they play an important role in preventing food and liquids from entering the airways."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The palatine muscles are a group of muscles that elevate or alter the shape of the soft palate. \r\rThe palatine muscles consist of the:\r\r- Levator veli palatini\r- Tensor veli palatini\r- Uvular muscle\r- Palatoglossus"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The facial surface or anterior surface of the maxilla is the external surface of the bone which is directed anteriorly and laterally."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Lesser trochanter of femur and body inferior to it"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The occipitomastoid suture joins the occipital bone with the mastoid portion of the temporal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The bregma marks the point at which the sagittal and coronal sutures meet. In a baby, this area is membranous and is called the anterior fontanelle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The temporal fossa is a shallow space on the side of the skull. It is bounded by the temporal lines and terminates below at the level of the zygomatic arch."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Medial part of orbital margin"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The cochlea is the sensory organ of the inner ear that is responsible for the sensation of hearing. It consists of fluid filled chambers that respond to waves of pressure in the environment. The fluid translates the pressure waves and vibrations into sounds as the fluid mechanically stimulates hair cells that lie in the epithelium over which the fluid sits. The cochlear coil contains inner and outer hair cells. The primary output of the cochlea to the brain for translation into sounds is via the inner hair cells. The outer hair cells primarily receive inputs from the brain that modify how the hair cells respond to sound vibrations, acting in a similar fashion as a preamplifier."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Medial surface of the lateral pterygoid plate of sphenoid bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The prevertebral muscles are a group of neck muscles that lie deep to the prevertebral layer of cervical fascia. They are involved in movements of the head and neck.\r\rThe prevertebral muscles consist of the:\r\r- Longus capitis\r- Longus colli\r- Scalene muscles\r- Suboccipital muscles"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "ORIGIN: Anteromedial surface of maxilla; anteromedial surface of mandible. 

INSERTION: Skin and fascia of the lips. 

ACTION: Compression and protrusion of the lips. 

NERVE SUPPLY: Zygomatic branches of facial nerve; buccal branches of facial nerve; marginal mandibular branch of facial nerve 

ARTERIAL SUPPLY: Superior labial artery; inferior labial artery"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The laryngeal skeleton, a cartilage structure surrounding the trachea, is made up of nine cartilages. The largest of the nine is called the thyroid cartilage and contains the larynx."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The posterior temporal branch of middle cerebral artery (aka posterior temporal artery) arises from the inferior terminal branch of the middle cerebral artery. It travels laterally, through the lateral sulcus, and then inferiorly along the lateral surface of the posterior part of the temporal lobe.\n\nIt provides an arterial supply to the lateral surface of the posterior part of the temporal lobe."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The posterior surface is the smooth, concave area on the posterior aspect of the manubrium of sternum. It is one of the two surfaces of the manubrium of sternum, the other being the anterior surface. It is an area that is bounded by the superior, inferior, and two lateral borders of the manubrium of sternum.\n\nThe posterior surface of manubrium of sternum provides an origin site for the sternohyoid and sternothyroid muscles. It also provides an attachment site for the sternocostal ligaments."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The anterior omohyoid lymph node is a lymph node belonging to the superior deep lateral cervical group of lymph nodes. It is located on top of the superior belly of the omohyoid muscle.\n\nThis node drains the tongue into the supraclavicular (inferior deep cervical) lymph nodes, and then to the jugular trunks at the jugulosubclavian junction. This node is also joined by other lymphatic afferent from the submental/suprahyoid and submandibular nodes."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tympanic part of the temporal bone is a curved plate of bone found between the squamous part of the temporal bone and in front of the mastoid process. The external acoustic meatus is found here."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The artery of central sulcus arises from the superior terminal branch of the middle cerebral artery. It travels superiorly, along the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere, towards the central sulcus, which it also travels along.\n\nIt provides an arterial supply to the central sulcus and the areas of the cerebral cortex that lie adjacent to it."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The lateral borders of xiphoid process are the irregular lateral margins of the xiphoid process. They form two of the three borders of the xiphoid process, the other being the superior border.\n\nEach lateral border of xiphoid process contains a superiorly located demifacet that forms the lower part of the seventh costal notch.\n\nThey provide attachment sites for the linea alba and the aponeuroses of the transversus abdominis muscle."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Temporal process of the zygomatic bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The oral cavity, or mouth, is the most rostral section of the digestive system. It is bounded rostrally by the alveolar arches and teeth and caudally by the oropharynx. \n\nIt contains the tongue which aids in mastication in conjunction with the hard and soft palates above it. The oral cavity is also involved in respiration and sound modulation."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Canine fossa of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The ethmoidal crest on the palatine bone is a ridge of bone which projects from the upper part of the internal surface of the palatine bone and articulates with the ethmoid bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The articular surface of the medial femoral condyle articulates with the medial condyle of the tibia. The articular surface of the lateral femoral condyle articulates with the lateral condyle of the tibia."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The body of the lacrimal bone is small and thin. On its external surface it contains a sulcus, the lacrimal sulcus, which is bound posteriorly by a ridge, the posterior lacrimal crest and anteriorly by the anterior lacrimal crest on the maxilla."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Base of the middle phalanx of second toe"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Posterior surface of the manubrium below the origin of the sternohyoid"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The lacrimal apparatus consists of the lacrimal gland, which secretes lacrimal fluid to moisten the surface of the cornea, the lacrimal canaliculi, which drain lacrimal fluid from the lacrimal lake, and the nasolacimal duct, which carries the lacrimal fluid to the nasal cavity."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Emissary veins proceed superiorly from the pterygoid plexus, penetrating the base of the skull, and draining into the cavernous venous sinus, which lies in the middle cranial fossa. Emissary veins are a recognised route via which infection can enter the central nervous system from a peripheral source such as a dental abscess."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The posterior nasal spine is a pointed posterior projection at the end of the nasal crest. It gives attachment to the musculus uvulae."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The squamous suture is a line which separates the squamous part of the temporal bone from the parietal bone above."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior border of the zygomatic bone is directed posteriorly and inferiorly. It gives origin to the masseter muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The inferior border of the zygomatic bone is directed posteriorly and inferiorly. It gives origin to the masseter muscles."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The infratemporal surface of the maxilla is the surface of the bone on the lateral side posterior to the zygomatic process which also forms part of the infratemporal fossa."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Floor of the orbit lateral to the lacrimal groove"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The alar vein drains the alar region of the nose and is a tributary of the external nasal vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Frontal process of maxilla"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "More commonly known as the tooth sockets, the dental alveoli are the depressions in the maxilla which contain the roots of the teeth."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Styloid process of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Plantar aspect of fifth metatarsal"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "At the level of the thyroarytenoid muscle the recurrent laryngeal nerve forms an anastomosis with the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. This structure is also called Galen's Anastomosis or the ramus anastomoticus and is involved in the sensory and autonomic innervation of the larynx."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The tongue is a muscular organ located within the oral cavity. It is involved in the processes of deglutition (i.e. swallowing) and speech. Internally, the tongue consists of muscles known as intrinsic tongue muscles, while externally, the tongue it is supported by what are known as extrinsic tongue muscles.\r\rThe extrinsic tongue muscles consist of the:\r\r- Genioglossus\r- Hyoglossus\r- Styloglossus"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Styloid process of temporal bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Description",
    "text" : "The jugular notch of sternum (suprasternal or presternal notch) is the large indentation found along the superior border of manubrium of sternum. It is located between the right and left clavicular notches and forms a palpable depression at the root of the neck. \n\nThe jugular notch provides an attachment site for the interclavicular ligament."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the palatine bone is part of the medial surface of the bone which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The middle nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the middle nasal concha and the inferior nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The diaphragma sellae (aka sellar diaphragm) is a piece of dura matter located superior to the hypophyseal fossa of the sphenoid bone and the pituitary gland. \n\nIt attaches anteriorly to the anterior clinoid process of the sphenoid bone and posteriorly to the posterior clinoid process of the sphenoid bone. \n\nThere is an opening in the centre of the diaphragma sellae that allows for the passage of the pituitary infundibulum."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Mastoid process"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The central retinal vein follows the same course as the central retinal artery through the optic nerve. It drains the retina of the blood supplied to it by the branches of the central retinal artery. It drains the retina into the superior ophthalmic vein.\n\nLike the central retinal artery, the central retinal vein may suffer from occlusion of the vessel. Occlusion of the central retinal vein can potentially damage the retina due to ischemia or edema, leading to blindness."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The submandibular lymph nodes are located in the submaxillary triangle beneath the mandible, proximal to the submandibular gland.\n\nTypically between 2 and 6 in number, these nodes drain the upper and lower lips, the anterior part of the tongue, then cheeks and the lateral portions of the nose, and also receive lymph from the facial and suprahyoid lymph nodes. The submandibular lymph nodes drain into the superior deep cervical lymph nodes, which in turn drain into the jugular trunks."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal surface of the maxilla is part of the internal surface of the maxilla which forms part of the border of the nasal cavity. The middle nasal meatus is the nasal cavity between the middle and inferior nasal concha."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The olfactory tract travels posteriorly before the fibres divide into the lateral stria and medial stria. These form the olfactory trigone, located in front of the anterior perforated substance. The fibers of the lateral stria finish in the olfactory cortex, and some fibers travel to the amygdala."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The intermediate cuneiform tarsal presents four articular facets - a medial, a lateral, a proximal, and a distal facet. \n\nThe medial articular surface of the intermediate cuneiform articulates with the medial cuneiform. The lateral articular surface of the intermediate cuneiform articulates with the lateral cuneiform. The proximal articular surface of the intermediate cuneiform articulates with the navicular bone. The distal articular surface of the intermediate cuneiform articulates with the base of the second metatarsal bone."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The squamous part of the temporal bone is the broad, flat and thin part of the bone which forms part of the lateral wall of the cranial vault, and the temple."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The nasal crest of the maxilla is a ridge of bone along the superior surface of the palatine process above the intermaxillary suture. The maxilla articulates with the vomer at this point."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The septal vein drains the septal region of the nose and is a tributary of the lateral nasal vein."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The corona mortis is an anatomical variant anastomosis located over the pubic ramus. It joins the obturator and external iliac or inferior epigastric veins. \n\nThe term 'corona mortis' translates as \"crown of death\", indicating the importance of this structure in orthopedic surgery. Accidental damage to this structure can cause significant hemorrhaging which may be difficult to then achieve hemostasis. It must therefore be considered cautiously during surgery."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Posterior nasal spine of palatine bone"
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The infraorbital groove is a shallow groove on the orbital surface of the maxilla which leads in to the infraorbital foramen. It allows passage of the infraorbital vessels and nerve."
  }
]

[
  {
