    "text" : "The cardiac lymphatic ring is a group of lymph nodes that form a ring around the cardia of the stomach. These nodes drain lymph from the cardia and fundus of the stomach to the intestinal trunk via the celiac lymph channels."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "Grooves on the ethmoid bone and on the frontal bone form the posterior ethmoidal canal through the articulation of the two bones. The openings to this canal are called the posterior ethmoidal foramina and are found on the medial wall of the orbit. The posterior ethmoidal vessels are transmitted through this canal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name" : "Quick Facts",
    "text" : "The vertebral foramen is an aperture formed by the union of the vertebral body and vertebral arch. It contains the vertebral (spinal) canal. This canal contains the layers of the meninges, the spinal cord (medulla spinalis) and the internal vertebral plexus of veins. The foramena of the cervical vertebrae C2-C7 are large and triangular. Those of the thoracic are small and circular and those of the lumbar are small, triangular foramina."
  }
]

[
  {
